// Scenario Lab asset library — Phase 7A: drive
const _AL_drive = {"id":"drive","title":"Phase 7A: drive","chapters":[{"id":"ch04","title":"Introduction","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Harlow's rhesus monkeys and the discovery of the third drive","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit 1949 psychology laboratory at the University of Wisconsin with eight rhesus monkeys in individual cages, each cage holding a small mechanical puzzle with a vertical pin, a hook, and a hinged cover. Harry Harlow, in a white lab coat, watches through the glass while jotting on a clipboard as a monkey confidently opens the puzzle. A faded chalkboard behind him lists the day's observation data.","player_mission":"Examine the evidence from Harlow's two-week puzzle experiment and sort each observation into the driving force it demonstrates. Choose the column that best accounts for why the monkeys solved the puzzles without any external incentive.","key_concepts":["In 1949 Harry Harlow and two colleagues ran a two-week learning experiment with eight rhesus monkeys at the University of Wisconsin.","The puzzle required three steps: pull out the vertical pin, undo the hook, and lift the hinged cover.","The monkeys began playing with the puzzles without outside urging, with focus, determination, and what looked like enjoyment.","By days 13 and 14 the monkeys solved the puzzles frequently and quickly; two-thirds of the time in less than sixty seconds.","Harlow reported that puzzle solution did not lead to food, water, or sex gratification, ruling out the biological drive.","Neither rewards nor punishments from the environment had trained the monkeys; nobody rewarded them with food, affection, or applause.","Harlow concluded that 'the performance of the task provided intrinsic reward,' a satisfaction with the activity itself.","Harlow eventually called this newly discovered drive 'intrinsic motivation.'","The prevailing science of the day recognized only two drives: the biological drive and the external reward-and-punishment drive.","Harlow urged scientists to 'close down large sections of our theoretical junkyard' for a more accurate account of behavior."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This asset tests the discrimination between the two traditional drives (biological and reward-and-punishment) and Harlow's proposed third drive of intrinsic motivation. Harlow's evidence showed significant learning and efficient performance were attained without resort to special or extrinsic incentives.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which of the three candidate drives best explains why Harlow's monkeys solved the mechanical puzzles? Sort each piece of evidence into the drive it points to.","column_a_label":"Biological drive (hunger, thirst, sex)","column_b_label":"External reward-and-punishment drive","column_c_label":"Intrinsic motivation (the third drive)","rows":[{"label":"The monkeys manipulated the puzzles 'unbidden by any outside urging,' unprompted by the experimenters","col":"c"},{"label":"Harlow reported: 'Solution did not lead to food, water, or sex gratification'","col":"c"},{"label":"Nobody taught the monkeys or rewarded success with food, affection, or applause","col":"c"},{"label":"Two-thirds of the time the monkeys cracked the puzzle in less than sixty seconds by days 13-14","col":"c"},{"label":"Harlow: 'The performance of the task provided intrinsic reward'","col":"c"},{"label":"Significant learning was attained and efficient performance maintained without resort to special or extrinsic incentives","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["The monkeys were solving the puzzles to earn the raisins they could see nearby, so the biological drive explains the behavior.","The experimenters had trained the monkeys step by step through conditioning, so the external reward-and-punishment drive explains the behavior.","The monkeys only mastered the puzzle in exchange for quiet applause from Harlow's colleagues."],"explanation":"Every observation sorts to column c: the monkeys' behavior lacked the object of any biological drive (no food, water, or sex gratification) and any external contingency (no rewards, punishments, or instruction). The learning and efficient performance occurred purely because solving the puzzle was its own reward, which is Harlow's third drive, intrinsic motivation. Columns a and b fail because the evidence explicitly rules out both known drives."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Raisins and dollars: how contingent rewards changed behavior","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen comparison scene. On the left, a 1949 monkey cage where Harlow drops raisins into a bowl as a monkey hesitates over a puzzle; the monkey's error count rises. On the right, a 1969 Carnegie Mellon lab room where Edward Deci, stopwatch in hand, watches through a one-way window as a student leaves the Soma puzzle pieces on a table and picks up a magazine. A timeline bar across the bottom marks sessions one, two, and three.","player_mission":"Compare the raisin experiment and Deci's Soma puzzle study. Sort each observation into the outcome it demonstrates: a short-term boost, an undermined intrinsic interest, or no measurable change.","key_concepts":["When Harlow rewarded the monkeys with raisins for solving the puzzles, they made more errors and solved them less frequently.","Harlow wrote that 'introduction of food in the present experiment served to disrupt performance,' a phenomenon not reported in the literature.","Edward Deci, a Carnegie Mellon psychology graduate student, chose the Soma puzzle cube for his 1969 dissertation study.","Deci paid Group A one dollar for every configuration successfully reproduced in the second session, while Group B never received pay.","During the secretly watched eight-minute free-choice period, paid Group A participants spent more than five minutes with the puzzle on day two.","On the unpaid third session, Group A played about two minutes less than in the paid session and about a full minute less than in the first session.","Deci concluded: 'When money is used as an external reward for some activity, the subjects lose intrinsic interest for the activity.'","Deci compared the reward effect to caffeine: a short-term boost that wears off and can reduce longer-term motivation.","Group B, which was never paid, played with the puzzle for a little longer than in previous sessions during the third day.","Deci argued that enhancing intrinsic motivation means not concentrating on external-control systems such as monetary rewards."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This asset tests the undermining effect of contingent rewards documented by Harlow and Deci: rewards deliver a short-term engagement boost, but using money as an external reward makes people lose intrinsic interest in the activity over the longer term. The effect is the reverse of the Motivation 2.0 assumption that reward intensifies interest.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Each row describes an outcome from Harlow's raisin test or Deci's Soma sessions. Which conclusion does the evidence as a whole support? Sort each observation into the pattern it demonstrates.","column_a_label":"Short-term engagement boost (rewards spur temporary interest)","column_b_label":"Undermined intrinsic interest (rewards erode lasting motivation)","column_c_label":"No measurable change (behavior stays stable)","rows":[{"label":"Harlow's monkeys made more errors and solved puzzles less frequently once raisins were introduced","col":"b"},{"label":"Deci's paid Group A spent more than five minutes on the puzzle during day-two free choice, up from three-and-a-half to four minutes","col":"a"},{"label":"On the third, unpaid session, Group A's free-choice puzzle time fell about a minute below the first session baseline","col":"b"},{"label":"Deci: 'When money is used as an external reward for some activity, the subjects lose intrinsic interest for the activity'","col":"b"},{"label":"Deci likened the reward effect to a jolt of caffeine that keeps you working a few more hours before wearing off","col":"a"},{"label":"Never-paid Group B played with the puzzle for a little longer than before on the final session","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The evidence shows rewards always intensify interest and enhance performance, exactly as the pay-for-performance view predicts.","The evidence shows money has no effect on intrinsic interest either way, since both groups behaved identically.","The evidence shows the monkeys and students simply lost interest over time because the puzzles became boring."],"explanation":"The pivotal rows sort to column b: contingent reward followed by its removal left Harlow's monkeys and Deci's Group A performing worse than baseline, precisely Deci's finding that money used as an external reward destroys intrinsic interest. The day-two rows are column a decoys because payment did produce a visible short-term boost, and the never-paid Group B rows are column c decoys, showing that without any reward contingency interest held steady. The weight of evidence is the undermining effect, column b."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The raisin reversal: rewarding the puzzle made it worse","visual_frame_description":"A two-panel laboratory tableau. In the first panel, a rhesus monkey in a Wisconsin cage clicks a puzzle's hinged cover open with confident paws while Harlow beams. In the second panel, the same monkey stares at a dish of raisins beside the puzzle, makes a fumbling error, and stops, while Harlow scribbles 'disrupted performance' on his clipboard. A dramatic red glow surrounds the dish of raisins.","player_mission":"Confront the puzzle of the raisin reward. First explain why the monkeys mastered the puzzle with no incentive at all. Then diagnose what happened when Harlow added the reward that conventional theory promised would improve their performance.","key_concepts":["Harlow's rhesus monkeys played with the mechanical puzzles with focus, determination, and what looked like enjoyment.","By days 13 and 14, the monkeys solved the puzzles frequently and quickly, two-thirds of the time in less than sixty seconds.","The puzzle required pulling out a vertical pin, undoing a hook, and lifting a hinged cover.","Harlow noted the solution did not lead to food, water, or sex gratification.","Harlow concluded the performance of the task provided intrinsic reward; the joy of the task was its own reward.","Harlow emphasized the strength and persistence of the monkeys' drive to complete the puzzles.","Harlow believed this drive may be as basic and strong as the other drives and as efficient in facilitating learning.","Introducing raisins as rewards led the monkeys to make more errors and solve the puzzles less frequently.","Harlow called the raisin disruption 'a phenomenon not reported in the literature.'","Harlow urged scientists to close down large sections of their theoretical junkyard and offer fresher accounts of human behavior."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the central mechanism of intrinsic motivation: the satisfaction of the task itself is its own reward and can be as basic and strong as the biological and reward drives. The evidence from the raisin test is that adding an external reward to an intrinsically interesting activity disrupted performance instead of improving it.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Intrinsic motivation: Harlow's third drive, in which the performance of the task provides intrinsic reward, is powerful enough to produce significant learning with no external incentive, and contingent extrinsic rewards can actively disrupt it.","stage_1_observation":"You are standing in Harlow's 1949 primate lab. Eight rhesus monkeys sit in cages, each with a three-step mechanical puzzle: pull the vertical pin, undo the hook, lift the hinged cover. Nobody has taught the monkeys, and nobody has rewarded them, but they manipulate the puzzles with focus and determination, and by days 13-14 they crack the puzzle in under sixty seconds two-thirds of the time.","stage_1_question":"Harlow reported that puzzle solution did not lead to food, water, or sex gratification, and nobody offered rewards or punishments. What accounts for the monkeys' rapid mastery of the puzzle?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The performance of the task provided intrinsic reward. The monkeys found solving the puzzle gratifying in itself, and Harlow proposed this as a third drive that may be as basic and strong as the biological and external drives and as efficient in facilitating learning.","stage_2_intervention":"The puzzle deepens. Harlow applies the conventional wisdom: if a little motivation is good, a raisin reward should make the monkeys perform even better. He introduces raisins for solving the puzzles during the experiment and counts the errors and completion rates.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Performance got worse: the monkeys made more errors and solved the puzzles less frequently. As Harlow wrote, the introduction of food served to disrupt performance, a phenomenon not reported in the literature. Extrinsic incentives imposed on an intrinsically interesting task undermined the very behavior they were meant to encourage."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Deci's eight-minute test: the Soma sessions and the money effect","visual_frame_description":"A 1969 Carnegie Mellon lab room seen through a one-way mirror. Seven colored Soma puzzle pieces lie scattered on a table beside copies of Time and The New Yorker; a female student fiddles with the pieces while Edward Deci, stopwatch in hand on the other side of the glass, calmly records her free-choice minutes. A small chalk ledger tracks session one, two, and three times beside him.","player_mission":"Master Deci's free-choice paradigm. First read why the paid students suddenly seemed more interested in the puzzle. Then diagnose the surprise waiting in the third session when the money disappeared.","key_concepts":["In the summer of 1969, Edward Deci was a Carnegie Mellon graduate student in psychology with an MBA from Wharton pursuing a dissertation on motivation.","Deci used the Soma puzzle cube, seven pieces that could be assembled into a few million possible configurations.","Participants were divided into experimental Group A and control Group B, each attending three one-hour sessions on consecutive days.","Deci paid Group A one dollar per successful configuration in session two; the sum is roughly six dollars today.","Midway through each session Deci left the room for eight minutes, claiming he needed a computer to process times, and watched through a one-way window.","In session two, paid Group A participants spent more than five minutes on the puzzle during free choice, up from three-and-a-half to four minutes.","In session three, unpaid again, Group A spent about two minutes less than in the paid session and about a minute less than in session one.","The never-paid Group B played with the puzzle a little longer than before during the third free-choice period.","Deci concluded that when money is used as an external reward, subjects lose intrinsic interest for the activity.","Deci described rewards as a short-term boost like caffeine that wears off and can reduce longer-term motivation."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the free-choice paradigm and the undermining effect of contingent cash: an external reward produces a visible short-term engagement boost, but once the reward is removed, intrinsic interest falls below its original baseline. Deci confirmed the effect in two additional studies shortly after the Soma experiment.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The free-choice paradigm and the money effect: paid participants show extra interest while the money is flowing, but when money is used as an external reward for an activity, the subjects lose intrinsic interest, with post-reward engagement dropping below the pre-reward baseline.","stage_1_observation":"It is 1969 at Carnegie Mellon. Deci runs three daily one-hour sessions. Each participant assembles Soma configurations; Group A is paid one dollar per configuration only in session two. After the second configuration of each session, Deci excuses himself for eight minutes, claiming the mainframe needs his data, and secretly watches through a one-way window. On day two, the paid Group A students suddenly linger on the puzzle for more than five minutes, well above their earlier three-and-a-half to four minutes.","stage_1_question":"Why did Group A suddenly spend so much more free-choice time with the puzzle on the day they were being paid?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The external reward produced a short-term engagement boost: with money on offer the students geared up for the chance to earn more, exactly what Motivation 2.0 would predict. This boost is real but, as Deci later showed, it is a jolt of caffeine, not a lasting change in interest.","stage_2_intervention":"Day three: Deci announces there is only enough money for one day, so today is unpaid. The routine repeats: two configurations, the interruption, the eight-minute free-choice window while Deci watches from behind the mirror. Group A's puzzle time now plunges.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Group A spent about two minutes less than in the paid session and about a minute less than in their very first session, falling below their original baseline. Deci's conclusion: when money is used as an external reward for an activity, subjects lose intrinsic interest for the activity. The short-term boost wore off and carried a lasting cost to motivation."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Rosalind's prototype bonuses: pay-for-performance at Brightloop Studio","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled design studio in late afternoon light. Rosalind, a product design director in a charcoal blazer, stands in front of a whiteboard covered with a proposed bonus spreadsheet titled 'Prototype Bonus Plan', while eight designers sketch at their desks around an unfinished 3D model. A coffee cup and a stack of Post-it notes sit between her and the camera, where the player, her advisor, is seated.","player_mission":"Counsel Rosalind, a design director who wants to attach cash bonuses to each prototype her studio produces. Guide her through the evidence on monetary rewards and intrinsic motivation so her team's creative output is protected, not eroded.","key_concepts":["Deci found that when money is used as an external reward for an activity, subjects lose intrinsic interest for the activity.","Deci wrote that one interested in developing intrinsic motivation should not concentrate on external-control systems such as monetary rewards.","Rewards can deliver a short-term boost, like a jolt of caffeine, but the effect wears off and can reduce longer-term motivation.","Harlow's monkeys made more errors and solved the puzzle less frequently when raisins were introduced as rewards.","Human beings have an inherent tendency to seek out novelty and challenges, to extend and exercise their capacities, to explore, and to learn.","This third drive is fragile; it needs the right environment to survive.","Most organizations still operate on outdated assumptions rooted more in folklore than in science about what motivates people.","Short-term incentive plans and pay-for-performance schemes usually don't work and often do harm, yet businesses keep pursuing them.","The same incentive practices have infiltrated schools, where students are plied with iPods, cash, and pizza coupons.","The mismatch between what science knows and what business does is the gap Drive intends to repair."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the undermining effect of contingent cash rewards on intrinsically interesting creative work, the mechanism behind Deci's Soma finding that external-control systems such as monetary rewards erode intrinsic interest. The optimal counsel is to strengthen the task's intrinsic appeal rather than price each unit of output.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Brightloop Studio is a twelve-person product design agency in Portland. Rosalind, the design director, is under pressure from the CEO to lift throughput before the quarterly review. She has drafted a plan paying each designer $150 per approved prototype and wants your opinion before the all-hands meeting. Her team currently treats prototyping as the most enjoyable phase of their work, but Rosalind worries that without a tangible reward they will slow down.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Rosalind","line":"I've drafted a plan: $150 per approved prototype, paid at the end of each week. Simple, transparent, and it rewards exactly the output we need."},{"speaker":"You","line":"Rosalind, what does your team do during the free moments of a project week, when nobody is watching what they work on?"},{"speaker":"Rosalind","line":"They tinker. Last month two of them built the medical-brace concept over a weekend, unpaid, just because it was fun."},{"speaker":"You","line":"Exactly. That's intrinsic motivation at work. Gordon and Priya spent a weekend on the brace because the task itself was the reward."},{"speaker":"Rosalind","line":"But cash is clearer than goodwill. Won't the bonus just push more people to prototype faster?"},{"speaker":"You","line":"In the short run, maybe a jolt. But Deci's free-choice studies showed that when money becomes the external reward, people lose intrinsic interest the moment the reward stops."},{"speaker":"Rosalind","line":"So you're saying my bonus plan could make prototyping feel like piecework? That's the opposite of what I want."},{"speaker":"You","line":"Harlow saw it with monkeys and raisins: add the reward, and the monkeys made more errors and solved less. Protect the fun, protect the flow, and the throughput follows."}],"choices":["Drop the per-prototype bonus. Keep salaries fair, then invest in the work itself: more interesting briefs, fewer confining specs, and public credit for what the team loves.","Keep the full per-prototype bonus plan. Money is the clearest signal a designer can get, and the team will simply produce more.","Keep the bonuses but hand out small weekly treats, studio swag, and pizza on top so nobody loses interest.","Keep the bonuses and add a leaderboard, with the lowest weekly total reviewed by the CEO to keep everyone honest."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The third drive and the undermining effect of contingent monetary rewards: when money is used as an external reward for an activity, subjects lose intrinsic interest, so managers should not concentrate on external-control systems such as monetary rewards.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice removes the contingent reward from the intrinsically interesting act of prototyping, exactly what Deci prescribed for developing intrinsic motivation in employees. Choice 1 fails because contingent cash converts a self-directed activity into paid piecework, producing the caffeine-boost-then-crash pattern from the Soma sessions. Choice 2 mistakes gestures for the mechanism: swag and pizza do not reverse the controlling effect of the per-unit price once it is in place. Choice 3 compounds the damage by adding surveillance, which treats designers like machine parts and signals distrust, precisely the external-control system Deci warned against."}]},{"id":"ch05","title":"CHAPTER 1 - The Rise and Fall of Motivation 2.0","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Motivation 1.0, 2.0, and 2.1: the operating systems of behavior","visual_frame_description":"A console-style screen split into three eras. Left, a savannah at dawn with early humans gathering food while a saber-toothed tiger prowls in the distance, labeled 'Motivation 1.0'. Center, a factory floor circa 1900 with a Taylor-era time-study engineer holding a stopwatch, labeled 'Motivation 2.0'. Right, a 1960s office where a manager loosens his tie in a relaxed, flexible workspace, labeled 'Motivation 2.1'. A status bar along the top reads: OPERATING SYSTEM UPGRADE SEQUENCE.","player_mission":"Audit humanity's motivational operating systems. Sort the evidence rows into the operating system each one belongs to, then identify which system the chapter's evidence shows is failing.","key_concepts":["Societies have operating systems: layers of laws, social customs, and economic arrangements resting on assumptions about human behavior.","Motivation 1.0 was the early operating system, based on the biological drive to survive, guided behavior roughly fifty thousand years ago.","Motivation 1.0 worked until humans formed complex societies that required cooperation and restraint of the biological drive.","Motivation 2.0 arose from the second drive to seek reward and avoid punishment, channeled by humans into everything from contract law to convenience stores.","At the heart of Motivation 2.0 is the assumption: reward the good and punish the bad to improve performance, productivity, and excellence.","The Industrial Revolution ran on Motivation 2.0, fueled in part by Frederick Winslow Taylor's scientific management.","In the 1950s Abraham Maslow, a former student of Harry Harlow's, developed humanistic psychology, questioning behavior as ratlike seeking of stimuli.","In 1960 MIT professor Douglas McGregor challenged the presumption that humans are fundamentally inert without external rewards and punishments.","McGregor-era refinements such as relaxed dress codes and flexible schedules were a modest improvement that Pink calls Motivation 2.1.","Motivation 2.0 is easy to understand, simple to monitor, and straightforward to enforce, but it has become incompatible with contemporary business."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This asset tests the successive operating systems of human motivation: the biological drive of Motivation 1.0, the reward-and-punishment platform of Motivation 2.0, and the modest humanistic refinements of Motivation 2.1. The chapter's claim is that Motivation 2.0, though deeply embedded, is proving incompatible with modern work and needs a full-scale upgrade.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which operating-system era does the preponderance of this evidence describe? Sort each row into the system it belongs to, then choose the column that holds the system the evidence is centered on.","column_a_label":"Motivation 1.0 (biological survival drive)","column_b_label":"Motivation 2.0 (seek reward, avoid punishment)","column_c_label":"Motivation 2.1 (humanistic refinements)","rows":[{"label":"Fifty thousand years ago, the drive to survive guided most behavior, from gathering food to fleeing the saber-toothed tiger","col":"a"},{"label":"Core assumption: the way to improve performance and encourage excellence is to reward the good and punish the bad","col":"b"},{"label":"Channeled the second drive to develop everything from contract law to convenience stores","col":"b"},{"label":"Frederick Winslow Taylor's scientific management was software crafted to run atop this platform","col":"b"},{"label":"The engine of commerce has been fueled equally by carrots and sticks","col":"b"},{"label":"Maslow's humanistic psychology questioned the idea that behavior was purely ratlike seeking of positive stimuli and avoidance of negative stimuli","col":"c"},{"label":"McGregor-inspired refinements: dress codes relaxed, schedules became more flexible","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The evidence shows the biological survival drive still governs modern commerce, making Motivation 1.0 the correct column.","The evidence shows the Maslow and McGregor refinements fully replaced reward-and-punishment logic with self-actualization.","The evidence shows Motivation 2.1, with its relaxed dress codes, was the era that built the Industrial Revolution."],"explanation":"The bulk of the evidence sorts to column b: reward-and-punishment logic built Motivation 2.0, powered Taylor's scientific management, and fueled commerce with carrots and sticks. One row belongs to column a because it describes the savannah-era biological drive, and two rows belong to column c because Maslow's and McGregor's humanistic refinements were only modest patches on the old platform. The center of gravity of the evidence is Motivation 2.0, the deeply embedded system that the chapter argues is now crashing."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Encarta versus Wikipedia: the encyclopedia that defied Motivation 2.0","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen boardroom in 1995. On one side, a gleaming Microsoft-branded CD-ROM case labeled 'Encarta' on a marble table, staffed by professional writers in ties. On the other, a folding table with a single laptop and scattered handwritten notes where an anonymous volunteer edits an article titled 'Encyclopedia'. Between them, a shadowy economist in a professor's gown skeptically weighs the two on a scale.","player_mission":"Run the 1995 forecasting experiment. Sort the evidence about both encyclopedias into its rightful column, then judge which model the actual 2010 outcome vindicated.","key_concepts":["The thought experiment: predict which of two encyclopedias would be larger and more popular in 2010, one funded by Microsoft, one built by unpaid volunteers.","Microsoft's encyclopedia would pay professional writers, editors, and well-compensated managers to craft articles on thousands of topics.","The volunteer encyclopedia would be created by hobbyists with no special qualifications, contributing twenty or thirty hours a week for free.","In 1995, no sober economist would have predicted the volunteer model to win; the incentives were judged all wrong.","On October 31, 2009, Microsoft pulled the plug on MSN Encarta, its disc and online encyclopedia, after sixteen years on the market.","Wikipedia became the largest and most popular encyclopedia in the world, eight years after its inception.","By then Wikipedia had more than 13 million articles in some 260 languages, including 3 million in English alone.","The conventional view of human motivation has a very hard time explaining the Wikipedia result.","Volunteers were not paid a dollar, euro, or yen and even lost potential income by contributing free labor.","Pink calls Wikipedia a case where the predictions of Motivation 2.0 seem to defy the laws of behavioral physics."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This asset tests the explanatory failure of Motivation 2.0: a corporate encyclopedia with paid professionals lost to a free encyclopedia built by unpaid hobbyists, because the conventional reward-and-punishment view has no room for intrinsic motivation. The evidence vindicates the volunteer model despite its grossly 'wrong' incentives.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which encyclopedia model does the evidence, taken together, vindicate? Sort each row into the column it describes, then choose the column where the proof lands.","column_a_label":"MSN Encarta (profit-driven corporate model)","column_b_label":"Wikipedia (volunteer open model)","column_c_label":"Motivation 2.0 expectations (the 1995 prediction)","rows":[{"label":"Paid professional writers and editors plus well-compensated managers overseeing completion on budget and on time","col":"a"},{"label":"Created by tens of thousands of people who write and edit articles for fun, with nobody paid a dollar, euro, or yen","col":"b"},{"label":"Contributors sometimes gave twenty and thirty hours per week of their labor for free","col":"b"},{"label":"Became the largest and most popular encyclopedia in the world, with more than 13 million articles in some 260 languages","col":"b"},{"label":"Pulled the plug on October 31, 2009, after sixteen years on the market","col":"a"},{"label":"In 1995, nearly every business principle said this model was the certain winner","col":"c"},{"label":"The conventional view of human motivation has a very hard time explaining this result","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The evidence vindicates Encarta, whose paid professionals ultimately produced a larger and more popular encyclopedia.","The evidence vindicates the Motivation 2.0 prediction, since the incentives of the corporate model proved decisive.","The evidence shows both encyclopedias were equally successful and neither model changed the market."],"explanation":"The rows in column b describe the volunteer model's inputs and its outcome-the largest encyclopedia in the world, built by unpaid hobbyists-while the column a rows describe the corporate model that was shut down after sixteen years. The column c rows capture the 1995 prediction that the profit-driven model would win, a prediction the actual 2010 outcome contradicted. The proof therefore lands in column b: Wikipedia's triumph is the evidence that intrinsic motivation, not pay, can sustain world-class production."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"What fuels open source: enjoyment, flow, and the gift to the community","visual_frame_description":"A cozy home office at 2 a.m., a hoodie-clad programmer smiling at a laptop running a Firefox browser, a server rack glowing softly behind him with Linux and Apache logos. In a corner of the screen, a survey pie chart labeled '684 developers' shows a large slice marked 'enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation'. A mailbox icon at the bottom reads: gift delivered to the programmer community.","player_mission":"Analyze the survey and market evidence about open-source contributors. Sort each finding into the motivational force it reveals, then select the column that shows what ultimately fuels the open-source movement.","key_concepts":["Open source depends on intrinsic motivation with the same ferocity that older business models rely on extrinsic motivation.","MIT professor Karim Lakhani and BCG consultant Bob Wolf surveyed 684 open-source developers, mostly in North America and Europe.","Lakhani and Wolf found that enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation, how creative a person feels when working on the project, is the strongest and most pervasive driver.","A large majority of the surveyed programmers reported frequently reaching the state of optimal challenge called 'flow.'","Three German economists studying open-source projects found a set of predominantly intrinsic motives at work.","Among the German findings: the fun of mastering the challenge of a given software problem drives participation.","Among the German findings: the desire to give a gift to the programmer community drives participation.","Firefox, a free open-source Web browser created almost exclusively by volunteers, has more than 150 million users.","Linux, devised by an army of unpaid programmers, powers one in four corporate servers.","Apache, maintained by a far-flung global group of volunteers, holds 52 percent of the corporate Web server market."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This asset tests the motivational base of open source: enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation, confirmed as the strongest and most pervasive driver in Lakhani and Wolf's survey of 684 developers, alongside the optimal-challenge state of flow and gift motives found by German economists. The market evidence shows volunteer-built software powering a large share of corporate infrastructure.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which motivational force does the evidence show is behind open-source participation? Sort each finding into the column it demonstrates, then choose the column that the survey evidence names as the strongest driver.","column_a_label":"Extrinsic incentives (reputation, career, pay)","column_b_label":"Intrinsic motives (enjoyment, challenge, gift)","column_c_label":"Flow (optimal challenge during work)","rows":[{"label":"Lakhani and Wolf: 'enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation' was the strongest and most pervasive driver in their survey of 684 developers","col":"b"},{"label":"German economists: participants are driven by 'the fun of mastering the challenge of a given software problem'","col":"b"},{"label":"German economists: a 'desire to give a gift to the programmer community' also drives participation","col":"b"},{"label":"A large majority of surveyed programmers frequently reached the state of optimal challenge called 'flow'","col":"c"},{"label":"Firefox, created almost exclusively by volunteers, now has more than 150 million users","col":"b"},{"label":"Linux powers one in four corporate servers and Apache holds 52 percent of the corporate Web server market","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The evidence shows open-source contributors are driven mainly by payments and job offers issued by corporations that adopt their software.","The evidence shows open-source participation is best explained by the biological survival drive of Motivation 1.0.","The evidence shows contributors stay because they are contractually required to maintain their volunteer posts."],"explanation":"The survey and economist findings all sort to column b: the strongest and most pervasive driver is enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation, expressed as fun in mastering a challenge and the desire to give a gift to the community. The flow row sorts to column c because it is a distinct experiential state the chapter defines separately, while the market-share rows reinforce column b by showing that unpaid volunteers sustain Firefox, Linux, and Apache. Column a fails because payment was not what the surveys named as the strongest motive."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Purpose maximizers: L3Cs, social businesses, and for-benefit organizations","visual_frame_description":"A gallery of new-organization plaques on a wall. One plaque reads 'L3C - Vermont, April 2008' above a North Carolina furniture factory being renovated with green technology; another reads 'Grameen Social Business - with the profit-maximization principle replaced by the social-benefit principle' beneath a portrait of Muhammad Yunus; a third shows Mozilla's Firefox logo styled as a nonprofit charter. A brass nameplate at the bottom reads: THE FOR-BENEFIT MOVEMENT.","player_mission":"Inspect the new species of business organizations described in the chapter. Sort each entity into its organizational category, then identify the category these hybrids embody.","key_concepts":["Traditional U.S. business forms-partnerships, S corporations, C corporations, and more-all aim to maximize profit.","In April 2008 Vermont became the first U.S. state to allow the low-profit limited liability corporation, dubbed the L3C.","An L3C operates like a for-profit business generating at least modest profits, but its primary aim is to offer significant social benefits.","A North Carolina L3C buys abandoned furniture factories, updates them with green technology, and leases them back to beleaguered manufacturers at a low rate.","Nobel Peace Prize winner Muhammad Yunus creates 'social businesses' that sell in an open market in the service of a larger social mission.","Yunus's formulation: the profit-maximization principle is replaced by the social-benefit principle.","The Fourth Sector Network in the United States and Denmark promotes the 'for-benefit organization,' economically self-sustaining and animated by a public purpose.","Mozilla, the entity that gave us Firefox, is organized as a for-benefit organization.","Three U.S. entrepreneurs invented the B Corporation, which requires bylaws amended so incentives favor long-term value and social impact.","These new entities are purpose maximizers, unsuited to Motivation 2.0 because they flout its very principles."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This asset tests the emergence of purpose-maximizing organizations: L3Cs, social businesses, for-benefit organizations, and B Corporations that trade the profit-maximization principle for a social-benefit principle. These hybrids flout Motivation 2.0's norms and signal where organizing is heading.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which organizational category do the chapter's new hybrid entities belong to? Sort each entity into its column, then choose the column that captures the movement's direction.","column_a_label":"Traditional profit-maximizing corporation","column_b_label":"Purpose-maximizing hybrid","column_c_label":"Open-source software collective","rows":[{"label":"Publicly held corporation owned by shareholders, with managers and a board bearing the overriding responsibility to maximize shareholder gain","col":"a"},{"label":"Vermont's 2008 L3C: operates like a for-profit business but its primary aim is to offer significant social benefits","col":"b"},{"label":"Yunus's social businesses, 'with the profit-maximization principle replaced by the social-benefit principle'","col":"b"},{"label":"Mozilla, the entity behind Firefox, organized as a 'for-benefit' organization","col":"b"},{"label":"B Corporation: bylaws amended so incentives favor long-term value and social impact instead of short-term economic gain","col":"b"},{"label":"A North Carolina L3C updating abandoned furniture factories to help revitalize a struggling region","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The evidence shows the L3C, social business, and B Corporation are simply new names for traditional profit-maximizing corporations.","The evidence shows these hybrids are open-source software collectives organized like Wikipedia and Firefox.","The evidence shows these entities exist to replace Motivation 2.0 itself with a legally mandated reward system."],"explanation":"Every hybrid entity-the L3C, Yunus's social businesses, Mozilla's for-benefit form, and the B Corporation-sorts to column b: each trades profit maximization for a social-benefit purpose while remaining economically self-sustaining. The public corporation row sorts to column a as the contrast case, and none of the entities are software collectives, so column c is empty. The direction of the rows is unmistakable: purpose maximizers are emerging because they flout Motivation 2.0's very principles."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Algorithmic versus heuristic work: where carrots and sticks fail","visual_frame_description":"A conveyor-belt factory floor on the left where a checkout clerk scans groceries in an endless loop, labeled 'algorithmic'; on the right, a sunlit agency studio where two people sketch ad concepts on a wall covered in torn poster boards, labeled 'heuristic'. A thin red dotted line separates the two worlds, and a whiteboard in the middle displays the McKinsey split: 30 percent algorithmic, 70 percent heuristic.","player_mission":"Classify each work example and finding into the right category of task. Then decide which column the chapter's evidence says depends on the third drive, where external rewards do the most damage.","key_concepts":["An algorithmic task follows a set of established instructions down a single pathway to one conclusion.","A heuristic task has no algorithm, so you must experiment with possibilities and devise a novel solution.","Working as a grocery checkout clerk is mostly algorithmic; creating an ad campaign is mostly heuristic.","During the twentieth century, most work was algorithmic, even white-collar work that could be reduced to a script or formula.","McKinsey & Co. estimates that in the United States only 30 percent of job growth now comes from algorithmic work.","McKinsey & Co. estimates that 70 percent of U.S. job growth now comes from heuristic work.","Routine work can be outsourced or automated; artistic, empathic, nonroutine work generally cannot.","Teresa Amabile of Harvard Business School found external rewards and punishments work nicely for algorithmic tasks.","Amabile found external rewards and punishments can be devastating for heuristic tasks.","Amabile's intrinsic motivation principle of creativity: intrinsic motivation is conducive to creativity; controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to creativity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This asset tests the algorithmic-heuristic distinction and Amabile's intrinsic motivation principle of creativity: external rewards and punishments work nicely for algorithmic tasks but can be devastating for heuristic ones. Since most U.S. job growth is now heuristic, the central tenets of Motivation 2.0 may impair the work modern economies depend on.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which category does the chapter's evidence show is dominant in modern job growth and most vulnerable to external rewards? Sort each row into its column, then choose the column the evidence centers on.","column_a_label":"Algorithmic task (single pathway to one conclusion)","column_b_label":"Heuristic task (experiment to a novel solution)","column_c_label":"Routine work that can be outsourced or automated","rows":[{"label":"Follow a set of established instructions down a single pathway to one conclusion","col":"a"},{"label":"No algorithm exists; you must experiment with possibilities and devise a novel solution","col":"b"},{"label":"Working as a grocery checkout clerk, much the same thing over and over in a certain way","col":"a"},{"label":"Creating an ad campaign, coming up with something new","col":"b"},{"label":"McKinsey: 70 percent of U.S. job growth now comes from this kind of work","col":"b"},{"label":"Amabile: external rewards can be devastating for this kind of task","col":"b"},{"label":"Routine accounting work that a downloadable thirty-dollar tax program can perform","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The evidence centers on algorithmic tasks, which Amabile showed external rewards can devastate.","The evidence centers on routine offshored work, since most U.S. job growth now comes from rule-based labor.","The evidence shows heuristic tasks are best motivated by carrots and sticks because they lack clear algorithms."],"explanation":"The rows sort cleanly: definitions and examples of following a set path belong to column a, the checkout clerk and tax-program rows belong to column a or c as the routine work that can be offshored or automated, and every finding about modern job growth and Amabile's research sorts to column b. The evidence centers on heuristic work because McKinsey shows it is 70 percent of U.S. job growth and Amabile shows external rewards are devastating for it, making the dependence on intrinsic motivation the chapter's key claim."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The encyclopedia showdown: beating the 1995 forecast","visual_frame_description":"A time-bending boardroom where 1995 and 2010 overlap. On one side, an economist in academic robes smirks at a PowerPoint slide of the Microsoft Encarta business plan with checkmarks next to paid writers and monitored managers. On the other side, the same room is lit by the blue glow of a Wikipedia search page showing 13 million articles in 260 languages, and the Encarta slide fades like a ghost. A counter in the corner reads: October 31, 2009 - plug pulled.","player_mission":"Defeat the 1995 forecast. First argue which encyclopedia the conventional business logic of the day demands will win. Then explain the real 2010 outcome and the motivational mechanism that made it possible.","key_concepts":["In 1995 the crystal-ball test asked which of two encyclopedias would be more successful in 2010.","The Microsoft model paid professional writers, editors, and well-compensated managers to craft articles on thousands of topics.","The volunteer model had no qualifications, no pay, and contributors who donated twenty and thirty hours per week.","In 1995 no sober economist would have picked the volunteer model; the incentives were judged all wrong.","Microsoft pulled the plug on MSN Encarta on October 31, 2009, after sixteen years on the market.","Wikipedia became the largest and most popular encyclopedia in the world within eight years of its inception.","Wikipedia had more than 13 million articles in some 260 languages, including 3 million in English alone.","The second encyclopedia existed online and was free for all users.","The conventional view of human motivation has a very hard time explaining the Wikipedia result.","Wikipedia represents the most powerful new business model of the twenty-first century: open source."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the failure of Motivation 2.0's prediction machinery: by its logic the paid corporate encyclopedia must win, yet the volunteer-built Wikipedia defeated it. The mechanism is intrinsic motivation, which the conventional reward-and-punishment view cannot accommodate.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Motivation 2.0 assumes paying people and aligning their incentives is the surest route to performance, so it predicts the corporate encyclopedia's victory. The actual result, Wikipedia's triumph, shows that intrinsic motivation can sustain world-class production that no amount of conventional incentive design predicted.","stage_1_observation":"It is 1995. You are seated across from a skeptical economist with a Ph.D. and an MBA classroom behind her. You describe two encyclopedias: one funded and staffed by Microsoft with paid writers, paid editors, and well-compensated managers; the other free, online, built by tens of thousands of unpaid hobbyists contributing twenty to thirty hours a week. You ask her to predict which will be the largest and most popular in 2010.","stage_1_question":"By the logic of Motivation 2.0, which encyclopedia must win-and why does the economist answer without hesitation?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The Microsoft encyclopedia must win. Its incentives are aligned-profits for the company, pay for writers, editors, and managers-while the volunteer project's incentives are all wrong: its contributors earn nothing and even forgo remunerative labor. Every business principle of 1995 points the same way, so the answer was considered a no-brainer.","stage_2_intervention":"The crystal ball speaks. On October 31, 2009, Microsoft pulls the plug on MSN Encarta after sixteen years, while Wikipedia becomes the largest and most popular encyclopedia in the world with more than 13 million articles in some 260 languages within eight years. A colleague asks you to explain how the outcome that 'shouldn't be possible' happened.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Wikipedia triumphed because its volunteers are driven by intrinsic motivation: enjoyment of writing and editing, the challenge of the work, and the purpose of giving an encyclopedia to the world for free. The conventional view of human motivation has a very hard time explaining this result, which is why Pink calls it a defiance of the laws of behavioral physics."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The ghost of Frederick Taylor: why scientific management misfires today","visual_frame_description":"A modern open-plan product studio at dusk, where a translucent spirit in an early-1900s suit and wire spectacles hovers behind the shoulder of a manager, whispering. On the manager's screen, a time-and-motion spreadsheet collides with a wall of post-it ideas and half-built prototypes. A subtitle glows beneath the ghost: 'Work consists mainly of simple, not particularly interesting, tasks.'","player_mission":"Battle the ghost of Frederick Winslow Taylor. First justify why his scientific management worked so well for the industrial era. Then decide what happens when his incentive-and-monitor doctrine is applied to today's heuristic knowledge work.","key_concepts":["Frederick Winslow Taylor invented 'scientific management' in the early 1900s as software crafted to run atop Motivation 2.0.","Taylor's approach treated workers like parts in a complicated machine functioning smoothly when each part did the right work in the right way.","Scientific management rewarded the behavior sought and punished the behavior discouraged, expecting people to respond rationally to external forces.","Most twentieth-century work was algorithmic, reducible to a script, a spec sheet, a formula, or a series of steps that produced a right answer.","Today routine white-collar work is racing offshore to wherever it can be done cheapest, such as India, Bulgaria, and the Philippines.","Computers are replacing simple intellectual labor, so a cousin the CPA faces competition from thirty-dollar tax programs.","McKinsey estimates that only 30 percent of U.S. job growth now comes from algorithmic work, and 70 percent from heuristic work.","Amabile found external rewards and punishments work nicely for algorithmic tasks.","Amabile found external rewards and punishments can be devastating for heuristic tasks.","Amabile's intrinsic motivation principle of creativity: intrinsic motivation is conducive to creativity; controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to creativity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the fit between Taylor's scientific management and the nature of the work: rewards and monitoring worked when tasks were routine and algorithmic, but are devastating for heuristic tasks. The mechanism is Amabile's intrinsic motivation principle of creativity, which holds that controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to creativity.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Scientific management, the software of Motivation 2.0, assumes work is simple, routine, and best handled by proper incentives and careful monitoring. That assumption fit the algorithmic industrial era, but as work turned heuristic, the same doctrine damages the performance it is meant to secure.","stage_1_observation":"A ghost hovers over a factory floor in 1910: Frederick Winslow Taylor insists that work consists mainly of simple, not particularly interesting, tasks, and the only way to get people to do them is to incentivize them properly and monitor them carefully. Workers are treated like parts in a machine; the company rewards the behavior it seeks and punishes the behavior it discourages, and people respond rationally to these extrinsic motivators. Productivity soars through the Industrial Revolution.","stage_1_question":"Why did Taylor's scientific management work so well for the industrial economy, and why was it considered 'software' for its era?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because most twentieth-century work was algorithmic: tasks followed a single pathway to one right answer and could be reduced to scripts, specs, formulas, or steps. With predictability and routine at the core, rewarding the sought behavior and punishing the discouraged behavior produced exactly the rational response Motivation 2.0 promised, which is why the engine of commerce was fueled equally by carrots and sticks.","stage_2_intervention":"The ghost reappears, now whispering into the ear of a manager at a design agency whose briefs are open-ended and whose profits depend on novel campaigns. The manager adopts Taylor's doctrine: piece-rate bonuses per finished concept, strict timesheets, and monitoring of every idle minute. Morale sags and the agency's best creative work dries up, but the manager insists the incentives were proper.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The doctrine misfires because this is heuristic work: no algorithm exists, so success depends on experimenting with possibilities and devising novel solutions. As Teresa Amabile found, external rewards and punishments work nicely for algorithmic tasks but can be devastating for heuristic ones, because intrinsic motivation is conducive to creativity while controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to it. Carrots and sticks turned an intrinsically enjoyable craft into monitored piecework."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The two-dollar rejection: fair play beats the rational calculator","visual_frame_description":"A psychology lab table with ten dollars in cash between two chairs. A warm-hearted man offers two dollar bills with an open palm. A woman's hands hover over the money, conflicted; a thought bubble shows two numbers, '2 > 0', being crossed out by a red 'NO' stamp labeled 'FAIR PLAY'. In the background, a 2002 Nobel medal glints, captioned 'Kahneman, with Tversky'.","player_mission":"Defeat the myth of the rational calculator. First explain why people reject a two-dollar offer in the ten-dollar sharing game. Then show what that predictable irrationality does to Motivation 2.0's premise that incentives always get a rational response.","key_concepts":["Economics, as Pink's first professor taught, is not the study of money but the study of behavior.","The old view held that people are rational calculators of their economic self-interest, always doing what is best for them.","In 2002 the Nobel Foundation awarded the economics prize to Daniel Kahneman, an American psychologist, largely for showing we are not always rational calculators.","Kahneman did the work with Israeli scholar Amos Tversky, helping force a change in how we think about what we do.","In the ten-dollar sharing game, if the offer is accepted both keep the money; if rejected, neither gets anything.","Most people accepted offers of six dollars and probably five, but most people rejected offers of two dollars and below.","Rejecting two dollars makes no sense for wealth maximization: two dollars is more than zero, yet fairness, revenge, or irritation overrides the calculator.","The experiment has been replicated around the world with the same pattern of rejections.","We don't save enough for retirement even though it is to our clear economic advantage to save.","We hang on to bad investments longer than we should because the pain of losing money feels sharper than the joy of gaining the same amount.","Dan Ariely, author of Predictably Irrational, concludes that we are irrational and predictably so."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the collapse of the rational-calculator assumption: in the ultimatum-style ten-dollar game, most people worldwide reject a two-dollar offer because fairness, revenge, and irritation override calculation of self-interest. Kahneman and Tversky's behavioral-economic research shows humans are predictably irrational, undermining the premise of Motivation 2.0 that people always respond rationally to extrinsic incentives.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The rational-calculator fiction vs. predictable irrationality: people routinely reject free money in the ten-dollar sharing game because social and emotional considerations override wealth maximization, exactly the kind of finding that breaks Motivation 2.0's assumption of rational response to incentives.","stage_1_observation":"You watch an experiment replicated around the world. Someone is given ten dollars to share, some, all, or none, with a partner. If the partner accepts, both keep the money; if the partner rejects, neither gets anything. Offers of six dollars are accepted, offers of five dollars are probably accepted, but offers of two dollars and below are rejected by most people, even though accepting means the partner is two dollars richer and rejecting means nothing.","stage_1_question":"Why would anyone reject a two-dollar offer when accepting is worth two dollars and rejecting is worth zero?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because humans are not hyperrational calculators of economic self-interest. Notions of fair play, the desire for revenge, or simple irritation override the cognitive calculator in most people, which is why the rejection pattern replicates around the world. Kahneman, who won the 2002 Nobel in economics for work with Amos Tversky, helped show exactly this: we are not always rational calculators of our economic self-interest.","stage_2_intervention":"The battle escalates to a boardroom. A CFO insists his software developer can be 'rationally motivated' by sharpening incentive tiers and pain-at-risk, because everyone calculates. His VP of engineering objects, and you are called in. As evidence, consider the retirement savings gap, the bad investments people hold because loss pain outweighs equal gain joy, and the way adding an irrelevant third television changes which of two otherwise identical sets people choose.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The CFO's premise is false. We are predictably irrational, as Dan Ariely's Predictably Irrational argues: we don't save enough for retirement, we cling to losing investments because losing stings more than gaining the same amount pleases, and irrelevant third choices flip our decisions. Since the very premise of extrinsic incentives is that we will always respond rationally to them, and we clearly don't, Motivation 2.0 is hard to reconcile with how economists now think about what we do."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Bruno Frey's Mature Economic Man: intrinsic motivation in economics","visual_frame_description":"A Zurich seminar room at dusk. Bruno Frey, an economist in a tweed jacket, stands at a chalkboard drawing a figure labeled 'Homo Oeconomicus Maturus' with two chambers: a calculator and a heart. Behind him, a slide shows a weekend clarinetist practicing sheet music, a person handing in a resignation letter from a lucrative job, and a monkey at a puzzle, all captioned 'No raisins required'.","player_mission":"Battle for an upgraded view of economic man. First explain what Frey adds to behavioral economics. Then apply his thesis to a company redesigning its incentive system for knowledge workers.","key_concepts":["Bruno Frey, an economist at the University of Zurich, is the most prominent scholar widening behavioral economics toward intrinsic motivation.","Frey argues we need to move beyond Homo Oeconomicus, the fictional wealth-maximizing robot.","Frey proposes Homo Oeconomicus Maturus, or Mature Economic Man, 'endowed with a more refined motivational structure.'","Frey writes that intrinsic motivation is of great importance for all economic activities.","Frey writes that it is inconceivable that people are motivated solely or even mainly by external incentives.","People leave lucrative jobs to take low-paying ones that provide a clearer sense of purpose.","People work to master the clarinet on weekends with little hope of making money, which Motivation 2.0 cannot explain.","People play with puzzles even when they don't get raisins or dollars, echoing Harlow's monkeys.","The hyperrational calculator-brained person is a convenient fiction, not a real human being.","If people act irrationally for backward-looking reasons, there is no reason they can't also act for significance-seeking, self-actualizing reasons.","Since we are predictably irrational, we could also be predictably transcendent."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests Bruno Frey's extension of behavioral economics: replacing Homo Oeconomicus with Homo Oeconomicus Maturus, a figure with a more refined motivational structure in which intrinsic motivation is central to all economic activity. The mechanism is that humans act for purpose and significance as well as incentives, so it is inconceivable that external incentives alone drive economic behavior.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Homo Oeconomicus Maturus: the mature account of economic behavior must include intrinsic motivation and the refined motivational structure of real humans, because it is inconceivable that people are motivated solely or even mainly by external incentives.","stage_1_observation":"You are invited to a University of Zurich seminar where Bruno Frey is defending his position that behavioral economics, for all its progress, is still too narrow. Around you sit examples he loves to cite: a surgeon leaving a lucrative practice for a low-paying clinic job because it gives her work meaning, a colleague who spends weekends mastering the clarinet with no prospect of money, and a lab report on monkeys that solve puzzles for the sheer satisfaction.","stage_1_question":"What exactly does Frey argue the economics of the future must add to Homo Oeconomicus?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Frey argues we must move beyond Homo Oeconomicus, the fictional wealth-maximizing robot, to Homo Oeconomicus Maturus, who is more mature in being 'endowed with a more refined motivational structure.' Intrinsic motivation is of great importance for all economic activities, and it is inconceivable that people are motivated solely or even mainly by external incentives.","stage_2_intervention":"The seminar leads to an executive session. A Fortune 500 CFO, impressed by pay-for-performance, proposes stripping all base pay components down to maximal variable bonuses because 'people are calculators and respond to money.' Your role: show how Frey's thesis defeats this design for a workforce whose work is creative, interesting, and self-directed.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The pure variable-bonus design treats employees as Homo Oeconomicus. Frey's thesis defeats it: humans also act for significance-seeking, self-actualizing reasons, as shown by people trading lucrative jobs for purposeful ones, mastering instruments with no financial payoff, and solving puzzles without raisins. If we are predictably irrational, we can be predictably transcendent; a refined motivational structure requires intrinsic motivation, so pay alone is inconceivable as the main engine."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Work beyond the manager's gaze: the self-directed workforce","visual_frame_description":"A city at 9 a.m. shown as a constellation of glowing home offices, each a small box with a different professional at a desk: a nurse scheduling telehealth calls, a coder merging pull requests, a consultant drafting a proposal. An aerial map labels clusters '33.7 million telecommute monthly, 14.7 million daily' and '18 million non-employer businesses'. A manager's empty office chair sits alone in the corner, gathering dust.","player_mission":"Confront the collapse of Motivation 2.0's monitoring assumption. First explain how the self-directed workforce breaks the supervise-manage model. Then judge the hiring philosophy that assumes self-motivation instead of manufacturing it.","key_concepts":["Motivation 2.0 holds that because work is supposed to be dreary, people need careful monitoring so they don't shirk.","America alone has more than 18 million 'non-employer businesses'-businesses without any paid employees.","People in non-employer businesses have no underlings to manage and no bosses to manage them; they must be self-directed.","In the United States, 33.7 million people telecommute at least one day a month.","In the United States, 14.7 million people telecommute every single day.","Telecommuting places a substantial portion of the workforce beyond the gaze of a manager, forcing them to direct their own work.","Organizations are becoming leaner and less hierarchical, trimming the fatty middle and leaving managers to oversee larger numbers of people.","When managers oversee more people, each person is scrutinized less closely.","As organizations flatten, companies need people who are self-motivated, becoming more like Wikipedia, where nobody manages the Wikipedians.","Routine, not-so-interesting jobs require direction; nonroutine, more interesting work depends on self-direction.","One anonymous business leader tells job candidates: 'If you need me to motivate you, I probably don't want to hire you.'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the incompatibility between Motivation 2.0's monitor-and-supervise logic and a workforce that is increasingly self-directed and beyond managerial gaze. The mechanism is self-direction: nonroutine, interesting work depends on it, so monitoring-based motivation collapses and organizations must operate more like Wikipedia.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The monitoring assumption of Motivation 2.0 dies when work is self-directed: with millions of telecommuters, non-employer businesses, and flattened hierarchies, nobody can supervise the shirking the system fears, and nonroutine interesting work depends on self-direction rather than external monitoring.","stage_1_observation":"You stand at the window of a head office where the seats of managers are emptier than those of the specialists. On the screen behind you: the U.S. Census Bureau's 18 million non-employer businesses, businesses with no paid employees and therefore no one to manage or be managed by; then the telework numbers, 33.7 million Americans working from home at least one day a month and 14.7 million working from home every day, beyond any manager's gaze; finally the organizational chart, flattening as the fatty middle is trimmed and each manager is asked to oversee far more people.","stage_1_question":"Why does Motivation 2.0's assumption that people must be monitored to avoid shirking fail in the face of these numbers?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because monitoring is physically and organizationally impossible at that scale. Non-employer businesses have no bosses to do the motivating, telecommuters sit beyond the manager's gaze and must direct their own work, and flattened hierarchies give each manager too many people to scrutinize individually. The logic of Motivation 2.0 depends on oversight it can no longer deliver, so organizations must rely on self-directed, self-motivated people.","stage_2_intervention":"You join a hiring panel where a business leader ends interviews with an unusual statement, telling prospective employees: 'If you need me to motivate you, I probably don't want to hire you.' A skeptical recruiter objects, arguing the company should 'manage motivation' with perks and carrots. You must decide which stance the evidence supports for jobs that are nonroutine and interesting.","stage_2_correct_answer":"The leader's stance is right for nonroutine, interesting work. Routine, not-so-interesting jobs require direction, but nonroutine, more interesting work depends on self-direction, as Wikipedia shows-nobody manages the Wikipedians and nobody tries to motivate them, and that is exactly why Wikipedia works. For a workforce of self-directed specialists, hiring self-motivated people beats manufacturing motivation with prize schemes."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The bounty that backfired: open-source support at Midwest Health Cooperative","visual_frame_description":"A basement IT war room in a hospital cooperative. Maya, the IT director, projects a message board where volunteer contributors discuss a bug tracker; a proposed bounty table labeled '$25 per fixed bug' hovers next to it, casting a long shadow. Karim, a volunteer maintainer on a video call from Berlin, shrugs with a half-smile while an espresso cup beside him waits unattended.","player_mission":"Advise Maya, IT director at a hospital cooperative whose software stack runs on volunteer-maintained open-source tools. The board wants per-bug bounties to speed fixes. Guide her through what the research says actually drives open-source contributors before she announces the policy.","key_concepts":["Open source depends on intrinsic motivation with the same ferocity that older business models rely on extrinsic motivation.","Lakhani and Wolf surveyed 684 open-source developers and found enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation is the strongest and most pervasive driver.","A large majority of surveyed programmers frequently reached the state of optimal challenge called 'flow.'","Three German economists found open-source participants are driven by predominantly intrinsic motives.","The 'fun of mastering the challenge of a given software problem' is a central motive among open-source participants.","The 'desire to give a gift to the programmer community' also drives open-source participation.","Open-source people haven't taken vows of poverty; participation can burnish reputations and sharpen skills that enhance earning power.","Firefox, created almost exclusively by volunteers, has more than 150 million users.","Linux powers one in four corporate servers, and Apache holds 52 percent of the corporate Web server market.","Motivation 2.0 has little room for the intrinsic impulses that power open source."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests what actually fuels open-source participation: enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation, the fun of mastering a challenge, and the desire to give a gift to the community, all confirmed by Lakhani and Wolf's 684-developer survey and German economists' studies. Contingent cash bounties reframe the work as paid piecework and can crowd out those intrinsic drivers.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Midwest Health Cooperative runs its patient portal and scheduling systems on free open-source software maintained by volunteers across the world. A recent vulnerability took three weeks to get patched, and the board has drafted a plan to pay $25 per accepted bug fix to 'accelerate the community.' Maya, the IT director, must decide whether to adopt the bounties or present an alternative. She has called you, a motivation consultant, into a meeting with Karim, a lead volunteer maintainer, on the line from Berlin.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Maya","line":"The board's logic is simple: pay people for fixes and we'll get more fixes, faster. Twenty-five dollars per merged patch, paid monthly."},{"speaker":"Karim","line":"I've been maintaining this library for nine years. I've never been paid a cent, and I've merged over four hundred patches. The board is about to add the one thing we never wanted in here."},{"speaker":"You","line":"Karim, what keeps you coming back to a bug tracker on Friday nights after a full consulting week?"},{"speaker":"Karim","line":"Honestly? The puzzle. A gnarly race condition is like a Rubik's cube that bites back. And when a hospital in Ohio tells me their portal works because of a fix I wrote, that's the payment."},{"speaker":"Maya","line":"So you're telling me the one thing research shows drives contributors is the intrinsic joy of the work and the gift of it to the community?"},{"speaker":"You","line":"Exactly. Lakhani and Wolf surveyed 684 developers and found enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation was the strongest and most pervasive driver, and most of them hit 'flow' regularly."},{"speaker":"Karim","line":"Put a price on a patch and suddenly the interesting bugs get cherry-picked for the easy ones, and the gift culture turns into a gig economy. I've seen it happen in two other projects."},{"speaker":"Maya","line":"So instead of bounties, we publicly thank contributors, sponsor the project's yearly meetup, and route our engineers' time to the hard bugs as a gift back?"},{"speaker":"You","line":"Now you're reinforcing the motive that actually moves the community, and you'll get the reliability you need as a side effect."}],"choices":["Park the bounty policy. Ask the board to fund what contributors actually value: recognition, travel sponsorship to the project's conference, and dedicated engineer hours to take on the hardest bugs as a gift to the community.","Adopt the bounty plan as drafted. Twenty-five dollars per fix is a clear, measurable incentive that will speed up the most important patches.","Adopt the bounties but post them only for boring, low-prestige fixes so the interesting work stays volunteer-driven.","Adopt the bounties and add a monthly leaderboard of top paid contributors so the board can see the investment working."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The enjoyment-based intrinsic motivation principle of open source: Lakhani and Wolf found intrinsic enjoyment is the strongest and most pervasive driver of the 684 developers surveyed, alongside the fun of mastering a challenge and the gift motive, so cash bounties reframe the work as paid piecework and suppress those drivers.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice protects the drivers the research actually names: enjoyment, mastery, and the gift to the community, replacing contingent cash with recognition and support. The bounty choice fails because it converts a self-directed gift culture into priced piecework, crowding out the intrinsic motives Lakhani and Wolf found strongest. Pricing only 'boring' bugs still attaches external contingencies and divides the community, and the leaderboard choice adds public comparison that makes the payment scheme more salient still. Karim's testimony and the 684-developer survey both affirm that the cooperative gets more reliability by feeding the community's intrinsic motivation than by pricing it."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The per-idea fee: pricing creativity at Nocturne Agency","visual_frame_description":"A walnut-and-brass conference room at a Chicago ad agency. Ava, the creative director in a linen blazer, stands before mood boards of a car campaign while Diego, a stoic client procurement executive, slides a single-page contract across the table with '$2,000 per approved concept' in bold. A whiteboard behind them shows two columns, 'algorithmic' and 'heuristic', with the heuristic column glowing.","player_mission":"Negotiate the contract structure with a client who wants to pay per approved creative idea. Keep the campaign's creative quality intact by resisting incentive schemes that the science shows are devastating for heuristic work.","key_concepts":["A heuristic task is one where no algorithm exists, so you must experiment with possibilities and devise a novel solution.","An algorithmic task follows a set of established instructions down a single pathway to one conclusion.","Creating an ad campaign is mostly heuristic work.","Teresa Amabile of Harvard Business School found external rewards and punishments work nicely for algorithmic tasks.","Amabile found external rewards and punishments can be devastating for heuristic tasks.","Amabile's intrinsic motivation principle of creativity: intrinsic motivation is conducive to creativity; controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to creativity.","McKinsey estimates 70 percent of U.S. job growth now comes from heuristic work and 30 percent from algorithmic work.","Routine work can be outsourced or automated; artistic, empathic, nonroutine work generally cannot.","Because work has become more creative and less routine, it has also become more enjoyable.","Csikszentmihalyi found people are much more likely to report optimal experiences on the job than during leisure.","Work that economists once considered a 'disutility' is becoming a 'utility' people pursue even without a tangible return, as Vocation Vacations shows."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests Amabile's intrinsic motivation principle of creativity in a negotiation: controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to creativity while intrinsic motivation is conducive to it, and external rewards that work for algorithmic tasks can be devastating for heuristic ones. The optimal strategy refuses to price individual creative ideas and protects the team's intrinsic engagement instead.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Nocturne Agency, a 40-person creative shop, is one round from winning the Everest Motors account, a campaign worth $4 million a year. Everest's procurement chief, Diego, wants to 'align incentives': Nocturne would be paid $2,000 for every concept the client approves, with nothing for concepts rejected in review. Ava, the creative director, fears this will turn idea generation into monitored piecework and gut the campaign. She has asked you, her strategy adviser, to join the negotiation call before she signs anything.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Diego","line":"Pay-per-approved-concept is simple: we buy what we use and we never pay for flops. It keeps every creative hour accountable to the brand."},{"speaker":"Ava","line":"Diego, our concepts are not widgets. Half the winning work arrives as a wild card scribbled after a walk, not as a line item on a timesheet."},{"speaker":"You","line":"There's a research-backed reason that deal worries us. Harvard's Teresa Amabile found external rewards work fine for algorithmic tasks-rule-following work-with definite steps and one right answer."},{"speaker":"Diego","line":"And what does she say about creative work like ours?"},{"speaker":"You","line":"That controlling extrinsic rewards can be devastating for heuristic tasks. Intrinsic motivation is conducive to creativity; controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to it."},{"speaker":"Ava","line":"That's the entire campaign at stake. If every concept gets priced and judged, my teams will design to the review, not to the road."},{"speaker":"Diego","line":"So you want me to hand over four million dollars on good faith and no scoreboard?"},{"speaker":"You","line":"No-document the quality bar, pay per milestone of an agreed process, and let the craft be judged by the work, not by what it cost per rejected idea."},{"speaker":"Ava","line":"Price the risk, not the ideas. Fund the exploration, then we'll bet on the outcome together."}],"choices":["Refuse per-idea pricing. Propose a fixed campaign fee with milestone payments tied to an agreed process, and let the creative team work without per-concept surveillance.","Accept per-idea pricing as offered, since paying only for approved concepts is the fairest way to hold the agency accountable.","Accept per-idea pricing but only for final campaign deliverables, keeping concept development unpriced.","Accept per-idea pricing and install the client's dashboard to monitor every rejected concept in real time so the agency learns faster."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The intrinsic motivation principle of creativity (Teresa Amabile): intrinsic motivation is conducive to creativity and controlling extrinsic motivation is detrimental to it, so external rewards that suit algorithmic tasks can be devastating for heuristic tasks like campaign ideation.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice keeps contingent pricing off the creative act itself, exactly what Amabile's principle demands: concept generation is heuristic work and a per-idea bounty is a controlling extrinsic motivator that would narrow exploration to what reviewers will approve. Choice 1's ethical premise fails because contingent payment per approved output is precisely the controlling reward the research warns against. Choice 2 still attaches the price to the creative act at its most vulnerable stage. Choice 3 adds monitoring, magnifying the controlling character of the scheme and converting enjoyable craft into auditable piecework, the pattern the chapter shows dragging down performance exactly where it matters most."}]},{"id":"ch06","title":"CHAPTER 2 - Seven Reasons Carrots and Sticks (Often) Don’t Work . . .","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Baseline Rewards and the Threshold Effect","visual_frame_description":"Nadia, a recruiter at a midsize logistics firm, studies a wall chart of employee complaints. Across the room, a newly hired warehouse organizer complains weekly about pay while her colleague shrugs off a missed bonus. The on-screen chart renders every complaint and perk in three color-coded columns, and Nadia's cursor hovers over the salary cell as the player is asked to sort each item.","player_mission":"Classify each on-screen item into the correct column: baseline rewards, contingent if-then rewards, or punishments. Confirm that every row belongs to the same category before locking in your answer and explaining your reasoning.","key_concepts":["Baseline rewards are salary, contract payments, benefits, and perks that let people earn a living.","If someone's baseline rewards are inadequate or inequitable, her focus turns to the unfairness of her situation and the anxiety of her circumstance.","Below the baseline threshold, you get neither the predictability of extrinsic motivation nor the weirdness of intrinsic motivation - you get very little motivation at all.","Once baseline rewards are past that threshold, carrots and sticks can achieve precisely the opposite of their intended aims.","Motivation 2.0 rests on two simple ideas: rewarding an activity gets you more of it and punishing an activity gets you less of it.","Pink calls baseline rewards the starting point for any discussion of motivation in the workplace.","Rewards below the fairness threshold cannot be substituted by perks or prizes of any kind.","The threshold logic mirrors Newton's first law: elegant and simple principles that fail at the subatomic scale of the third drive."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the baseline-rewards threshold: adequate and equitable pay removes financial anxiety so that deeper motivation can operate, while inadequate pay dominates all attention. Pink argues the threshold must be crossed before either extrinsic predictability or intrinsic motivation can surface at all.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Nadia's chart lists items from the chapter's opening discussion of workplace motivation. Which single kind of reward do ALL of the following rows describe?","column_a_label":"Baseline rewards - adequate and equitable salary, contract payments, benefits, and perks","column_b_label":"Contingent 'if-then' rewards - bonuses promised in advance and tied to specific performance","column_c_label":"Punishments - fines and penalties imposed on unwanted behavior","rows":[{"label":"A salary that meets the simple fact of life that people have to earn a living","col":"a"},{"label":"Contract payments and standard benefits such as health insurance","col":"a"},{"label":"A modest perks package that keeps employees from obsessing over unfairness","col":"a"},{"label":"The threshold below which an employee's focus shifts to the unfairness of her situation","col":"a"},{"label":"The floor that must be crossed before either extrinsic or intrinsic motivation can flourish","col":"a"},{"label":"The starting point of workplace motivation that Pink discusses before any carrot or stick","col":"a"},{"label":"Money that, when missing, produces anxiety and very little motivation of any sort","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Bonus checks contingent on hitting quarterly targets","Fines charged for arriving late","Prize certificates promised for completing a task"],"explanation":"Every row names a piece of baseline reward - salary, contract payments, benefits, and perks - or a property of the threshold itself. Baseline rewards are not contingent on performance and not punitive; they simply make compensation adequate and equitable so that the anxiety of unfairness does not crowd out all motivation, which is exactly why none of the rows describes an if-then bonus or a fine."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Hidden Costs of Rewards (Preschool Drawing Study)","visual_frame_description":"Inside a sunlit preschool classroom, three teachers lay out paper and markers during free play while two researchers watch from behind a one-way mirror. In the corner, a child who once loved drawing now pushes a crayon away. Floating labels identify each experimental group, and the player must identify which group every observed behavior describes.","player_mission":"Match each two-week-later observation to its experimental group: expected-award, unexpected-award, or no-award children. Identify the single group that every row describes and defend the classification.","key_concepts":["Lepper, Greene, and Nisbett watched preschoolers during free play to identify children who chose to spend time drawing.","The 'expected-award' children were shown a 'Good Player' certificate and asked if they wanted to draw in order to receive it.","The 'unexpected-award' children were simply asked to draw and received a certificate only afterward.","The 'no-award' children drew without any promise or delivery of a certificate.","Two weeks later, the expected-award children showed much less interest and spent much less time drawing.","Unexpected-award and no-award children drew just as much, and with the same relish, as before the experiment.","Only contingent rewards - if you do this, then you'll get that - had the negative effect on intrinsic motivation.","If-then rewards require people to forfeit some of their autonomy, draining an activity of its enjoyment.","The classic 1978 volume on these findings was called 'The Hidden Costs of Rewards,' edited by Lepper and Greene.","The phenomenon was described earlier as the Sawyer Effect: rewards can transform an interesting task into a drudge."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the hidden costs of contingent rewards: expected, promised prizes turned drawing from play into work, while unexpected or absent rewards left intrinsic motivation intact. Deci's later analysis of 128 experiments confirmed that tangible rewards tend to have a substantially negative effect on intrinsic motivation.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"The classroom observations below were recorded two weeks after the experiment ended. Which group of children do ALL of these observations describe?","column_a_label":"Expected-award children - promised a 'Good Player' certificate for drawing","column_b_label":"Unexpected-award children - received a certificate only after choosing to draw","column_c_label":"No-award children - neither promised nor given any certificate","rows":[{"label":"Two weeks later showed much less interest in drawing","col":"a"},{"label":"Spent much less time with the paper and markers set out during free play","col":"a"},{"label":"Children who had clearly enjoyed drawing before the reward was introduced","col":"a"},{"label":"The group in which the Sawyer Effect had taken hold","col":"a"},{"label":"Those whose intrinsic motivation was dampened by an expected, promised prize","col":"a"},{"label":"The children for whom those alluring prizes had turned play into work","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Children who drew as much and with as much relish as before the experiment","Children who never saw a certificate at any point","Children whose drawing time increased after receiving an award"],"explanation":"All six rows describe the expected-award group: children promised a certificate in advance who, two weeks later, drew less. The unexpected-award and no-award groups maintained their prior interest, which is why those groups appear in the distractors rather than the rows - the distinguishing variable was not the reward itself but whether it was contingent and expected."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Heuristic Tasks and Reward-Narrowed Focus","visual_frame_description":"On a laboratory desk sit a candle, a box of tacks, and a book of matches beside a wooden wall. A clock ticks as two participants stare at the materials, one of them glancing nervously at a stopwatch that promises a $5 prize. The screen asks the player to sort seven task descriptions into the category they all share.","player_mission":"Determine which kind of task all seven rows describe: heuristic, algorithmic, or purely mechanical. Apply the candle-problem logic to lock in the classification.","key_concepts":["The candle problem, devised by psychologist Karl Duncker in the 1930s, asks people to attach a candle to a wall so wax doesn't drip on the table.","The solution requires overcoming 'functional fixedness' - seeing the tack box not as a container but as a platform for the candle.","Sam Glucksberg timed two groups solving the candle problem, offering $5 to the fastest quarter and $20 to the fastest solver.","The incentivized group took nearly three and a half minutes longer to find the solution.","Rewards narrow our focus; that is helpful when there is a clear path to a solution, but they blinkered the wide view that allows new uses for old objects.","Amabile has found that extrinsic rewards can be effective for algorithmic tasks but dangerous for right-brain undertakings.","Algorithmic tasks depend on following an existing formula to its logical conclusion.","Rewarded subjects often have a harder time seeing the periphery and crafting original solutions.","Intrinsic motivation - doing something because it is interesting, challenging, and absorbing - is essential for high levels of creativity."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the distinction between heuristic and algorithmic tasks: contingent rewards narrow focus, which helps on formula-driven tasks but cripples open-ended, inventive problem solving. Glucksberg's timed candle experiments showed the prize-seeking group solving the puzzle on average three and a half minutes slower than the reward-free group.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"All of the rows below are examples of the same kind of task - the kind where 'if-then' rewards narrow focus and hurt performance. Which kind is it?","column_a_label":"Heuristic, right-brain tasks - demand flexible problem-solving, inventiveness, and conceptual understanding","column_b_label":"Algorithmic tasks - following an existing formula to its logical conclusion","column_c_label":"Mechanical tasks - pure motor skill with no cognitive demand","rows":[{"label":"Solving Duncker's candle problem","col":"a"},{"label":"Iterating a novel product design that has never been tried before","col":"a"},{"label":"Discovering a nonobvious solution when there is no set path to follow","col":"a"},{"label":"Producing an original artistic work from a blank canvas","col":"a"},{"label":"Seeing the periphery of a problem and spotting new uses for old objects","col":"a"},{"label":"Conceptual work that demands breaking from the path to find a novel strategy","col":"a"},{"label":"The kind of undertaking where Amabile found contingent rewards can be dangerous","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Restocking shelves by following a printed store layout","Executing a standard audit checklist line by line","Filling a tube assembly in four prescribed steps"],"explanation":"Each row describes a task solved by heuristics - breaking from a set path to discover a novel strategy, exactly the profile of the candle problem. The distractors are algorithmic or mechanical tasks, where a clear path exists and an incentive can safely accelerate progress, which is why they do not belong in this matrix."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Incentive Size and Performance (Ariely's Madurai Study)","visual_frame_description":"In a rented hall in Madurai, India, eighty-seven participants fling tennis balls at a target, unscramble anagrams, and repeat strings of digits. A clipboard lists the three prize tiers: 4 rupees, 40 rupees, and 400 rupees - nearly five months' pay. The player must sort the study's findings into the single pattern they all share.","player_mission":"Classify each finding from the Madurai experiment into the correct column, then identify the pattern that describes all of the results. Choose the column that matches the direction of the incentive-performance relationship.","key_concepts":["Dan Ariely and three other economists ran the study in Madurai, India, where living costs allowed large rewards without breaking the researchers' banks.","Eighty-seven participants played games requiring motor skills, creativity, or concentration: tossing tennis balls at a target, unscrambling anagrams, and recalling strings of digits.","Reward tiers were 4 rupees (about a day's pay), 40 rupees (about two weeks' pay), and 400 rupees (about five months' pay).","Participants offered the medium bonus performed no better than those offered the small one.","The 400-rupee super-incentivized group fared worst of all, lagging behind both other groups by nearly every measure.","The researchers wrote: 'In eight of the nine tasks we examined across the three experiments, higher incentives led to worse performance.'","The results were reported for the Federal Reserve Bank of Boston.","In 2009, scholars at the London School of Economics analyzed fifty-one studies of corporate pay-for-performance plans and found financial incentives can result in a negative impact on overall performance.","The authors warned that one cannot assume introducing or raising incentives always improves performance."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the incentive-size paradox: for tasks requiring even rudimentary cognitive skill, larger contingent rewards produced worse performance, with the highest-paid group performing worst in eight of nine tasks. The finding held in the LSE's 2009 review of fifty-one pay-for-performance studies, which likewise found incentives can dampen overall performance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"The rows below are findings from Ariely and colleagues' Madurai experiments. Which single pattern do ALL of them describe?","column_a_label":"Higher incentives led to worse performance, with the biggest rewards backfiring","column_b_label":"Higher incentives steadily improved performance on every task","column_c_label":"Incentives affected only physical effort and never cognitive or creative tasks","rows":[{"label":"Participants offered 400 rupees lagged behind both smaller-reward groups by nearly every measure","col":"a"},{"label":"The medium-bonus group performed about the same as the small-bonus group","col":"a"},{"label":"In eight of the nine tasks across the three experiments, higher incentives led to worse performance","col":"a"},{"label":"Larger rewards backfired on tasks requiring motor skills, creativity, or concentration","col":"a"},{"label":"A result the researchers reported to the Federal Reserve Bank of Boston","col":"a"},{"label":"A pattern echoed by the London School of Economics review of fifty-one pay-for-performance plans","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["The largest reward group outscored all others on every task","The smallest reward group performed worst because the money was meaningless","Only the anagram task responded to incentive size while all others were unaffected"],"explanation":"Every row states that bigger monetary incentives coincided with equal or worse performance - the core result of the Madurai study and of the LSE's fifty-one-study review. The distractors assume the Motivation 2.0 pattern of larger rewards producing better performance, which is precisely the assumption the data refuted."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Goal Setting Gone Wild","visual_frame_description":"Three news screens flicker behind a conference table: a Sears garage overcharging a customer, the Enron tower at dusk, and a Ford Pinto on a test track. A business school panel displays a warning label: 'Goals may cause systematic problems.' The player sorts each scandal into one shared category.","player_mission":"Sort each corporative scandal or misbehavior into its shared category among three columns. Identify the common mechanism that links Sears, Enron, Ford, and the rest.","key_concepts":["Goals can help us tune out distractions, try harder, work longer, and achieve more.","Business school scholars from Harvard, Kellogg, Eller, and Wharton argued goal setting should be prescribed selectively, presented with a warning label, and closely monitored.","Goals people set for themselves and devote to attaining mastery are usually healthy.","Goals imposed by others - sales targets, quarterly returns, standardized test scores - can have dangerous side effects.","Substantial evidence demonstrates that goal setting can induce unethical behavior.","Sears imposed a sales quota on its auto repair staff, and workers responded by overcharging customers and completing unnecessary repairs.","Enron set lofty revenue goals, and the race to meet them by any means catalyzed the company's collapse.","Ford was so intent on producing a certain car at a certain weight and price by a certain date that it omitted safety checks and unleashed the dangerous Ford Pinto.","Executives game quarterly earnings, counselors doctor transcripts, and athletes use steroids to trigger bonuses.","When the reward is the activity itself, there are no shortcuts; the only route to the destination is the high road."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests goal-induced unethical behavior: imposed, short-term, measurable goals narrow focus so sharply that people take the quickest route to the reward, including shortcuts. The scholars' warning label - goals may cause unethical behavior, increased risk taking, and decreased cooperation - is illustrated by Sears, Enron, and Ford.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"The rows below are examples cited in the chapter's discussion of goals. Which single outcome do ALL of them describe?","column_a_label":"Unethical behavior induced by imposed, extrinsically rewarded goals","column_b_label":"Healthy self-set mastery goals that deepen learning","column_c_label":"Goals that increase cooperation and reduce risk taking","rows":[{"label":"Sears' auto repair staff overcharging customers and completing unnecessary repairs to meet quotas","col":"a"},{"label":"Enron's race to meet lofty revenue goals by any means possible","col":"a"},{"label":"Ford omitting safety checks to produce its Pinto at the targeted weight, price, and date","col":"a"},{"label":"Executives gaming quarterly earnings so they can snag a performance bonus","col":"a"},{"label":"Secondary school counselors doctoring transcripts so seniors can get into college","col":"a"},{"label":"Athletes injecting steroids to post better numbers and trigger lucrative bonuses","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Students choosing difficult problems to learn more in a subject they love","Teams sharing information voluntarily to improve a joint design","Workers doing the bare minimum to collect a paycheck"],"explanation":"Every example is a documented case of people taking the low road to reach an imposed, extrinsically rewarded goal - overcharging, doctoring records, doping, or omitting safety checks. The distractors describe mastery-oriented self-chosen goals, where no shortcut exists because the person disadvantaged would be oneself."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Sawyer Effect (Tom Sawyer's Fence)","visual_frame_description":"A sleepy Missouri fence line on a Saturday morning: Tom Sawyer drags a brush across the whitewash while Ben Rogers ambles by, apple in hand, laughing at Tom's 'sorry lot.' Tom suddenly straightens up and announces that painting a fence is a fantastic privilege - so captivating that he refuses to let Ben try, until Ben trades the apple for a few strokes.","player_mission":"Study the fence scene and identify the motivational principle Twain extracts from it. Then take over as a summer camp arts director who must decide whether to pay kids for painting a mural, choosing the option that preserves intrinsic motivation.","key_concepts":["Tom Sawyer was faced with whitewashing Aunt Polly's 810-square-foot fence and found the task a burden.","When Ben mocked him, Tom pretended the job was a fantastic privilege, and Ben traded his apple for the chance to paint.","Twain's principle: 'Work consists of whatever a body is OBLIGED to do, and that Play consists of whatever a body is not obliged to do.'","English gentlemen drove four-horse passenger coaches for the privilege of paying for it, but would resign if offered wages.","Rewards can perform behavioral alchemy: they can transform an interesting task into a drudge and turn play into work.","The Sawyer Effect can diminish performance, creativity, and even upstanding behavior.","Only contingent, expected 'if-then' rewards dampen intrinsic motivation.","Rewards narrow our focus; that is helpful on routine tasks but harmful on inventive ones."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the Sawyer Effect: a contingent reward converts an intrinsically interesting activity into an obligation, draining it of enjoyment. Twain's carriage-driving gentlemen and the preschool drawing experiments both show that paying or rewarding people for what they already enjoy can make them enjoy it far less.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The Sawyer Effect is the central concept: rewards can perform a weird sort of behavioral alchemy, transforming an interesting task into a drudge and turning play into work. Because Tom turned a chore into a privilege, other boys paid for the right to do it - the reverse of paying people to do what they already love.","stage_1_observation":"Watch the fence scene: first Tom is miserable at the thought of whitewashing, then, when Ben arrives, he acts as if painting is a privilege. Ben begs to try. Next to the scene, a footnote shows wealthy English gentlemen paying money to drive four-horse coaches - yet the story says they would resign if offered wages for the service.","stage_1_question":"Why would the gentlemen who pay to drive coaches resign if wages were offered, and why did Ben hand over his apple? Options: A) Money makes the activity feel valuable and worth having. B) Being paid converts a privilege into an obligation, draining the activity of its intrinsic enjoyment. C) People only enjoy activities that cost them something. D) The gentlemen feared the wages would attract competition.","stage_1_correct_answer":"B) Being paid converts a privilege into an obligation, draining the activity of its intrinsic enjoyment. Twain's principle is that work is what a body is obliged to do and play is what it is not obliged to do - offering wages turns play into work, and that is precisely the behavioral alchemy the Sawyer Effect describes.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the director of a summer arts camp. Nine-year-old campers love painting the mural wall, and the wall is half finished with two weeks left. A well-meaning counselor suggests offering each camper $5 for every quarter of the mural they complete to guarantee it gets done. You must decide how to structure the mural project.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Skip the per-section payment. Offering a contingent, expected 'if-then' prize would convert their play into work and drain the enjoyment from painting - exactly what happened to the preschoolers promised a 'Good Player' certificate. Keep the reward the activity itself, step back, and let the campers finish the wall as play.","stage_1_options":"A) Money makes the activity feel valuable and worth having. B) Being paid converts a privilege into an obligation, draining the activity of its intrinsic enjoyment. C) People only enjoy activities that cost them something. D) The gentlemen feared the wages would attract competition."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Blood Donation and Crowding Out Altruism","visual_frame_description":"At a regional blood center in Gothenburg, Sweden, a recruiter hands a clipboard to one of 153 women in a bright waiting room. Three sign-up tables are labeled: voluntary, 50 kronor payment, and 50 kronor payable to a children's cancer charity. Donation rates appear on a screen as the player weighs national blood policy.","player_mission":"Analyze the three donation conditions and predict their outcomes based on crowding-out. Then step into the health ministry and choose the payment policy that protects altruistic donation rather than suppressing it.","key_concepts":["In 1970, British sociologist Richard Titmuss speculated that paying for blood would actually reduce the blood supply.","Two Swedish economists tested Titmuss's hunch at a regional blood center in Gothenburg with 153 interested women.","The voluntary group, offered no payment, saw 52 percent of women donate blood.","The group offered 50 kronor (about $7) saw only 30 percent of women donate - nearly half as many.","The group offered 50 kronor with an immediate option to donate it to a children's cancer charity saw 53 percent donate.","Payment tainted an altruistic act and 'crowded out' the intrinsic desire to do something good.","The reward itself was not inherently destructive; the option to give it away negated the effect.","When the Italian government gave blood donors paid time off work, donations increased because the law removed an obstacle to altruism.","Mixing rewards with inherently noble tasks is dangerous, but some rewards are not inherently bad in all circumstances.","Voluntary blood donations invariably increase during natural disasters and other calamities."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests motivational crowding-out: offering cash for an altruistic act converts a moral act into a transaction, and donation rates collapsed from 52 percent to 30 percent. Titmuss's philosophical hunch was confirmed in Gothenburg, while the Italian paid-time-off example shows rewards that remove obstacles do not crowd out generosity.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is crowding out: when a monetary incentive is attached to an intrinsically noble act, the payment taints the act and suppresses the intrinsic desire to do good. The Gothenburg experiment showed payment nearly halving donation rates, while an option to donate the fee to charity restored participation to voluntary levels.","stage_1_observation":"Observe the Gothenburg recruitment room: the recruiter explains each group's terms before anyone commits. The voluntary table fills steadily; the cash table's sign-ups stall. On screen, bars track the eventual donation rates: 52 percent voluntary, 30 percent paid, 53 percent charity-option, and a footnote shows donations from paid-time-off donors in Italy climbing.","stage_1_question":"What do the final donation rates reveal about adding a monetary incentive to an altruistic act? Options: A) Cash raised donation rates, confirming Motivation 2.0. B) Cash nearly halved donation rates, crowding out the intrinsic desire to do good. C) Cash had no effect whatsoever on any group. D) Cash only affected first-time donors, not regular ones.","stage_1_correct_answer":"B) Cash nearly halved donation rates, crowding out the intrinsic desire to do good. The 50-kronor payment dropped participation from 52 percent to 30 percent, while the charity-option group behaved like the voluntary group - evidence that the payment tainted the altruistic act and suppressed it, exactly as Titmuss predicted.","stage_2_intervention":"As director of the national health ministry, you must choose a policy for boosting the blood supply. The options: A) Pay every donor 50 kronor cash, escalating to 100 kronor if donations lag. B) Offer paid time off work for donation, as Italy did. C) Add a small cash bonus only during seasonal shortages. D) Require employers to hold 'donation day' events with door prizes for attendance.","stage_2_correct_answer":"B) Offer paid time off work for donation, as Italy did. This reward removes an obstacle to altruism without tainting the act itself, so donations increased rather than declining. Cash payment for giving blood would crowd out the intrinsic desire to do good, and escalating or door-prize schemes attach transactions to the act instead of removing barriers.","stage_1_options":"A) Cash raised donation rates, confirming Motivation 2.0. B) Cash nearly halved donation rates, crowding out the intrinsic desire to do good. C) Cash had no effect whatsoever on any group. D) Cash only affected first-time donors, not regular ones."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Haifa Day-Care Fine","visual_frame_description":"At a sunny day care center in Haifa, Israel, parents rush through the lobby doors at 4:10 P.M. while a toddler waves from the window. A freshly posted sign announces a NS 10 fine for late pickup, approved by the Authority for Private Day-Care Centers in Israel. Below the sign, a chart climbs week by week.","player_mission":"Study the day-care experiment's design and predict what the fine did, then decide the manager's next move when the fine backfires. Choose the intervention that restores punctuality without converting a moral obligation into a transaction.","key_concepts":["Economists Uri Gneezy and Aldo Rustichini studied child care facilities in Haifa, Israel, for twenty weeks.","During the first four weeks, they recorded how many parents arrived late each week as a baseline.","Before the fifth week, a sign was posted imposing a NS 10 fine on parents who collected children after 16:10.","The theory was: 'When negative consequences are imposed on a behavior, they will produce a reduction of that particular response.'","After the fine, the economists observed a steady increase in the number of parents coming late.","The late rate finally settled at a level higher and almost twice as large as the initial one.","The 'possibility of an increase in the behavior being punished was not even considered' in the literature.","Parents were punctual partly from an intrinsic desire to treat the teachers fairly.","The fine shifted the parents' decision from a partly moral obligation to a pure transaction - there wasn't room for both.","The punishment didn't promote good behavior; it crowded it out."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests crowding out by punishment: the fine converted a moral obligation toward the teachers into a purchasable transaction, and lateness nearly doubled instead of falling. Gneezy and Rustichini showed the threat of a fine edged aside the third drive, exactly as the kronor payment did in the blood experiment.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that punishment, like reward, can crowd out intrinsic motivation: parents were punctual out of fairness toward the teachers, but the fine reframed lateness as a purchasable transaction. The result was a steady increase in lateness to nearly twice the baseline rate.","stage_1_observation":"Observe the four-week baseline: a modest number of parents arrive late each week, and teachers stay an extra few minutes without complaint. When the NS 10 fine sign goes up before week five, track the weekly chart. The line drifts upward for weeks instead of falling, and the economists' notes describe 'head scratching' at a result the literature never anticipated.","stage_1_question":"What happened to the number of late parents after the fine was introduced? Options: A) Lateness fell sharply and stayed low. B) Lateness was unchanged from the baseline. C) Lateness rose steadily, settling at a level almost twice the initial one. D) Lateness briefly fell, then returned exactly to baseline.","stage_1_correct_answer":"C) Lateness rose steadily, settling at a level almost twice the initial one. Instead of reducing the punished response, the fine increased it: the threat converted a moral obligation into a transaction, and the literature had not even considered the possibility that the punished behavior would increase.","stage_2_intervention":"Six weeks into the experiment, you are the day-care manager watching the board meet: one member wants to double the fine to NS 20; another wants to add a public wall listing the names of late parents; a third argues the fine should simply be withdrawn and the original announcement apologized for. You must choose the board's course.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Withdraw the fine and repair the moral relationship. Doubling the fine or publishing names deepens the transaction and adds humiliation; the experiment shows the fine itself crowded out parents' intrinsic desire to be scrupulous about punctuality with the teachers. Removing it restores the partly moral obligation that originally kept most parents on time.","stage_1_options":"A) Lateness fell sharply and stayed low. B) Lateness was unchanged from the baseline. C) Lateness rose steadily, settling at a level almost twice the initial one. D) Lateness briefly fell, then returned exactly to baseline."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Reward Anticipation and Addiction (Knutson's Scanner)","visual_frame_description":"Inside an fMRI lab, a volunteer lies in a giant scanner while a screen flashes win and loss conditions. A brain map lights up the nucleus accumbens in warm gold whenever a cash win is anticipated. Beside the scan, the economist Anton Suvorov's equations scroll, and a casino floor glitters in the background.","player_mission":"Interpret the brain-scan evidence about reward anticipation, then act as a sales director deciding whether to escalate your sales contest prizes. Choose the policy that avoids turning rewards into an addiction-like escalation.","key_concepts":["Brian Knutson used fMRI to watch volunteers' brains during a game involving the prospect of winning or losing money.","When participants knew they had a chance to win cash, a burst of the brain chemical dopamine surged to the nucleus accumbens.","The same basic physiological process that occurs in reward anticipation is what happens in addiction.","Most addictive drugs send a fusillade of dopamine to the nucleus accumbens; the feeling delights, then dissipates, then demands another dose.","Brain scans of people anticipating monetary rewards look disturbingly similar to scans of people given cocaine, nicotine, or amphetamines.","Knutson found that activation in the nucleus accumbens predicts both risky choices and risk-seeking mistakes.","Casinos surround guests with reward cues because anticipation of rewards may push people from risk-averse to risk-seeking behavior.","Anton Suvorov's principal-agent model shows a contingent reward signals the task is undesirable and forces the principal to keep offering rewards.","Once a reward is offered, 'the principal is doomed to give it again in the second' round, and larger payments are eventually required.","Paying people to stop smoking often works in the short run because it replaces one dangerous addiction with another more benign one."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the addictive character of contingent rewards: anticipated rewards trigger dopamine surges in the nucleus accumbens mirroring drug addiction, and Suvorov's model shows the giver becomes trapped in escalating payments. Reward anticipation also fuels risky, risk-seeking mistakes rather than wiser decisions.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is reward addiction: contingent rewards trigger the same dopamine-to-nucleus-accumbens response as addictive drugs, and once offered they make the recipient expect them on every similar task, forcing givers to escalate. Anticipated rewards also predict risky, risk-seeking mistakes rather than better decisions.","stage_1_observation":"Watch the scanner screen: when the game signals a potential win, the nucleus accumbens lights up; when a potential loss is signaled, it stays dark. A graph overlays Knutson's finding that the same activation predicts risky choices and risk-seeking mistakes, and Suvorov's model shows a principal who pays once is 'doomed to give it again in the second.'","stage_1_question":"What does activation in the nucleus accumbens during reward anticipation predict? Options: A) Perfectly rational, risk-averse decision making. B) Both risky choices and risk-seeking mistakes. C) A temporary increase in cooperation with rivals. D) No observable change in any subsequent behavior.","stage_1_correct_answer":"B) Both risky choices and risk-seeking mistakes. Knutson found nucleus accumbens activation predicts risky choices and risk-seeking mistakes, which is why casinos surround guests with reward cues - anticipation of rewards can switch people from risk-averse to risk-seeking behavior.","stage_2_intervention":"You direct sales at a software company. Your quarterly contest prizes have already grown from $500 to $2,000 because reps shrug off the smaller amounts, and several reps now ask which prize bump they will get before agreeing to run new campaigns. The VP proposes a $10,000 mega-prize drawing at year-end to 'get everyone fired up.' Decide how to respond.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Reject the escalating mega-prize and unwind the contingent contest. The pattern described is textbook reward escalation: each prize becomes the status quo and demands a larger one, while the anticipation fuels risky, reward-chasing behavior rather than better performance. Keep baseline pay fair, give public noncontingent recognition, and stop dangling ever-larger carrots.","stage_1_options":"A) Perfectly rational, risk-averse decision making. B) Both risky choices and risk-seeking mistakes. C) A temporary increase in cooperation with rivals. D) No observable change in any subsequent behavior."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Quarterly Earnings and Short-Term Myopia","visual_frame_description":"In a glass-walled conference room, two stock charts hang side by side: one company guides analysts every quarter, the other rarely. A ticker scrolls earnings estimates while a researcher's note reads: 'The very presence of goals may lead employees to focus myopically on short-term gains.' In the corner, a 2008 housing-for-sale sign rots at an angle.","player_mission":"Evaluate the evidence linking quarterly guidance to long-term growth, then decide as CFO whether executive pay should ride on quarterly earnings. Choose the policy that lifts the company's sights toward the horizon.","key_concepts":["Extrinsic motivators can reduce the depth of thinking, focusing people on what is immediately before them rather than the distance.","Publicly held companies often aim single-mindedly at performance over the next three months, obsessing over quarterly earnings.","Researchers found that companies that spend the most time offering guidance on quarterly earnings deliver significantly lower long-term growth rates.","Earnings-obsessed companies typically invest less in research and development.","The scholars warned: 'The very presence of goals may lead employees to focus myopically on short-term gains.'","In the 2008-2009 economic calamity, each player focused on short-term reward and ignored long-term effects on themselves or others.","Economic bubbles are a bad case of extrinsically motivated myopia.","Mastery is inherently long-term because complete mastery is unattainable - even Roger Federer will never fully master tennis.","Schoolchildren paid to solve problems typically choose easier problems and therefore learn less.","Students who get a prize for reading three books often won't pick up a fourth.","Greatness and nearsightedness are incompatible; meaningful achievement depends on lifting one's sights toward the horizon."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the short-term myopia induced by tangible if-then motivators: a laser focus on quarterly earnings narrows the horizon, depresses R&D, and yields lower long-term growth. The 2008 crisis illustrated how everyone gaming the near-term reward nearly collapsed the entire system.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is extrinsically motivated myopia: tangible if-then rewards focus attention on the immediate prize and crowd out the long view. Companies obsessed with quarterly guidance grow more slowly in the long run, a pattern the 2008 economic crisis made vivid.","stage_1_observation":"Observe the two companies: Meridian spends enormous resources guiding analysts each quarter and resists R&D proposals; Vista quietly builds a two-year product plan and rarely offers earnings guidance. A note on the researcher's desk links heavy guidance to lower long-term growth, and a chart shows quarterly beaters lagging over five years.","stage_1_question":"What do researchers find about companies that spend the most time offering guidance on quarterly earnings? Options: A) They deliver the highest long-term growth because transparency attracts investors. B) They deliver significantly lower long-term growth rates and typically invest less in R&D. C) They show identical growth to non-guiding companies once risk is adjusted. D) They outperform all competitors during recessions only.","stage_1_correct_answer":"B) They deliver significantly lower long-term growth rates and typically invest less in R&D. The earnings-obsessed companies successfully achieve their short-term goals while threatening the health of the company two or three years hence - exactly the myopia the goal-setting researchers warn about.","stage_2_intervention":"You are founding CFO of a new division and must design executive compensation: Option A) All bonuses tied to beating quarterly EPS estimates. B) Salaries at market baseline with 70 percent of incentive pay tied to three-year goals and customer outcomes. C) A quarterly EPS bonus plus R&D budget cuts to fund it. D) Payment in stock options that vest only if the stock hits an 18-month target.","stage_2_correct_answer":"B) Salaries at market baseline with 70 percent of incentive pay tied to three-year goals and customer outcomes. This keeps baseline rewards adequate while stretching the compensation horizon past the quarterly obsession that depressed growth, reduced R&D, and fed the myopic behavior behind the 2008 crisis. The other options all concentrate attention on near-term numbers.","stage_1_options":"A) They deliver the highest long-term growth because transparency attracts investors. B) They deliver significantly lower long-term growth rates and typically invest less in R&D. C) They show identical growth to non-guiding companies once risk is adjusted. D) They outperform all competitors during recessions only."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Math Workbook Allowance (Suvorov's Addictive Rewards)","visual_frame_description":"In a warm kitchen on a Tuesday evening, nine-year-old Maya slumps over a math workbook while her father Marcus washes dishes and her mother Eva sips tea at the table. The workbook has a half-finished page; a stack of LEGO bricks sits untouched on the counter, and a piggy bank on the shelf catches the lamplight.","player_mission":"Listen to the family debate about paying Maya for workbook pages and choose what Marcus actually says to her. Select the response that protects Maya's intrinsic motivation and avoids locking the family into escalating payments.","key_concepts":["Rewarding an activity will get you more of it; punishing it will get you less - the two elegant ideas of Motivation 2.0.","Contingent 'if-then' rewards require people to forfeit some of their autonomy.","If-then rewards can spring a hole in the bottom of the motivational bucket, draining an activity of its enjoyment.","Suvorov's principal-agent model: by offering a reward, a principal signals to the agent that the task is undesirable.","Pay your son to take out the trash and you have pretty much guaranteed the kid will never do it again for free.","Once offered, a contingent reward makes an agent expect it whenever a similar task is faced, compelling the principal to use rewards over and over again.","The existing reward will quickly feel less like a bonus and more like the status quo, forcing larger rewards to achieve the same effect.","Anticipated rewards trigger dopamine to the nucleus accumbens, the same circuitry as addiction.","Schoolchildren paid to solve problems typically choose easier problems and therefore learn less.","Try to encourage a kid to learn math by paying her for each workbook page and she will become more diligent in the short term but lose interest in math in the long term."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests Suvorov's reward-addiction model and the Sawyer Effect: an if-then payment signals that math is undesirable, teaches Maya to expect payment, and drains the activity's intrinsic appeal. Pink warns that per-page payments produce short-term diligence but long-term loss of interest in math.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Marcus is fed up with nightly battles over Maya's math workbook. He proposes paying Maya $2 per completed page, hoping short-term diligence will build a habit. Eva is uneasy - she remembers the family's reading program where the same trick produced three books and then nothing. Marcus has to decide what to say to Maya tonight, and the stakes are Maya's long-term attitude toward math and whether homework remains a nightly fight.","dialogue":["Eva: Marcus, don't. In the summer reading program we paid her per book, and she read exactly three - the minimum - and has barely touched a book since.","Marcus: But that's different, the pages are the problem. If I pay per page, she'll actually sit down and finish the workbook.","Maya: (looking up from her workbook) Are you going to give me money for doing pages?","Eva: The bigger issue is what the money says about the math. If we pay her, we're telling her math is a chore that nobody would do for free.","Marcus: But at least the pages would get done tonight.","Maya: I hate that the page has twenty questions. I only want to do the geometry ones.","Eva: Then let her choose the geometry ones. She's not going to enjoy it more because you're waving a dollar at her.","Marcus: (sighs) Alright, I need to actually decide what I'm going to say to her."],"choices":["Maya, I'll give you two dollars for every workbook page you finish tonight. The more you do, the more you earn.","Let's leave the money out of it. Maya, which pages look most interesting to you? Show me the tricky ones and we'll crack them together.","If you don't finish three pages tonight, no LEGO for the rest of the week. Your call.","If you finish all five pages tonight, I'll double your allowance this month - the whole five dollars."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Suvorov's reward-addiction model and the Sawyer Effect: a contingent reward signals that the task is undesirable and forces the giver to keep paying, while an if-then payment drains intrinsic motivation, converting play into work and leaving little motivation once the prize disappears.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it keeps the reward the activity itself - Maya's curiosity about geometry - and treats the task as something jointly interesting, mirroring how intrinsic motivation survives only without contingent payments. Choice 0 creates the exact if-then trap: short-term diligence, a fading buzz, and a guarantee she will expect payment for every future page. Choice 2 substitutes a stick for a carrot, which crowds out the moral or intrinsic pull just as the day-care fine did, and Choice 3 merely converts the per-page bribe into an conditional allowance - still a contingent deal that signals math is undesirable work."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Quarter-End Revenue Call","visual_frame_description":"In a corner office at Meridian Software on the last day of the quarter, regional sales director Tom leans over a spreadsheet glowing with a $400,000 revenue gap. Account executive Priya sits across from him, phone in hand, listening to a customer who has not yet signed. The clock on the wall reads 3:47 P.M.","player_mission":"Play Priya and decide how to answer Tom's pressure to book unearned revenue before midnight. Choose the response that refuses the shortcut and defends the high-road logic of intrinsic motivation.","key_concepts":["When an extrinsic goal is paramount - particularly a short-term, measurable one with a big payoff - it can restrict our view of the broader dimensions of our behavior.","Goal setting can induce unethical behavior: executives game quarterly earnings so they can snag a performance bonus.","The problem with making an extrinsic reward the only destination that matters is that some people will choose the quickest route there, even if it means taking the low road.","When the reward is the activity itself, there are no shortcuts; the only route to the destination is the high road.","It is almost impossible to act unethically when the person disadvantaged is not a competitor but yourself.","The very presence of goals may lead employees to focus myopically on short-term gains and lose sight of long-term effects.","Financial incentives can result in a negative impact on overall performance, per the LSE review of fifty-one pay-for-performance plans.","Rewards narrow our focus, which is helpful when there is a clear path but dangerous otherwise."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the unethical-behavior flaw of if-then goals: the quarterly bonus narrows Tom's focus so far that he asks a rep to record revenue that is not earned. Pink notes that when the reward is the activity itself there are no shortcuts, because the person disadvantaged would be oneself.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Priya is four days from closing a genuinely good deal with a hospital network, but the contract has not been signed. Tom's bonus - and Priya's - depends on hitting the quarter's number, and Tom suggests flagging the deal as revenue now and backdating paperwork after the signature. The conversation happens in front of finance's open-door policy, and the stakes are Priya's integrity, the company's books, and her relationship with the buyer.","dialogue":["Tom: We are $400,000 short with four hours of runway. Your hospital deal pushes us over the line - you just have to get it on the books today.","Priya: Tom, they haven't signed. Legal review runs through Monday.","Tom: We both know the deal is good. Flag it as closed tonight, backdate the paperwork when the signature lands. Nobody audits a single deal.","Priya: If I book revenue that isn't earned, finance will report a quarter that didn't actually happen.","Tom: That's their problem. My problem is a bonus cliff, and yours is too. Do you want to be a team player here or not?","Priya: I want to make the number honestly. If recording it now means pretending it happened, I don't think that's teamwork - I think that's what got companies like this one into the news.","Tom: (long pause) So what are you telling me?"],"choices":["Fine - I'll flag it as closed tonight and we'll sort the paperwork after the signature. One deal won't change anything.","I won't book revenue that isn't earned, Tom. The customer signs Monday and we book it then - and I'll tell finance exactly what happened so we can explain it. My job is to sell well, not to make this quarter look better than it is.","Let me think about it for an hour. I'll get back to you before the close.","If I do this for you, I want a guarantee on my commission payout plus a written note that you authorized the timing."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The unethical-behavior and short-term-myopia flaws of contingent goals: an imposed, short-term, measurable goal narrows focus and clears the way for shortcuts, whereas behavior driven by the activity itself - doing one's best, delighting the customer - leaves no low road because the disadvantaged party would be oneself.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 refuses the shortcut and makes the customer and the truth the destination: the intrinsic route has no low road, and it protects both Priya and the company from the myopia that produced scandals like Enron. Choice 0 capitulates to the quickest route and would corrupt the books for a bonus cliff. Choice 3 is passive - the ambiguity leaves Tom free to book the revenue himself, and Choice 4 merely asks for better terms on the same unethical act, confirming that contingent rewards can convert people into willing participants in short-term gaming."}]},{"id":"ch07","title":"CHAPTER 2A - . . . and the Special Circumstances When They Do","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Routine Tasks: When 'If-Then' Rewards Work","visual_frame_description":"In a fluorescent-lit office, a worker rolls posters into mailing tubes, caps them, and applies labels in four mechanical steps, while a colleague stares at an empty design screen. Glucksberg's second candle problem is sketched on a whiteboard: tacks emptied from the box, the solution visible. The player sorts six workplace tasks into their shared category.","player_mission":"Determine which single category all six workplace examples share - the kind of task where 'if-then' rewards give a safe motivational boost. Classify each row accordingly and confirm the pattern.","key_concepts":["Glucksberg's second candle-problem version emptied the tacks onto the desk, revealing the empty box and the solution.","With the solution revealed, the participants vying for the reward solved the problem faster than their counterparts.","The first question to ask before using external motivators: is the task at hand routine?","A routine task requires following a prescribed set of rules to a specified end.","Deci, Ryan, and Koestner: 'Rewards do not undermine people's intrinsic motivation for dull tasks because there is little or no intrinsic motivation to be undermined.'","Ariely and his colleagues found that when the task involved only mechanical skill, bonuses worked as expected - the higher the pay, the better the performance.","When the task called for even rudimentary cognitive skill, a larger reward led to poorer performance.","For routine tasks, rewards can provide a small motivational booster shot without the harmful side effects.","Much daily work - TPS reports, boring email, drudge work - still is not all that interesting.","In these situations it is best to try to turn work into play by increasing variety, making it more game-like, or using it to master other skills."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the ruthe-trine task boundary: conditional rewards safely boost mechanical, rule-following tasks because there is little intrinsic motivation to undermine, as Glucksberg's revealed-solution version demonstrated. The caution is that even rudimentary cognitive skill reverses the effect, with larger rewards producing poorer performance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"All of the rows below describe the same kind of work - the kind where 'if-then' rewards can safely boost performance. Which kind is it?","column_a_label":"Routine tasks - prescribed steps to a specified end, with little creative demand","column_b_label":"Heuristic, creative tasks - require invention, breakthrough thinking, and conceptual insight","column_c_label":"Tasks requiring even rudimentary cognitive skill, where larger rewards improve performance","rows":[{"label":"Rolling posters into mailing tubes, capping them, and applying labels and postage","col":"a"},{"label":"Filling out standard TPS reports","col":"a"},{"label":"Answering routine, boring email","col":"a"},{"label":"A four-step assembly job where none of the steps is notably interesting","col":"a"},{"label":"All manner of drudge work that doesn't necessarily fire the soul","col":"a"},{"label":"Work where following the prescribed rules takes you to a specified end","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Conceiving an inspiring poster that captivates imaginations and draws a crowd","Designing an original marketing campaign from a blank page","Tasks where rewards undermined performance because the box hid the solution"],"explanation":"Each row names a rule-following task with a clear path and little intrinsic appeal - the profile where contingent rewards act as a safe booster shot. The distractors are creative, heuristic undertakings, where the same rewards narrow focus and backfire, and one decoy misstates the cognitive-skill finding: even rudimentary cognitive skill made larger rewards harmful."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"'Now That' Rewards for Creative Work","visual_frame_description":"In the design studio of a small nonprofit, Renee and Sam stand beside a finished event poster while their manager Dani holds two cards: one reads 'If you top last year's attendance you'll get a ten-percent bonus,' the other reads 'Now that the poster turned out so well, lunch is on me.' The player must classify each reward description.","player_mission":"Sort the six reward descriptions into the correct column, identifying the single reward style all rows represent. Lock in the classification and explain why this style protects creativity.","key_concepts":["For creative, right-brain, heuristic tasks, offering an 'if-then' reward is a recipe for reduced performance.","The essential requirement: any extrinsic reward should be unexpected and offered only after the task is complete.","Where 'if-then' rewards are a mistake, shift to 'now that' rewards - 'Now that you've finished the poster and it turned out so well, I'd like to celebrate by taking you out to lunch.'","Deci and colleagues: tangible rewards given unexpectedly after a task are less likely to be experienced as the reason for doing the task, and less likely to be detrimental to intrinsic motivation.","Amabile found in some studies that the highest levels of creativity were produced by subjects who received a reward as a kind of a bonus.","A cash bonus handed to the design team after a great poster, with nothing promised beforehand, does not snuff their creativity.","Holding out a prize at the beginning of a project - offered as a contingency - focuses attention on obtaining the reward rather than attacking the problem.","Repeated 'now that' bonuses can quickly become expected 'if-then' entitlements, which can ultimately crater effective performance.","Baseline rewards - wages and benefits - must be adequate and fair before any of this works."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the 'now that' reward strategy for nonroutine work: rewards delivered unexpectedly after completion are not experienced as the reason for the task and so preserve intrinsic motivation and creativity. Amabile found the highest creativity among subjects who received such a bonus after the fact.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"All of the rows below are examples of the same style of reward - the one recommended for creative work. Which style is it?","column_a_label":"A 'now that' reward - unexpected, offered only after the task is complete","column_b_label":"An 'if-then' reward - promised in advance, contingent on performance","column_c_label":"Baseline compensation - regular wages and benefits","rows":[{"label":"'Now that you've finished the poster and it turned out so well, I'd like to celebrate by taking you out to lunch'","col":"a"},{"label":"Buying the design team a case of beer after the poster is done, with nothing promised beforehand","col":"a"},{"label":"A cash bonus handed over after the project, when no reward was ever announced","col":"a"},{"label":"Rewards Amabile found produced the highest levels of creativity","col":"a"},{"label":"Rewards Deci says are less likely to be experienced as the reason for doing the task","col":"a"},{"label":"A gesture that simply offers appreciation for stellar work","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Announcing a ten-percent bonus if the poster 'rocks my world' before the team starts","Promising a reward if attendance tops last year's event","Crafting a poster because the assignment said exactly how to do it"],"explanation":"Every row describes a reward that appears only after completion, with no prior promise - the defining feature of 'now that' rewards, which Deci shows are not experienced as the reason for doing the task. The distractors are contingent, announced-in-advance deals that focus attention on the prize and squash heuristic thinking."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Weekend Poster Mailing (Routine Task Battle)","visual_frame_description":"At Community Arts Bridge, a small nonprofit, twenty thousand freshly printed posters sit in cardboard stacks while manager Dani stares at her calendar - the mailing must go out this weekend. Her ten staff members lounge by the coffee machine, none eager to spend Saturday rolling posters into tubes. Four recommendation cards lie on her desk.","player_mission":"Diagnose whether the mailing task is routine, then choose how to enlist the staff for the weekend blitz. Select the plan that combines safe contingent rewards with the three practices that make them work.","key_concepts":["The first question when contemplating external motivators: is the task at hand routine?","This mailing task is the very definition of routine: roll posters, insert into tubes, cap them, apply mailing label and postage - four steps, none notable interesting.","For routine tasks, rewards can provide a small motivational booster shot without the harmful side effects.","Coercing people into mind-numbing work may get compliance but damages morale and long-term commitment.","Tangible contingent rewards can undermine intrinsic motivation and creativity, but those drawbacks matter less when the assignment inspires no deep passion and requires no deep thinking.","Offer a rationale for why the task is necessary - a boring job becomes more meaningful when part of a larger purpose.","Acknowledge that the task is boring; the acknowledgment is an act of empathy.","Allow people to complete the task their own way - think autonomy, not control - stating the outcome you need without specifying each step.","Piecework payment for every poster inserted, enclosed, and sent is a legitimate option for boosting productivity.","A big office-wide party or gift certificates for participants are acceptable incentives for routine work."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle applies the routine-task rule: when an assignment is rule-following and uninspiring, contingent rewards are safe because little intrinsic motivation exists to undermine. The chapter's poster-mailing case shows the rewards work best when paired with a rationale, an honest acknowledgment of boredom, and autonomy over method.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the routine-task decision rule from Glucksberg's second candle problem: when the path is clear and the task is mechanical, an if-then reward acts as a booster rather than a poison. The chapter's own example is this weekend poster mailing with its four mechanical steps.","stage_1_observation":"Observe the mailing room: rolls of posters, tubes, caps, label rolls, and a postage scale laid out along a folding table. One staff member rolls a poster and slips it into a tube to demonstrate; the team watches without volunteering. On your desk, the chapter's question echoes: does this task require following a prescribed set of rules to a specified end?","stage_1_question":"Is the poster mailing a routine task, and what follows from that classification? Options: A) It is routine, so rewards can safely boost participation with few side effects. B) It is creative, so any reward will crush performance. C) It is routine, but all rewards remain dangerous regardless. D) It is neither routine nor creative, so motivation tools are irrelevant.","stage_1_correct_answer":"A) It is routine, so rewards can safely boost participation with few side effects. The task is four prescribed steps to a specified end - rolling, inserting, capping, and labeling - with almost no intrinsic motivation to undermine, so contingent rewards act as a booster shot without the harmful side effects seen in creative work.","stage_2_intervention":"You must decide how to enlist the ten staff members and a few volunteers for the weekend: Option A) Order everyone to come in, with disciplinary notes for refusals. B) Ask for volunteers and hope they materialize. C) Offer 'if-then' rewards - gift certificates or piecework pay per poster - plus a rationale tied to the mission, an honest acknowledgment that the task is boring, and freedom over how each person packs posters. D) Offer the same rewards but specify exactly how each poster must be rolled and each label affixed.","stage_2_correct_answer":"C) Offer 'if-then' rewards - gift certificates or piecework pay per poster - plus a rationale tied to the mission, an honest acknowledgment that the task is boring, and freedom over how each person packs posters. Coercion damages morale and long-term commitment, volunteers will not materialize, and rewards without the rationale-empathy-autonomy package forfeit the engagement gains - while micromanaging the process (option D) replaces autonomy with control.","stage_1_options":"A) It is routine, so rewards can safely boost participation with few side effects. B) It is creative, so any reward will crush performance. C) It is routine, but all rewards remain dangerous regardless. D) It is neither routine nor creative, so motivation tools are irrelevant."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Inspiring Poster (Creative Task Battle)","visual_frame_description":"Nine months later at Community Arts Bridge, the mailing was flawless and the last event was a smash. Now Dani stands outside the design studio where Renee's team must create an inspiring poster for the next event. A speech bubble shows the tempting line: 'If you come up with a poster that rocks my world, you'll get a ten-percent bonus.'","player_mission":"Diagnose why an 'if-then' offer would wreck the poster project, then guide Dani through the aftermath when the team delivers a stunning design anyway. Choose the motivational response that preserves creativity going forward.","key_concepts":["Creating a poster is not routine; it requires conceptual, breakthrough, artistic thinking.","'If-then' rewards are an ideal way to squash conceptual, breakthrough, artistic thinking.","Do not announce: 'If you come up with a poster that rocks my world, then you'll get a ten-percent bonus.'","The best approach is to have already established a genuinely motivating environment: adequate baseline rewards, a congenial place to work, autonomy, mastery opportunity, and daily duties tied to a larger purpose.","With those elements in place, provide a sense of urgency and significance - and then get out of the talent's way.","Any extrinsic reward should be unexpected and offered only after the task is complete.","Amabile found the highest levels of creativity among subjects who received a reward as a kind of a bonus.","Nontangible rewards such as praise and positive feedback are much less corrosive than cash and trophies.","Deci found positive feedback can have an enhancing effect on intrinsic motivation.","Informational or enabling motivators can be conducive to creativity, while controlling motivators clobber it."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the creative-task rule: a contingent if-then reward on heuristic work narrows focus and squashes breakthrough thinking, so the manager must rely on baseline, autonomy, mastery, and purpose - then stay out of the way. When the work excels, unexpected 'now that' recognition with specific feedback preserves creativity.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is that creative, right-brain work must be managed without contingent rewards: the conditions of genuine motivation - baseline fairness, autonomy, mastery, and purpose - do the motivating, and any reward after the fact should be unexpected. A ten-percent 'if-then' bonus on a poster is a proven recipe for reduced performance.","stage_1_observation":"Observe the design studio scene: Renee's team is energized, mood boards cover the wall, and attendance targets from last year circulate in an email. Dani holds her tongue over a draft email offering a ten-percent bonus if the poster 'rocks my world or boosts attendance over last year.' The screen reminds you that the baseline pay is fair, the office is congenial, and the team already has autonomy and a clear mission.","stage_1_question":"How should Dani motivate the design work for the new poster? Options: A) Offer a ten-percent bonus if attendance tops last year - incentives sharpen artistic focus. B) Rely on the already-established conditions - fair baseline, autonomy, mastery, and purpose - provide urgency and significance, then get out of the talent's way. C) Assign the team a strict brief and deadline with step-by-step approval gates. D) Delay announcing the bonus until two days before the deadline to 'keep them hungry.'","stage_1_correct_answer":"B) Rely on the already-established conditions - fair baseline, autonomy, mastery, and purpose - provide urgency and significance, then get out of the talent's way. A ten-percent if-then offer is an ideal way to squash conceptual, breakthrough thinking, while strict gates remove the autonomy that creative work requires; the chapter prescribes establishing the environment and then stepping back.","stage_2_intervention":"The team finishes an absolutely stunning poster - bold, original, and on time. Renee and Sam look exhausted but proud, and Dani's phone buzzes with three possible responses: A) Tell the team 'This poster was perfect. You did it exactly the way I asked.' B) Promise each of them a cash bonus before they start the save-the-date series, to keep the momentum going. C) Say nothing and walk away, because all rewards are dangerous. D) Offer an unexpected celebration - lunch or a cash bonus now that the work is done - and add specific, informational praise about the color and layout choices.","stage_2_correct_answer":"D) Offer an unexpected celebration - lunch or a cash bonus now that the work is done - and add specific, informational praise about the color and layout choices. An unexpected, post-hoc 'now that' reward is not experienced as the reason for working, and specific praise focused on effort and strategy enhances intrinsic motivation rather than manipulating it. Outcome-only controlling praise, promised future bonuses, and silence all forfeit the chance to reinforce genuine motivation.","stage_1_options":"A) Offer a ten-percent bonus if attendance tops last year - incentives sharpen artistic focus. B) Rely on the already-established conditions - fair baseline, autonomy, mastery, and purpose - provide urgency and significance, then get out of the talent's way. C) Assign the team a strict brief and deadline with step-by-step approval gates. D) Delay announcing the bonus until two days before the deadline to 'keep them hungry.'"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Praise Conversation (Specific Feedback)","visual_frame_description":"Late Friday afternoon in the Community Arts Bridge design studio, the finished poster glows on the wall-screen. Renee, the lead designer, slumps back in her chair while Sam, the junior designer, nervously adjusts the type size one last time. Dani stands in the doorway holding two coffee cups, deciding exactly what to say.","player_mission":"Play Dani and choose how to praise the design team after the spectacular poster. Select the response that delivers specific, informational feedback and a 'now that' celebration without contingencies, preserving the team's intrinsic motivation for the next project.","key_concepts":["Praise and positive feedback are much less corrosive than cash and trophies.","Deci found in his original experiments and subsequent analysis that positive feedback can have an enhancing effect on intrinsic motivation.","Amabile found that while controlling extrinsic motivators can clobber creativity, informational or enabling motivators can be conducive to it.","People are thirsting to learn how they are doing, but only if the information isn't a tacit effort to manipulate their behavior.","Do not tell the design team: 'That poster was perfect. You did it exactly the way I asked.'","Feedback that focuses on specifics - like 'great use of color' - is more effective than generic praise.","Praise is more effective when it is about effort and strategy rather than about achieving a particular outcome.","A 'now that' reward - unexpected and post-completion - is less risky than an 'if-then' promise.","Repeated 'now that' bonuses can quickly become expected 'if-then' entitlements.","For creative, right-brain, heuristic tasks, you are best off if your 'now that' rewards provide praise, feedback, and useful information."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests informational praise: specific feedback about effort and strategy - combined with an unexpected 'now that' celebration - enhances intrinsic motivation, while outcome-focused or manipulative praise reads as control. Amabile found informational motivators conducive to creativity; the wrong words convert it back into a transaction.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"The poster is a triumph, and Dani has exactly one conversation to set the tone for the save-the-date series. Renee cares most about the color direction she fought for; Sam is insecure about his header redesign. If Dani's praise is controlling or outcome-focused, the team will read it as manipulation; if it is specific and informational, it deepens engagement. The stakes include the quality of every future project the team produces.","dialogue":["Dani: Renee, Sam - that poster is spectacular. The board will have to build a bigger venue.","Renee: Thanks, Dani. Honestly, the palette direction almost killed us - we scrapped everything Thursday night.","Sam: I rebuilt the header layout five times. I still wonder whether the headline type needs to be bigger.","Dani: I want you to know how I'm going to talk about this project with the board.","Renee: (leaning forward) What will you tell them?","Sam: And... is the save-the-date series next? I have ideas about the type.","Dani: (looking at the poster, then at the two of them) Let me tell you what I actually think."],"choices":["If you two crank out a series that lifts attendance twenty percent over last year, I'll add ten percent to your bonuses.","This poster was perfect - you did exactly what I asked, right down to the letter. That's exactly what I wanted.","That palette shift near the headline is what makes it sing - the risk you took Thursday was the right one. Sam, the header rework gives the whole piece its energy, and the type reads bold without shouting. Tell me what you'd each tackle differently on the save-the-dates. And lunch is on me tomorrow - no catch, just thanks.","You'll both get a bonus - just make sure the next series is even better so we don't have to defend it."],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Informational praise and the 'now that' reward: specific feedback about effort and strategy - not outcomes or compliance - is experienced as useful information rather than manipulation, enhancing intrinsic motivation, while unexpected post-completion recognition avoids the corrosive contingency of 'if-then' deals.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 2 works because it names exactly what succeeded - the palette risk and the header rework - treats the praise as information about craft, invites the team's own thinking about next steps, and attaches a celebration only after the work, matching Amabile's finding that specific feedback and enabling motivators are conducive to creativity. Choice 0 imposes an if-then bonus that squashes the heuristic thinking needed for the series. Choice 1 is the controlling praise the chapter explicitly warns against - 'perfect, exactly as I asked' reduces the work to compliance. Choice 3 converts appreciation into an expectation of escalation, the first step toward 'now that' bonuses drifting into 'if-then' entitlements."}]},{"id":"ch08","title":"CHAPTER 3 - Type I and Type X","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Type I, Type X, and McGregor's Theory Y","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen training room in a Rochester office tower. Mentor Priya stands beside three glowing sorting bins labeled Type I, Type X, and Theory Y, while laminated statement cards float toward the screen. In the corner, a framed photo shows the blocky Meliora Hall building at the University of Rochester where Deci and Ryan worked together.","player_mission":"Sort all seven statement cards into the correct bin by classifying each claim as Type I behavior, Type X behavior, or a Theory Y assumption. Draw on the chapter's definitions: intrinsic versus extrinsic fuel and McGregor's humanistic view of employees.","question":"Priya holds up the card that reads: 'Taking an interest in work is as natural as play or rest, and under proper conditions people will accept, and even seek, responsibility.' Which bin does this statement belong to?","column_a_label":"Type I behavior","column_b_label":"Type X behavior","column_c_label":"Theory Y assumption","rows":[{"label":"The main motivator is the freedom, challenge, and purpose of the undertaking itself; any other gains are welcome, but mainly as a bonus.","col":"a"},{"label":"An intense focus on external rewards can deliver fast results, but the approach is difficult to sustain and does not assist in mastery.","col":"b"},{"label":"Taking an interest in work is as natural as play or rest.","col":"c"},{"label":"At its center is the second drive; it is fueled more by extrinsic desires than intrinsic ones.","col":"b"},{"label":"It behaves like clean energy: inexpensive, safe to use, and endlessly renewable.","col":"a"},{"label":"Creativity and ingenuity are widely distributed in the population, so under the proper conditions people will accept and even seek responsibility.","col":"c"},{"label":"It concerns itself less with the inherent satisfaction of an activity and more with the external rewards to which that activity leads.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Type I people always turn down raises and refuse to cash paychecks.","Type X behavior is a fixed trait wired in at birth and can never change.","Self-determination theory argues rewards are never necessary in any workplace."],"explanation":"The question's claim is McGregor's Theory Y stance from The Human Side of Enterprise, so it sorts to column c. The remaining rows sort the same way: claims about inherent satisfaction, freedom, challenge, and renewable energy describe Type I behavior (centered on the third drive); claims about external rewards, unsustainable short-term wins, and the second drive describe Type X behavior; and claims about natural interest in work and widely distributed creativity describe Theory Y. The chapter adds that Type I behavior is made, not born, so any Type X can become a Type I.","key_concepts":["Type I behavior is fueled more by intrinsic desires than extrinsic ones and concerns itself with the inherent satisfaction of the activity itself.","Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires and concerns itself with the external rewards to which the activity leads.","At the center of Type X behavior is the second drive; at the center of Type I behavior is the third drive.","For Type I's, the main motivator is the freedom, challenge, and purpose of the undertaking; for Type X's, the main motivator is external rewards and any deeper satisfaction is secondary.","Type I behavior is made, not born; it emerges from circumstance, experience, and context, and any Type X can become a Type I.","Type I's almost always outperform Type X's in the long run because an intense focus on extrinsic rewards is difficult to sustain and does not assist in mastery.","Douglas McGregor's Theory Y held that interest in work is as natural as play or rest and that creativity and ingenuity are widely distributed in the population.","McGregor's Theory X held that most people dislike work, fear responsibility, crave security, and must be coerced, controlled, directed, and threatened with punishment.","Type I behavior is like the sun: inexpensive, safe to use, and endlessly renewable, unlike coal-like Type X behavior."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the Type I versus Type X distinction (intrinsic versus extrinsic fuel for behavior) against McGregor's Theory Y assumptions about employees. The book argues that reward-fueled Type X behavior is coal-like and hard to sustain, while Type I behavior is a renewable resource that outperforms in the long run, making Theory Y the more accurate and effective starting point for management.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Type A, Type B, and Type X Patterns","visual_frame_description":"A 1950s San Francisco cardiology office rendered in sepia tones. Meyer Friedman's clipboard lists traits of his heart-disease-prone patients, and three filing drawers labeled Type A, Type B, and Type X wait to receive case notes. A vintage ticking clock on the wall underscores the chapter's 'hurry sickness' imagery.","player_mission":"Classify each description into the correct drawer: the Type A behavior pattern identified by Friedman and Rosenman, the contrasting Type B pattern, or the Type X motivational pattern from Pink's framework. Use the chapter's exact trait lists and definitions.","question":"Friedman and Rosenman noticed that some patients prone to heart disease displayed 'a particular complex of personality traits, including excessive competition drive, aggressiveness, impatience, and a harrying sense of time urgency.' Which category does this complex of traits describe?","column_a_label":"Type A behavior pattern","column_b_label":"Type B behavior pattern","column_c_label":"Type X behavior","rows":[{"label":"Engaged in a chronic, ceaseless struggle with themselves, with others, and with time.","col":"a"},{"label":"Rarely harried by life or made hostile by its demands.","col":"b"},{"label":"Fueled more by extrinsic desires than intrinsic ones, with the second drive at its center.","col":"c"},{"label":"Suffers from 'hurry sickness'; horn-honking and foot-tapping are its hallmarks.","col":"a"},{"label":"Just as intelligent and frequently just as ambitious as the contrasting type, but its 'drive' steadies the person rather than goading, irritating, and infuriating.","col":"b"},{"label":"Concerned less with the inherent satisfaction of an activity and more with the external rewards to which that activity leads.","col":"c"},{"label":"Deci found that people oriented toward control and extrinsic rewards showed greater public self-consciousness, acted more defensively, and were more likely to exhibit this pattern.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Type B people are less intelligent and less ambitious than Type A's.","The Type A and Type B labels were coined by Douglas McGregor in The Human Side of Enterprise.","Type X behavior is centered on the third drive, not the second drive."],"explanation":"The question's trait complex is Friedman and Rosenman's exact description of the Type A pattern, so the answer is column a. Rows describing time urgency, 'hurry sickness,' and the chronic struggle sort to Type A; rows describing a calm, steadying ambition sort to Type B (the cardiologists found Type B's just as intelligent and often just as ambitious); and rows about external rewards and the second drive sort to Type X. The final row captures Deci's finding that control- and reward-oriented people are more likely to display the Type A pattern.","key_concepts":["In the late 1950s, San Francisco cardiologists Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman found that heart-disease-prone patients shared a trait complex of excessive competition drive, aggressiveness, impatience, and a harrying sense of time urgency.","Friedman and Rosenman dubbed this behavioral pattern Type A, in contrast to Type B.","People displaying Type B behavior were rarely harried by life or made hostile by its demands.","Type B people were just as intelligent, and frequently just as ambitious, as Type A's, but their 'drive' steadied them rather than goading, irritating, and infuriating them.","Type B's did not suffer from the 'hurry sickness' of horn-honking, foot-tapping Type A's.","Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires, is centered on the second drive, and tracks external rewards rather than inherent satisfaction.","Deci found that people oriented toward control and extrinsic rewards showed greater public self-consciousness and acted more defensively.","Deci's research linked orientation toward control and extrinsic rewards to a greater likelihood of exhibiting the Type A behavior pattern.","Type I behavior is centered on the third drive and is fueled more by intrinsic desires than extrinsic ones."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the Type A/Type B personality distinction from Friedman and Rosenman's cardiology research against Pink's Type X motivational category. The chapter links the two traditions with Deci's finding that control- and extrinsic-reward-oriented people show greater public self-consciousness, act more defensively, and are more likely to exhibit the Type A pattern.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Motivation 2.0, Motivation 3.0, and Self-Determination Theory","visual_frame_description":"A control room with three vintage operating-system terminals: one labeled Motivation 2.0 displays carrots and sticks, one labeled Motivation 3.0 glows with flowing light, and a third shows three interlocking gears labeled competence, autonomy, and relatedness. A cursor blinks, waiting for the player to route each statement to its system.","player_mission":"Route each statement to the operating system it describes: the carrot-and-stick precepts of Motivation 2.0, the intrinsic-drive upgrade of Motivation 3.0, or Deci and Ryan's self-determination theory. Match each claim precisely to the chapter's language.","question":"Deci argues that rewards 'of course, they're necessary in workplaces and other settings,' but 'the less salient they are made, the better.' Which of the three frameworks begins with a notion of universal human needs and names competence, autonomy, and relatedness as the core of motivation?","column_a_label":"Motivation 2.0","column_b_label":"Motivation 3.0","column_c_label":"Self-determination theory","rows":[{"label":"The precept that offering steady employment and carefully calibrated rewards will make people do what executives and shareholders want.","col":"a"},{"label":"The upgrade needed to meet the new realities of how we organize, think about, and do what we do.","col":"b"},{"label":"Begins with a notion of universal human needs rather than a single tendency such as keen responsiveness to reinforcements or zippy calculation of self-interest.","col":"c"},{"label":"Depends on and fosters Type I behavior.","col":"b"},{"label":"Its main mechanisms are stifling: when people aren't producing, companies run over the problem with a carrot or a stick instead of diagnosing what the problem is.","col":"a"},{"label":"Argues that when the three innate psychological needs are satisfied, we are motivated, productive, and happy, and when they are thwarted, motivation, productivity, and happiness plummet.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["Motivation 3.0 depends on and fosters Type X behavior.","Self-determination theory predicts that offering rewards always increases motivation.","The three innate needs of self-determination theory are money, fame, and beauty."],"explanation":"The question describes self-determination theory's core claim, so the answer is column c. Rows about calibrated rewards, steady employment, and running over problems with carrots and sticks describe Motivation 2.0; rows about the needed upgrade and Type I behavior describe Motivation 3.0; and rows about universal human needs and the three innate needs describe SDT. The 'money, fame, beauty' distractor is the chapter's list of Type X validations, not an SDT need.","key_concepts":["Self-determination theory begins with a notion of universal human needs rather than a single tendency like reinforcement responsiveness, self-interest calculation, or psychosexual conflict.","SDT argues that human beings have three innate psychological needs: competence, autonomy, and relatedness.","When the three needs are satisfied, people are motivated, productive, and happy; when thwarted, motivation, productivity, and happiness plummet.","Motivation 2.0 operates on the precept that steady employment and carefully calibrated rewards will make people do what executives and shareholders want.","Motivation 3.0 is the upgrade needed to meet the new realities of how we organize, think about, and do what we do, and it depends on Type I behavior.","Ryan says that when people aren't producing, companies typically resort to rewards or punishment without doing the hard work of diagnosing what the problem is.","Deci says rewards are necessary in workplaces, but the less salient they are made, the better; when people use rewards to motivate, that is when they are most demotivating.","Human beings have an innate inner drive to be autonomous, self-determined, and connected to one another, and when that drive is liberated, people achieve more and live richer lives.","On a larger scale: SDT researchers have produced hundreds of papers spanning business, education, medicine, sports, exercise, personal productivity, environmentalism, relationships, and physical and mental health."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests how the carrot-and-stick precepts of Motivation 2.0, the upgrade to Motivation 3.0, and Deci and Ryan's self-determination theory are distinct operating systems of human motivation. The chapter's core claim is that SDT's three innate needs, when satisfied, drive motivation and well-being, while Motivation 2.0's rewards and punishments, when used as the primary levers, actively undermine them.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Theory X vs Theory Y at Meridian Textiles","visual_frame_description":"A gray factory floor at Meridian Textiles in Rochester, where floor manager Dale looms over the QA line with a printed pay-deduction memo. Beside him stands senior HR partner You, holding a copy of McGregor's The Human Side of Enterprise. Workers glance up from their stations as Dale argues his case.","player_mission":"Confront Dale's managerial assumptions, identify which theory of human behavior drives his approach, and stop his escalation of punishment before it destroys the team's motivation. Your moves decide whether the line recovers or collapses.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests McGregor's Theory X versus Theory Y from The Human Side of Enterprise (1960). Theory X assumes people fundamentally dislike work, fear responsibility, crave security, and must be coerced, controlled, directed, and threatened with punishment; Theory Y assumes interest in work is as natural as play or rest, creativity is widely distributed, and people accept and even seek responsibility under proper conditions. McGregor argued that if you believe in the 'mediocrity of the masses,' mediocrity becomes the ceiling on what you can achieve.","stage_1_observation":"Dale, the floor manager, walks past Evie's workstation and tells you: 'People only work for the paycheck. If I didn't watch the clock, they'd all be gone by lunch. That's why I dock pay for late arrivals and tie bonuses to widget counts.' He hands out a memo threatening pay deductions for missed quotas and warns that anyone who complains will be moved to the night shift.","stage_1_question":"Which set of assumptions about human behavior does Dale's management style reflect?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Theory X: Dale assumes employees fundamentally dislike work, fear taking responsibility, crave security, and badly need direction, so they must be coerced, controlled, directed, and threatened with punishment to put forth adequate effort. McGregor's 'mediocrity of the masses' belief makes mediocrity the ceiling on Dale's results.","stage_2_intervention":"Two weeks later, output has dropped further and Evie has filed a grievance. Dale wants to double the quota bonuses, add a public leaderboard naming the slowest workers, and extend deductions to anyone who misses a second target. You have authority to pick the response. Which stance matches what Ryan and Deci say about motivating people?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Stop the escalation and do the diagnostic work: ask why output is really falling and create conditions where the team's innate needs for competence, autonomy, and relatedness can flourish. Ryan's point is that companies run over the problem with a carrot or a stick instead of diagnosing the problem, and Deci says rewards are most demotivating precisely when they are used to motivate, so doubling bonuses and adding shame will deepen the collapse.","key_concepts":["Douglas McGregor, an MIT management professor who had served as president of Antioch College, argued in The Human Side of Enterprise that the problem with corporate leadership was its premises about human behavior.","Theory X holds that most people dislike work, fear responsibility, crave security, and must be coerced, controlled, directed, and threatened with punishment.","Theory Y holds that taking an interest in work is as natural as play or rest and that creativity and ingenuity are widely distributed in the population.","Under proper conditions, Theory Y holds, people will accept, and even seek, responsibility.","If your starting point is Theory X, managerial techniques will produce limited results or go awry entirely.","Believing in the 'mediocrity of the masses,' as McGregor put it, makes mediocrity the ceiling on what you can achieve.","Ryan says the hard work of motivating is diagnosing what the problem actually is, rather than trying to run over it with a carrot or a stick.","Deci says rewards are necessary in workplaces, but the less salient they are made, the better, and using rewards to motivate is when they are most demotivating.","SDT suggests focusing effort on creating environments where innate psychological needs flourish."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests McGregor's Theory X/Theory Y dichotomy: whether managers assume employees must be coerced and threatened or assume interest in work is natural and creativity widespread. The chapter argues the starting premise determines the ceiling of achievement, and pairs this with Ryan and Deci's claim that running over a problem with carrots or sticks substitutes for the real work of diagnosing it.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Turning a Type X Star into a Type I","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled consulting office at Calder Associates. Analyst Marco's desk is covered with bonus spreadsheets, and a phone timer buzzes on the table while he checks his quarterly numbers. You, a performance coach, sit across from him as a review meeting plays out on a wall screen.","player_mission":"Read Marco's behavioral pattern correctly, then choose the intervention that the chapter's science says will move him from Type X toward Type I for the long run. Resist the firm's instinct to escalate the size and visibility of financial rewards.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the Type X to Type I distinction. Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires and centered on the second drive; Type I behavior is fueled by intrinsic desires and the third drive. Type I's almost always outperform Type X's in the long run because an intense focus on external rewards delivers fast results but is difficult to sustain and does not assist mastery. The patterns are proclivities, not fixed traits: any Type X can become a Type I when circumstances support the shift.","stage_1_observation":"Marco is a brilliant analyst whose only metric is his bonus: he turns down client lunches, checks his quarterly numbers hourly, and erupts defensively when a junior colleague's idea earns praise. His phone timer buzzes constantly; he calls it his 'edge.' In the review meeting on screen, the firm raises his bonus target and Marco immediately grows tense, checking his watch and interrupting colleagues.","stage_1_question":"Which motivational pattern best describes Marco's behavior?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Type X with Type A overtones: he is powered by external rewards rather than the inherent satisfaction of the work, which puts the second drive at his center, and his time urgency and defensiveness echo the traits Deci linked to control- and reward-oriented people, who show greater public self-consciousness, act more defensively, and are more likely to exhibit the Type A behavior pattern.","stage_2_intervention":"Marco's firm wants to 'fix' him by tripling his commission ceiling and posting monthly pay rankings across the floor, arguing that bigger stakes will finally activate him. Based on the chapter's reasoning, which move is most likely to shift Marco toward Type I behavior over the long run?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Stop inflating the if-then motivators, which always grow more expensive, and instead reshape the conditions: make sure his baseline pay is adequate and equitable so money leaves the table, then give him freedom, challenge, and purpose in the work itself so that inherent satisfaction, the fuel of Type I behavior, can take over and support mastery, the source of achievement over the long haul.","key_concepts":["Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires than intrinsic ones, with the second drive at its center.","Type I behavior is fueled more by intrinsic desires than extrinsic ones, with the third drive at its center.","For Type X's, the main motivator is external rewards; any deeper satisfaction is welcome but secondary.","For Type I's, the main motivator is the freedom, challenge, and purpose of the undertaking itself; other gains are mainly a bonus.","Type I's almost always outperform Type X's in the long run, although an intense focus on extrinsic rewards can deliver fast results in the short term.","An extrinsic approach is difficult to sustain and does not assist in mastery, which is the source of achievement over the long haul.","Type I behavior is made, not born; these patterns are proclivities that emerge from circumstance, experience, and context, and any Type X can become a Type I.","Deci found that people oriented toward control and extrinsic rewards showed greater public self-consciousness, acted more defensively, and were more likely to exhibit the Type A behavior pattern.","The most successful people often aren't directly pursuing conventional notions of success but persist because of an internal desire to control their lives, learn about their world, and accomplish something that endures."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the Type X versus Type I distinction and the shift between them: extrinsic fuel delivers short-term wins but undermines mastery, while intrinsic fuel drives long-run achievement. The chapter argues the patterns are made, not born, so escalating if-then rewards will not fix Marco, but reshaping conditions to support autonomy, mastery, and purpose will.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Baseline Rewards and Reward Salience at Northgate Hospital","visual_frame_description":"A bright hospital corridor at Northgate where nurse Aida stands by her locker, holding a pay stub. A whiteboard behind her shows a new bonus ladder tied to patient-satisfaction scores, and her director paces nearby with an award plaque. The scene is lit with the cool tones of a compensation review meeting.","player_mission":"Diagnose why Aida's motivation cratered, then steer the hospital's compensation response away from flashy reward escalation and toward the science of baseline rewards. Your choice determines whether Aida returns to the trauma team she loved or burns out resentful.","boss_battle_explanation":"This battle tests the baseline rewards principle and the salience of money. Both Type X's and Type I's care about money: if compensation doesn't hit the baseline, adequate and equitable pay described in Chapter 2, motivation craters regardless of orientation. Once baseline is met, money should be made less salient so it takes the issue of money off the table rather than becoming the table itself, and recognition should function as feedback, not as a goal in itself.","stage_1_observation":"Aida, a stellar nurse, learns she is paid $9,000 less than the two nurses hired after her with equal tenure. Her unit's new 'performance bonus' is tied to patient-satisfaction scores, and the director loudly celebrates each payout. Since learning this, Aida has stopped volunteering for the trauma team she loved and does only the minimum on her shift chart.","stage_1_question":"According to the chapter's account of baseline rewards, what does the science predict for Aida's motivation?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Her motivation will crater regardless of whether she leans Type X or Type I, because a compensation package that is inadequate or inequitable relative to others doing similar work destroys motivation before questions of intrinsic versus extrinsic orientation even arise; in that state you get very little motivation at all.","stage_2_intervention":"Aida's director proposes tripling the patient-satisfaction bonus, hosting a large 'Pay for Performance' ceremony, and printing Aida's bonus rank on a lobby screen. Which response follows Deci and Ryan's reasoning about how to handle money and rewards?","stage_2_correct_answer":"First correct the baseline: pay Aida adequately and equitably relative to her peers, because until that is fixed no reward scheme can motivate her. Then keep money and recognition low-salience: once compensation is fair, the best use of money is to take the issue of money off the table so she can focus on the work itself, and recognition should act as feedback for Type I's rather than as a goal in itself or a public ranking.","key_concepts":["Both Type X's and Type I's care about money.","If an employee's compensation doesn't hit the baseline, or her pay isn't equitable compared to others doing similar work, her motivation will crater regardless of whether she leans X or I.","Once compensation meets the baseline, money plays a different role for Type I's than for Type X's.","One reason fair and adequate pay is essential is that it takes the issue of money off the table so Type I's can focus on the work itself.","For many Type X's, money is the table; it is why they do what they do.","Type I's like being recognized for their accomplishments because recognition is a form of feedback, not a goal in itself.","Deci says rewards are necessary in workplaces, but the less salient they are made, the better.","Using rewards to motivate is when they are most demotivating.","Type I behavior depends on three nutrients: autonomy, mastery, and purpose."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the baseline rewards principle from Chapter 2 applied to the Type I/Type X framework: inadequate or inequitable pay craters motivation for anyone, but once the baseline is met, money and recognition should be made less salient. The chapter argues that for Type I's, fair pay clears money off the table so freedom, challenge, and purpose can drive the work.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Diagnose Before You Dangle a Carrot","visual_frame_description":"A corner café in Rochester where Priya, a product manager, and Lena, a consultant familiar with Deci and Ryan's research, sit over cooling cups of coffee. Priya's phone shows a sprint burndown chart in red, and a notepad between them lists the QA team's recent misses.","player_mission":"Play Priya in a live conversation with Lena about your QA team's three losing sprints. Choose what you say next in the exchange, aiming for the response that matches how self-determination researchers say motivation actually works.","scenario_setup":"Priya's QA team has missed targets for three straight sprints. Her instinct is to offer a $500-per-sprint bonus for hitting targets and threaten written warnings for misses. Lena, a consultant grounded in Deci and Ryan's self-determination research, pushes back before Priya commits. The stakes: the release date slips if the team stays unmotivated, and Priya wants a fix that actually lasts.","dialogue":["Priya: We are three sprints behind. I am going to dangle a per-sprint bonus and issue a warning to anybody who misses the target again.","Lena: Hold on. What have you actually diagnosed about why the output is slipping?","Priya: Nobody has told me about a problem. But carrots and sticks are what managers do, right?","Lena: Deci and Ryan would call that running over the problem with a carrot or a stick. And rewards are most demotivating exactly when you use them to motivate.","Priya: So I should pay nothing and just hope the team feels inspired?","Lena: No. Rewards are necessary in workplaces; the less salient you make them, the better. Start by finding the actual problem. Maybe the scope changed mid-sprint, or the team lost an engineer who held the context together.","Priya: So the first move is conversation, not compensation surgery?","Lena: Diagnose first. Then build an environment where competence, autonomy, and relatedness can flourish, and the motivation follows."],"choices":["Fair enough. Before any bonus talk, I will sit with each tester to hear what is blocking them, fix the real blockers, and keep any reward quiet and secondary.","Lena is too theoretical. I will still add the $500 sprint bonus but hide it in a neutral line item so it is not salient.","I will double the bonus to $1,000 and send a weekly email ranking each tester's delivered counts to light the fire.","I will keep the written warnings but drop the bonus, so at least we stay credible."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Ryan's diagnosis-before-reward principle from self-determination theory: when people aren't producing, the real work is diagnosing the problem instead of running over it with a carrot or a stick, paired with Deci's claim that rewards are most demotivating when used to motivate and are best kept low-salience.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it diagnoses the underlying problem first, addresses the team's needs for competence and autonomy, and keeps rewards non-salient, matching Ryan's and Deci's guidance exactly. Choice 1 fails because a concealed incentive still operates as an if-then contingency that grows more expensive and manipulates without diagnosis. Choice 2 fails because escalating rewards and public rankings make rewards salient, feeding extrinsic orientation and the very demotivation Deci warns about. Choice 3 fails because it keeps the stick side of Motivation 2.0, threatening punishment without ever diagnosing what is actually wrong.","key_concepts":["Ryan says that when people aren't producing, companies typically resort to rewards or punishment, but the hard work is diagnosing what the problem actually is.","Ryan describes the failure mode as trying to run over the problem with a carrot or a stick.","Deci says rewards are necessary in workplaces and other settings, but the less salient they are made, the better.","When people use rewards to motivate, that is when they are most demotivating.","SDT argues that when the three innate psychological needs of competence, autonomy, and relatedness are satisfied, we are motivated, productive, and happy.","The main mechanisms of Motivation 2.0 are more stifling than supportive.","Deci and Ryan say the focus should be on creating environments for innate psychological needs to flourish.","Self-determination theory begins with a notion of universal human needs rather than a single tendency like responsiveness to reinforcements.","The third drive is part of what it means to be human, but whether it emerges depends on whether the conditions around us support it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests Ryan's diagnosis-before-reward principle: managers who reach for carrots and sticks skip the hard work of diagnosing the problem, and rewards become most demotivating precisely when they are used to motivate. The chapter's remedy is to keep rewards low-salience and build environments where competence, autonomy, and relatedness can flourish.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Money Off the Table: Baseline Pay First","visual_frame_description":"A loft office of a small nonprofit tech shop where co-founders Rafael and Julia face each other across a whiteboard covered in budget lines. Developer Omar's résumé sits between them, and a calculator and two competing offer letters share the table, one from a bank flashing a higher salary.","player_mission":"Play Julia in a live negotiation with co-founder Rafael over how to pay and motivate developer Omar. Choose the argument that follows the chapter's account of baseline rewards and the role of money for Type I's versus Type X's.","scenario_setup":"Rafael and Julia run a small nonprofit tech shop and must decide how to use their limited budget to keep developer Omar, who has a competing bank offer paying $18,000 more. Rafael wants to put the money into performance bonuses; Julia argues salaries come first. The stakes: hire Omar and keep the team's spirit, or lose him and set back the mission.","dialogue":["Rafael: Bonuses are better than salaries. They point people at what matters.","Julia: Only if compensation clears the baseline first. Both Type I's and Type X's care about money. If Omar's pay is low or unfair next to the bank offer, his motivation craters no matter what type he is.","Rafael: So you would hand him a fat salary and just hope he loves the work?","Julia: The opposite of hope. Once pay is adequate and equitable, the best use of money is to take the issue of money off the table.","Rafael: Then how do we recognize great work?","Julia: Type I's like recognition; it is feedback, not a goal in itself. A quiet, well-timed acknowledgment beats a public bonus ranking any day.","Rafael: You are saying the bonus structure I designed could backfire?","Julia: The less salient rewards are made, the better. If Omar's pay is fair, he will focus on the freedom, challenge, and purpose here. For a Type X, money is the table. For a Type I, money clears the table."],"choices":["Agree with Julia: close Omar's pay gap to the bank offer, keep any bonuses small and quiet, and let the work itself drive him.","Split the difference: give Omar a modest raise, then run a flashy Engineer of the Month bonus program to keep excitement up.","Fund Rafael's bonuses and tell Omar the uncapped upside means unlimited income potential.","Promise Omar the bank-level salary, but only after a six-month probation period of performance bonuses."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The baseline rewards principle: adequate and equitable compensation is the precondition for motivation of any kind, and once the baseline is met, money works best when made less salient so it takes the issue of money off the table, letting the work itself, freedom, challenge, and purpose, drive behavior.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it fixes the baseline first, removing compensation inequity so Omar's motivation doesn't crater, and keeps rewards low-salience so intrinsic motivation can operate. Choice 1 fails because a public monthly bonus program makes rewards highly salient and turns recognition into a goal in itself, which feeds the extrinsic orientation the chapter warns about. Choice 2 fails because it makes money 'the table' for Omar, converting the job into an if-then pursuit of uncapped pay that grows more expensive and never satisfies the third drive. Choice 3 fails because payment contingent on a probation period is precisely the kind of if-then contingency that undermines intrinsic motivation and delays the baseline Omar needs now.","key_concepts":["Both Type X's and Type I's care about money, and if compensation doesn't hit the baseline, motivation craters regardless of orientation.","The baseline consists of adequate pay and pay that is equitable compared to others doing similar work.","Once compensation meets the baseline, money plays a different role for Type I's than for Type X's.","A reason fair and adequate pay is so essential is that it takes the issue of money off the table so people can focus on the work itself.","For many Type X's, money is the table; it is why they do what they do.","Type I's like being recognized for their accomplishments because recognition is a form of feedback, not a goal in itself.","Deci says rewards are necessary, but the less salient they are made, the better.","Using rewards to motivate is when they are most demotivating.","If-then motivators always grow more expensive, as Chapter 2 enumerated."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the baseline rewards principle and the differing role of money for Type I's versus Type X's: inadequate or inequitable pay craters motivation for everyone, but once the baseline is met, money should be made less salient so it clears the table rather than becoming it. The chapter argues this lets freedom, challenge, and purpose drive performance, and treats recognition as feedback rather than a goal.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}}]},{"id":"ch09","title":"CHAPTER 4 - Autonomy","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Four T's: Autonomy over Task, Technique, and Team","visual_frame_description":"A sorting dashboard shows three glowing columns labeled Task, Technique, and Team. Practice cards float down a conveyor belt, each stamped with a workplace scenario such as an Atlassian FedEx Day or a Zappos customer service call. A score meter at the top tracks how many cards the player has binned correctly.","player_mission":"Sort each workplace practice card into the facet of autonomy it grants: task, technique, or team. Resolve the featured case first, Atlassian's FedEx Days, because landing it in the correct column unlocks the rest of the round.","key_concepts":["Type I behavior emerges when people have autonomy over the four T's: their task, their time, their technique, and their team.","Atlassian's FedEx Days are twenty-four-hour bursts held once a quarter when engineers work on any software problem they want and deliver it overnight to the whole company.","At 3M, William McKnight's 15 percent time policy, known inside the company as the bootlegging policy, produced innovations including Art Fry's Post-it notes.","Zappos does not monitor customer service employees' call times and does not require them to use scripts; reps handle calls the way they want.","At Whole Foods, a department's employees vote on whether to hire a job candidate full-time after the candidate works a thirty-day trial period on the team.","At W. L. Gore and Associates, anybody who wants to rise in the ranks and lead a team must assemble people willing to work with her.","At Georgetown University Hospital in Washington, D.C., many nurses have the freedom to conduct their own research projects, which have changed hospital programs and policies.","JetBlue routes customer service calls to employees' homes, removing them from physical monitoring and giving them autonomy over how they do their jobs.","Mike Cannon-Brookes refused to track engineers' time during 20 percent time because tracking was too controlling; he wanted to back his engineers and take it on faith that they would do good things.","Autonomy over team is the least developed of the four T's, but open-source projects show how self-assembling teams can reach beyond a single organization."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the four T's of autonomy, task, time, technique, and team, which Pink identifies as the essential facets of the Motivation 3.0 approach to work. The book grounds each facet in named cases, from Atlassian's FedEx Days and 3M's 15 percent time to Zappos's technique freedom and Whole Foods' team hiring votes.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each practice card into the column for the kind of autonomy it grants. Featured case: Atlassian's FedEx Days, where engineers spend a fixed burst of time on any software problem they choose, even if it is not part of their regular job. Which column does this practice belong in?","column_a_label":"Autonomy over task","column_b_label":"Autonomy over technique","column_c_label":"Autonomy over team","rows":[{"label":"Atlassian engineers spend a quarterly twenty-four-hour FedEx Day on any software problem they want","col":"a"},{"label":"At 3M, Art Fry used his 15 percent bootlegging time to invent Post-it notes","col":"a"},{"label":"Zappos customer service reps handle calls without scripts and without call-time monitoring","col":"b"},{"label":"Whole Foods department members vote on a job candidate after a thirty-day team trial","col":"c"},{"label":"Georgetown University Hospital nurses run their own research projects","col":"a"},{"label":"W. L. Gore leaders must assemble people willing to work with them","col":"c"},{"label":"JetBlue routes calls to service reps at home, free from physical monitoring","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["FedEx Days are a pay-for-performance plan where engineers earn bonuses for each delivered feature","3M officially banned the bootlegging policy, and employees practiced it in secret","Zappos requires reps to end every call by quoting a scripted sales line"],"explanation":"FedEx Days and 3M's 15 percent time let people choose what they build, so they grant autonomy over task, column a. Zappos and JetBlue free how the work is performed, which is technique, column b. Whole Foods voting and W. L. Gore leader assembly give employees a say over who they work with, which is team, column c. Time, the fourth T, is served by results-only work environments rather than by these practices."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Controlled vs Autonomous Motivation: The SDT Distinction","visual_frame_description":"Two silhouettes face each other across a split screen: one glows warm and moves freely between choices, the other is gray and chained to a conveyor of demands. A third figure stands apart on a lonely hill with a cowboy hat, labeled Independence. Cards listing behaviors slide beneath each figure for the player to assign.","player_mission":"Classify each behavior card as autonomous motivation, controlled motivation, or independence as Deci and Ryan define them. The featured card shows a law associate billing in six-minute increments; classify it first to unlock the remaining cards.","key_concepts":["Deci and Ryan cite autonomy as one of three basic human needs and call it the most important, the sun around which self-determination theory's planets orbit.","Deci and Ryan moved from categorizing behavior as intrinsically or extrinsically motivated to categorizing it as controlled or autonomous.","Autonomous motivation involves behaving with a full sense of volition and choice.","Controlled motivation involves behaving with the experience of pressure and demand toward specific outcomes that comes from forces perceived to be external to the self.","Autonomy is different from independence: it is not the rugged go-it-alone individualism of the American cowboy.","A person can be both autonomous and happily interdependent with others.","Autonomy appears to be a human concept rather than a Western one, with benefits found in Russia, Turkey, South Korea, and high-poverty Bangladesh.","A 2004 study by Deci, Ryan, and Paul Baard of investment bank workers found greater job satisfaction among employees whose bosses offered autonomy support.","Autonomy support means bosses see issues from the employee's point of view, give meaningful feedback and information, provide ample choice over what to do and how to do it, and encourage new projects.","Researchers found that people oriented toward autonomy and intrinsic motivation have higher self-esteem and better interpersonal relationships than those who are extrinsically motivated."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the distinction at the heart of self-determination theory: autonomous motivation, acting with full volition and choice, versus controlled motivation, acting under pressure toward externally perceived outcomes. Pink presents this shift in Deci and Ryan's taxonomy plus evidence that autonomy support raises job satisfaction and performance at an American investment bank.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each behavior into its SDT category. Featured case: a new associate at a large law firm must bill more than two thousand hours a year, logged in six-minute increments, or her job is at risk. Which column does her behavior belong in?","column_a_label":"Autonomous motivation (SDT)","column_b_label":"Controlled motivation (SDT)","column_c_label":"Independence (not autonomy)","rows":[{"label":"A software developer spends 20 percent time on a project she chooses herself","col":"a"},{"label":"A law associate logs every six-minute increment because her job depends on billable hours","col":"b"},{"label":"A do-it-yourself homesteader who relies on no one and answers to nobody","col":"c"},{"label":"An investment bank employee whose boss sees issues from her point of view and offers meaningful feedback","col":"a"},{"label":"Behaving with a full sense of volition and choice, in Deci and Ryan's definition","col":"a"},{"label":"A call center rep reads a required script under a manager listening in to time each call","col":"b"},{"label":"Acting with choice while remaining happily interdependent with colleagues","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Controlled motivation means behaving with a full sense of volition and choice","Deci and Ryan rank independence as the most important of the three basic needs","Autonomy support means telling employees exactly how to do their jobs"],"explanation":"The billable-hour associate experiences pressure and demand toward outcomes imposed by forces external to herself, which is precisely Deci and Ryan's definition of controlled motivation. Choosing freely, receiving autonomy support, and staying interdependent while self-directed all land in the autonomous column, while go-it-alone self-reliance is independence, which SDT treats as distinct from autonomy."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Time Autonomy: ROWE vs Cosmetic Flexibility vs the Billable Hour","visual_frame_description":"A three-lane clock tower scene: lane one shows a calm office where people drift in at noon on a rainy Friday, lane two shows a gated pasture with a fence being widened, and lane three shows a stamp mill crunching numbers in six-minute blocks. The player drags evidence plaques across the lanes.","player_mission":"Assign each evidence plaque to the time model it describes: the results-only work environment, cosmetic flexibility or empowerment, or the billable hour. Classify the featured Meddius case first, since it anchors the whole matrix.","key_concepts":["A ROWE, or results-only work environment, was created by Cali Ressler and Jody Thompson, former Best Buy human resources executives.","In a ROWE, people do not have schedules; they show up when they want and must only get their work done.","Jeff Gunther turned his company Meddius into a ROWE for a ninety-day experiment, then made it permanent after productivity rose and stress declined.","Gunther calls money a threshold motivator: once a company meets the baseline, dollars and cents do not much affect performance and motivation.","Gunther refused to tie specific goals to compensation because that creates a culture that says it is all about the money and not enough about the work.","Ressler and Thompson call formal flex time programs a con game because flexibility simply widens the fences and occasionally opens the gates.","Empowerment is not autonomy; it presumes the organization holds the power and benevolently ladles some of it into the waiting bowls of grateful employees.","The billable hour forces lawyers to keep scrupulous track of time, often in six-minute increments, veering their focus from output to input.","Chief Justice William Rehnquist warned that if one is expected to bill more than two thousand hours per year, there are bound to be temptations to exaggerate the hours actually put in.","At Best Buy's corporate offices, salaried employees put in as much time as it takes, productivity rose 35 percent, and voluntary turnover fell 320 basis points versus non-ROWE teams."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the difference between genuine time autonomy, cosmetic flexibility, and time-based control. Pink argues that a ROWE flips the model from input to output, while flex time and empowerment are control in sheep's clothing, and the billable hour is an autonomy-crushing relic of Motivation 2.0.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each evidence plaque into the time model it matches. Featured case: at Meddius in Charlottesville, staff have no schedules, show up when they want, and are judged only on completing their work, with goals kept separate from pay. Which column does this practice belong in?","column_a_label":"Results-only work environment (ROWE)","column_b_label":"Flexibility and empowerment (cosmetic control)","column_c_label":"Billable hour (Motivation 2.0 time tracking)","rows":[{"label":"Best Buy corporate employees work as much time as the work takes; productivity rose 35 percent and voluntary turnover dropped 320 basis points","col":"a"},{"label":"A company lets employees pick arrival windows but still polices lateness and counts hours","col":"b"},{"label":"A lawyer must record time in six-minute increments or risk her job","col":"c"},{"label":"Meddius staff have no schedules; they show up when they want and must only complete the work","col":"a"},{"label":"Ressler and Thompson's verdict on formal flex time programs: a con game","col":"b"},{"label":"Chief Justice Rehnquist notes that expected billing of over two thousand hours a year creates temptations to exaggerate hours put in","col":"c"},{"label":"Jeff Gunther keeps specific goals at Meddius but refuses to tie them to compensation","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["In a ROWE, every employee must work from home","The billable hour makes sense for nonroutine work such as law","Empowerment grants genuine autonomy because the organization delegates real power"],"explanation":"The Meddius practice matches Ressler and Thompson's ROWE design, in which people control when they work and are judged on results, not hours. Flex time that widens the fences but keeps surveillance is cosmetic control, and the billable hour is pure input-based tracking that shifts focus from solving problems to piling up hours. Only the ROWE flips the old model from putting in time to getting results."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Player or Pawn: Invented Management vs the Human Default Setting","visual_frame_description":"A split diorama shows a television and a bicycle floating beside a tree and a river, while below, a toddler crawls toward a toy while a gray-suited figure clips on a leash. The player sorts belief cards into three arches labeled Invention, Default Setting, and Motivation 2.0 Assumption.","player_mission":"Assign each belief card to the correct arch: management as invented technology, the human default setting, or the Motivation 2.0 assumption about human nature. Classify the featured toddler scene first, as it establishes the chapter's core claim.","key_concepts":["Management does not emanate from nature; as strategy guru Gary Hamel observes, management is a technology, like a television or a bicycle, something humans invented.","Like Motivation 2.0, management is a technology that has grown creaky, its central ethic remains control, and its chief tools remain extrinsic motivators.","The idea of management presumes that to take action or move forward we need a prod, and that absent a reward or punishment we would remain happily and inertly in place.","Management also presumes that once people get moving they need direction, because without a firm and reliable guide they would wander.","Pink argues our basic nature is to be curious and self-directed, citing the six-month-old or one-year-old who is curious and self-directed straight out of the box.","If a person is passive and inert at age fourteen or forty-three, that is not because it is their nature but because something flipped their default setting.","Management may be one of the forces switching the default setting, producing the very passivity it claims to manage.","Submerging part of our nature in the name of economic survival can be a sensible move, but today achievement swings on allowing it to surface.","Autonomy is the most important of Deci and Ryan's three basic needs, the sun around which self-determination theory's planets orbit.","Researchers have found a link between autonomy and well-being in North America, Western Europe, Russia, Turkey, South Korea, and high-poverty Bangladesh."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests Pink's players-versus-pawns claim: humans are wired to be active and engaged, not passive and inert, and management as currently constituted may be what flips the default setting toward passivity. The chapter grounds this in self-determination theory and in the observation that very young children are curious and self-directed out of the box.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each belief card into its arch. Featured case: you watch a one-year-old spend an hour exploring a toy kitchen without any reward or instruction. Which column does this observation support?","column_a_label":"Management as invented technology","column_b_label":"Human default setting: curious and self-directed","column_c_label":"Motivation 2.0 assumption: passive, needing prods","rows":[{"label":"Management is like a television or a bicycle, not like a tree or a river","col":"a"},{"label":"A six-month-old who is curious and self-directed straight out of the box","col":"b"},{"label":"Absent a reward or punishment, people would remain happily and inertly in place","col":"c"},{"label":"Management's central ethic remains control and its chief tools remain extrinsic motivators","col":"a"},{"label":"Autonomy is the sun around which self-determination theory's planets orbit","col":"b"},{"label":"Once people get moving, they need a firm and reliable guide or they would wander","col":"c"},{"label":"In Russia, Turkey, South Korea, and high-poverty Bangladesh, researchers find autonomy sought and beneficial","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Management is a natural phenomenon, like a river, present in every human society","Deci and Ryan describe autonomy as the least important of the three basic needs","Passive inertia at age fourteen is the natural human state out of the box"],"explanation":"The exploring toddler supports the claim that humans are wired to be curious and self-directed, column b: that is how we are out of the box. Cards about management's invented, control-centered character belong in column a, and cards describing people as inert without prods or as wanderers without guides express the Motivation 2.0 assumption, column c. The toddler scene is evidence that passivity is a flipped setting, not the factory default."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Where Autonomy's Payoffs Show Up: Workplace, School, and Across Cultures","visual_frame_description":"A world map dashboard lights up in three zones: an office tower, a lecture hall, and a globe with pins in Russia, Turkey, South Korea, and Bangladesh. Evidence plaques from studies fly to their matching zone, and a confirmation chime sounds for each correct placement.","player_mission":"Assign each research finding to the setting where it was observed: the workplace, education, or cross-cultural well-being research. Classify the featured law school study first, then place the remaining findings.","key_concepts":["A cluster of recent behavioral science studies finds autonomous motivation promotes greater conceptual understanding, better grades, enhanced persistence, higher productivity, less burnout, and greater psychological well-being.","In 2004, Deci and Ryan, along with Paul Baard of Fordham University, studied workers at an American investment bank and found greater job satisfaction among employees whose bosses offered autonomy support.","In the investment bank study, enhanced job satisfaction led to higher performance on the job.","Cornell University researchers studied 320 small businesses, half granting workers autonomy, and found the autonomy-granting firms grew at four times the rate of control-oriented firms and had one-third the turnover.","A 2007 study of two American law schools found students' overall well-being plummeted over three years, in large part because their need for autonomy was thwarted.","In the law school study, students with greater autonomy over course selection, assignments, and relations with professors showed far less steep declines and posted better grades and bar exam scores.","Researchers have found a link between autonomy and overall well-being in North America and Western Europe, as well as in Russia, Turkey, and South Korea.","Even in high-poverty non-Western locales like Bangladesh, social scientists have found that autonomy is something people seek and that improves their lives.","A sense of autonomy has a powerful effect on individual performance and attitude, and those effects carry over to the workplace.","Francis Green, a British economist, blames the lack of individual discretion at work for declining productivity and job satisfaction in the UK."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the evidential breadth of self-determination theory: the same autonomy-need claim is supported in workplace studies, educational studies, and cross-cultural well-being research. Pink marshals the 2004 investment bank study, the Cornell small-business study, a 2007 law school study, and evidence from Russia, Turkey, South Korea, and Bangladesh.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each finding into the setting where it was observed. Featured case: a 2007 study of two American law schools tracked students for three years, finding well-being plummeted largely because the need for autonomy was thwarted, while students with more say over courses and assignments did better. Which column does this study belong in?","column_a_label":"Workplace evidence","column_b_label":"Educational evidence","column_c_label":"Cross-cultural well-being evidence","rows":[{"label":"Deci, Ryan, and Paul Baard's 2004 study of an American investment bank found autonomy-supportive bosses raised job satisfaction and performance","col":"a"},{"label":"Cornell researchers found autonomy-granting small businesses grew at four times the rate and had one-third the turnover","col":"a"},{"label":"In a 2007 study of two American law schools, students with autonomy over courses and assignments posted better grades and bar exam scores","col":"b"},{"label":"Law students' well-being plummeted over three years as their need for autonomy was thwarted","col":"b"},{"label":"Researchers link autonomy to well-being in North America, Western Europe, Russia, Turkey, and South Korea","col":"c"},{"label":"Even in high-poverty Bangladesh, people seek autonomy and it improves their lives","col":"c"},{"label":"Autonomous motivation promotes greater conceptual understanding and enhanced persistence at school and in sport","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The Cornell study found control-oriented small businesses grew at four times the rate","Autonomy research has only been replicated inside the United States","The investment bank study found autonomy support reduced job satisfaction"],"explanation":"The featured law school study tracks student well-being, grades, and bar exam scores, so it is educational evidence, column b. The investment bank study and the Cornell small-business study measure jobs, firms, and turnover, placing them in the workplace column. Findings from Russia, Turkey, South Korea, and Bangladesh show autonomy benefits travel across cultures and even into high-poverty settings."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: Ending the Billable Hour","visual_frame_description":"The battle screen shows a midnight law firm corridor where associates in rolled sleeves log six-minute increments beneath a glowing billable-hours board. Dana's desk is piled with half-finished briefs while a partner frowns at a padded entry on her screen. The player analyzes the mechanism in stage one, then casts the deciding vote at the management committee table.","player_mission":"Diagnose why high bonuses and long hours are destroying Dana's craft, engagement, and honesty. Then cast the deciding vote among the firm's reform options, choosing the intervention that restores decision latitude and intrinsic motivation.","key_concepts":["Behavioral scientists use decision latitude to describe the choices, and perceived choices, a person has, and lawyers face intense demands with relatively little of it.","Social scientists offer three explanations for lawyers' misery: optimism harms them, law is often a zero-sum game, and they lack decision latitude.","A 2007 study of two American law schools found students' overall well-being plummeted over three years, in large part because their need for autonomy was thwarted.","Students with greater autonomy over course selection, assignments, and relations with professors showed far less steep declines and posted better grades and bar exam scores.","The billable hour forces most lawyers in large, prestigious firms to keep scrupulous track of their time, often in six-minute increments.","If the rewards come from time, then time is what firms will get, and lawyers' focus veers from output, solving a client's problem, to input, piling up hours.","High-stakes, measurable goals can drain intrinsic motivation, sap individual initiative, and even encourage unethical behavior.","Chief Justice William Rehnquist warned that if one is expected to bill more than two thousand hours per year, there are bound to be temptations to exaggerate the hours actually put in.","For nonroutine tasks such as law, the link between how much time somebody spends and what that somebody produces is irregular and unpredictable.","Some law firms now charge a flat rate rather than a time-based fee, with the presiding partner of one of New York's leading firms declaring this is the time to get rid of the billable hour."],"boss_battle_explanation":"The billable hour is an autonomy-crushing relic of Motivation 2.0. Lawyers log time in six-minute increments, their focus veers from output, solving the client's problem, to input, piling up as many hours as possible, and high-stakes measurable time goals drain intrinsic motivation, sap initiative, and even encourage unethical behavior.","stage_1_observation":"It is 2 A.M. in the marble corridors of a large New York law firm. Associate Dana has cleared her annual two-thousand-hour target by logging six-minute increments in near-total darkness, and her billable-hours dashboard shows green. Yet her briefs are getting sloppy, a partner caught a padded entry on Friday, and Dana, once the firm's most promising junior, now jokes that her work product is the spreadsheet itself.","stage_1_question":"Dana's bonuses have never been higher, and her hours have never been longer. Which mechanism best explains why her engagement, craft, and honesty are all collapsing at once?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The billable hour redirects Dana's focus from the output of her work, solving the client's problem, to its input, piling up hours, and when the rewards come from time, time is what firms get. Chief Justice Rehnquist warned that if one is expected to bill more than two thousand hours per year, there are bound to be temptations to exaggerate the hours actually put in, and such high-stakes measurable goals drain intrinsic motivation and sap individual initiative.","stage_2_intervention":"The management committee convenes to decide the firm's fate. Associate after associate describes watching the clock instead of the case. One partner proposes auditing six-minute logs weekly, another wants to raise the per-hour bonus rate, and a third suggests switching the practice group to flat-rate fees and abandoning the hourly log altogether. You have the deciding vote. Which intervention do you back?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Back the flat-rate switch and retire the hourly log, the direction already being taken by leading law firms, with the presiding partner of one of New York's leading firms declaring that this is the time to get rid of the billable hour. Flat fees restore decision latitude by loosening the link between time spent and reward, letting lawyers focus on solving the client's problem rather than piling up hours, which is the Type I, Motivation 3.0 path.","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the billable hour's assault on decision latitude, the choices and perceived choices a person has, and on intrinsic motivation. The book cites Chief Justice Rehnquist on the temptation to exaggerate hours and the rise of flat-rate billing at leading New York firms as the Motivation 3.0 correction.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: Defending 20 Percent Time","visual_frame_description":"The battle screen shows Atlassian's Sydney loft where engineers crash out code overnight while a wall projection ticks up a one-million-dollar cost figure beneath a green-eyeshade caricature. Project managers cluster in the foreground proposing quarterly time reports. The player first analyzes why Cannon-Brookes rejects tracking, then faces the finance guy's final challenge.","player_mission":"Identify the reasoning behind Cannon-Brookes's refusal to track engineers' time during 20 percent time. Then decide how to justify the one-million-dollar yearlong trial to the CFO, choosing the evidence-based rebuttal that keeps the autonomy program alive.","key_concepts":["FedEx Days at Atlassian are twenty-four-hour bursts of freedom and creativity, beginning at two P.M. on a Thursday and ending with a four P.M. Friday all-hands meeting.","In the spring of 2008, Atlassian announced that developers could spend 20 percent of their time working on any project they wanted, and in 2009 the practice became permanent.","Cannon-Brookes estimated that seventy engineers spending 20 percent of their time over a six-month period amounted to an investment of about one million dollars.","The company's chief financial officer was aghast at the price tag, and some project managers were unhappy because it meant ceding some of their control over employees.","When a few managers wanted to track employees' time to make sure they did not abuse the privilege, Cannon-Brookes said no.","Cannon-Brookes rejected time tracking because it was too controlling: he wanted to back his engineers and take it on faith that they would do good things.","According to Cannon-Brookes, people are way more efficient about 20 percent time than regular work time, skipping newsfeeds and Facebook.","In a yearlong trial, Atlassian developers launched forty-eight new projects under the 20 percent arrangement.","Cannon-Brookes's reply to the finance guy lists the things delivered, the zero turnover in engineering, and the highly motivated engineers always trying to perfect the product.","Most Atlassian employees came in substantially under the 20 percent figure because they did not want to let down their current teammates on ongoing projects."],"boss_battle_explanation":"Task autonomy at scale: Atlassian's quarterly FedEx Days grew into 20 percent time, where engineers spend a fifth of their working time on projects of their own choosing. The finance case against it was steep, seventy engineers at 20 percent for six months cost about one million dollars, but Cannon-Brookes refused to track his engineers because that was too controlling, and he answered the price objection with delivered products, zero engineering turnover, and motivated engineers.","stage_1_observation":"In Atlassian's sunny Sydney office, the chief financial officer stares at a spreadsheet: seventy engineers, 20 percent of their time, six months, a one-million-dollar price tag. A knot of project managers huddles outside, grumbling that they have ceded control over their people, and one proposes quarterly time reports so nobody can abuse the privilege. Cannon-Brookes, the cofounder, sits calm at the end of the table.","stage_1_question":"The project managers propose tracking each engineer's 20 percent hours to make sure the privilege is not abused. Why does Cannon-Brookes reject the tracking proposal outright?","stage_1_correct_answer":"He rejects it because tracking was too controlling: he wanted to back his engineers and take it on faith that they would do good things. Adding time surveillance would convert an autonomy-based program into a Motivation 2.0 control scheme, reintroducing exactly the input-based monitoring that the four T's of task and time autonomy are meant to dissolve.","stage_2_intervention":"Days later, the finance guy in the green eyeshades comes calling for a decision on the yearlong 20 percent time trial. Your options are to kill the program to save the million dollars, to keep it but require monthly hour reports, to cap it at one FedEx Day per quarter, or to keep the program free of tracking and answer the price objection with evidence. Which decision do you make?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep the program untracked and answer the finance guy with the same case Cannon-Brookes used: the long list of things delivered, zero turnover in engineering, and highly motivated engineers always trying to perfect and improve the product. The urge to measure can also be tempered by the finding that most Atlassian engineers came in substantially under the 20 percent figure because they did not want to let down their teammates on ongoing projects.","difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests task autonomy and the trust-based accountability of Motivation 3.0, in which people want to be accountable and control over task, time, technique, and team is the pathway to that accountability. The book documents Cannon-Brookes's refusal to track engineers, the one-million-dollar cost estimate, and the delivered-products, zero-turnover, motivated-engineers rebuttal to the price objection.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: Rewiring the Call Center","visual_frame_description":"The battle screen shows a beige Midwest call center warren: headsets strapped on, diet sodas at elbows, and managers with stopwatches while red call-duration warnings flash on a wall display. In the corner, Marisol's badge glows as she resolves a complaint her own way. The player studies the failure mechanism in stage one, then designs the center's overhaul in stage two.","player_mission":"Explain why the script-and-monitor system produces deadening routine and crushing turnover. Then choose the redesign that restores autonomy over technique and self-selects the right workforce, using the Zappos playbook.","key_concepts":["Call center reps have little decision latitude and their jobs are often the very definition of routine.","In most call centers, reps retrieve a script when a call comes in and follow it, sometimes word for word, hoping to get the caller off the line quickly.","Many call center managers listen in on reps' conversations and monitor how long each call lasts in an effort to boost productivity.","Call centers in the United States and the UK have annual turnover rates averaging about 35 percent, double the rate for other jobs.","In some call centers the annual turnover rate exceeds 100 percent, meaning none of the people working there today will be there a year from now.","Tony Hsieh's Zappos puts new hires through a week of training, then offers them two thousand dollars to leave if they feel the company is not for them.","Hsieh is hacking the Motivation 2.0 operating system, using an if-then reward not to motivate better performance but to weed out people unfit for a Motivation 3.0 workplace.","The people who remain at Zappos receive decent pay, and just as important, they have autonomy over technique: the company does not monitor call times or require scripts.","Zappos's turnover is minimal, and it consistently ranks as one of the best companies for customer service, ahead of Cadillac, BMW, and Apple and roughly equal to Jaguar and the Ritz-Carlton.","JetBlue relied on home-based telephone customer service employees from its 2000 launch and earned customer service rankings far ahead of its competitors."],"boss_battle_explanation":"Autonomy over technique: scripts and call monitoring strip customer service reps of decision latitude and turn their work into deadening routine. US and UK call centers average about 35 percent annual turnover, double the rate for other jobs, while Zappos drops scripts and call-time monitoring entirely, pays decently, and uses a paid two-thousand-dollar exit offer after the training week to self-select people who fit a Motivation 3.0 workplace.","stage_1_observation":"In a beige call center outside Chicago, dozens of reps sit in a warren of cramped cubicles, headsets strapped on, diet sodas at their elbows, reading scripts word for word off their monitors. A manager prowls with a stopwatch, and red call-duration warnings flash on the wall display. Rep Marisol, three weeks on the job, abandons the script to actually help an irate caller with a lost delivery and resolves the complaint in nine minutes, then gets written up for going off-script and blowing the three-minute target.","stage_1_question":"Marisol just delivered the best customer outcome of the shift and was punished for it. Why does the script-and-monitor system keep failing this center and others like it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because it strips reps of decision latitude, the choices and perceived choices a person has, and pushes their focus from the work itself to the measured time, so the job becomes pure routine and the workforce churns: call centers in the United States and the UK average about 35 percent annual turnover, double the rate for other jobs, and some centers exceed 100 percent annually.","stage_2_intervention":"The operations VP gives you one quarter to fix the center, with full design freedom. Your options are to keep the scripts but add bonuses for short calls, to cut the script to ten lines and require its first and last sentences, to adopt the Zappos model of no scripts, no call-time monitoring, decent pay, and a paid two-thousand-dollar exit offer after the training week, or to make an example of Marisol. Which do you choose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Adopt the Zappos model: end scripts and call-time monitoring, pay decently, and at the end of the seven-day training week offer every new hire two thousand dollars to leave if they feel Zappos is not for them. Hsieh deliberately uses this if-then reward not to motivate performance but to weed out people who are not fit for a Motivation 3.0 workplace, and the payoff was minimal turnover and top customer service rankings ahead of Cadillac, BMW, and Apple and roughly equal to Jaguar and the Ritz-Carlton.","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests autonomy over technique as the cure for routine, monitored work: reps who handle calls their own way show minimal turnover and elite customer service at Zappos. The book contrasts the 35 percent average annual call center churn in the US and UK with Zappos's results and explains the two-thousand-dollar exit offer as a clever hack of the Motivation 2.0 operating system.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: Scaffolding the Leap to ROWE","visual_frame_description":"The battle screen shows a billing group converted to a results-only environment: most desks are empty on a sunny Tuesday while productivity charts climb, and three staffers huddle around Elena with their old schedule books still open. The player diagnoses the strugglers' mechanism in stage one, then chooses the Friday decision at the CEO's desk.","player_mission":"Diagnose why Elena, a veteran of rigid eight A.M. rule firms, is failing where her coworkers flourish. Then choose the intervention that keeps the ROWE's productivity and stress gains while scaffolding the strugglers into autonomy.","key_concepts":["Transitioning to autonomy won't, and often can't, happen in one fell swoop.","If we pluck people out of controlling environments, when they have known nothing else, and plop them in a ROWE or an environment of undiluted autonomy, they will struggle.","Richard Ryan argues that organizations must provide scaffolding to help every employee find his footing in the transition.","At Meddius, two employees struggled with the freedom of the ROWE and left, while most others found their groove.","After a few weeks of the Meddius experiment, productivity rose, stress declined, and Gunther decided to go with ROWE permanently.","Some Meddius staff came from extremely controlling environments, including a company where late arrivals had to write an explanation for everyone else to read.","People in a ROWE still had specific goals they had to reach, such as completing a project by a certain time or ringing up a particular number of sales.","Gunther refused to tie goals to compensation because that creates a culture that says it is all about the money and not enough about the work.","Money is only a threshold motivator: once a company meets the baseline, dollars and cents do not much affect performance and motivation.","Different individuals prize different aspects of autonomy, and no one aspect is universally the most important."],"boss_battle_explanation":"Transitioning to autonomy cannot, and often cannot, happen in one fell swoop. Richard Ryan argues organizations must provide scaffolding to help every employee find his footing, because people plucked from controlling environments and dropped into undiluted autonomy will struggle. At Meddius, two employees struggled with the freedom and left, even as the rest found their groove, raised productivity, and lowered stress.","stage_1_observation":"You are the operations head converting a forty-person billing group to a results-only work environment. Three weeks in, overall productivity is up and stress scores are down, but three staffers, all refugees from a firm that demanded eight A.M. arrivals and public written explanations for any lateness, look lost in the empty schedule. Two of them have already missed deadlines, and one, Elena, has asked to talk about quitting.","stage_1_question":"Elena thrived for a decade under rigid rules and is now failing in a workplace that grants total time freedom. Why is she struggling so badly, even though her coworkers are flourishing?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because years of external control flipped her default setting toward passivity and dependence: when people have known nothing but controlling environments and are plucked out and dropped into a ROWE or an environment of undiluted autonomy, they struggle, which is exactly why Richard Ryan argues organizations must provide scaffolding to help every employee find his footing. Gunther's Meddius experiment saw the same pattern, with two employees who struggled with the freedom and left.","stage_2_intervention":"The CEO wants a decision by Friday. Your options are to abandon the ROWE and restore fixed schedules, to keep the ROWE but scaffold the strugglers with clear goals, help on demand, and weekly coaching meetings without resurrecting time surveillance, to let them sink or swim in undiluted autonomy, or to tie their specific goals to compensation so money sharpens their focus. Which do you choose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep the ROWE and scaffold: people still had specific goals they had to reach, help was available when needed, and Gunther refused to tie goals to compensation because that creates a culture that says it is all about the money and not enough about the work. Scaffolding honors his belief that money is only a threshold motivator once the baseline is met, and it lets each employee find her footing instead of jettisoning the productivity and stress gains of the results-only structure.","difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests Richard Ryan's scaffolding argument, that autonomy cannot be switched on overnight because people from controlling environments need help finding their footing. The book grounds it in the Meddius case, where two employees struggled with freedom and left while productivity rose and stress declined for the rest, and clarifies that goals and accountability stay, while compensation stays untied to goals.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Boss Battle: Building a Grouplet","visual_frame_description":"The battle screen shows a Redmond conference room where a vice president points at an org chart while your notebook displays Bharat Mediratta's grouplet profile, a small self-organized cluster of coders with an almost-no-budget banner overhead. The player studies the mechanism divide in stage one, then chooses how to spread the faster testing method.","player_mission":"Explain why a top-down mandate fails where a budgetless grouplet succeeds. Then decide how to launch the faster testing method by choosing the self-organized path that the research supports.","key_concepts":["Autonomy over team is a taller order than task, time, or technique, and it is one reason people are drawn to entrepreneurship.","Ample research has shown that people working in self-organized teams are more satisfied than those working in inherited teams.","Studies by Deci and others have shown that people high in intrinsic motivation are better coworkers.","Autonomy can be contagious: if you want to work with more Type I's, the best strategy is to become one yourself.","Bharat Mediratta's testing grouplet at Google slowly turned the organization on its axis.","A grouplet is a small, self-organized team that has almost no budget and even less authority, but that tries to change something within the company.","Mediratta says that if your 20 percent idea is a new product, it is usually pretty easy to find a few like-minded people and start coding away.","20 percent time initiatives usually slice across the organization chart, connecting people who share an interest rather than a department.","At Whole Foods, a department's employees do the hiring, voting on a candidate after a thirty-day trial period on the team.","At W. L. Gore and Associates, anybody who wants to rise in the ranks and lead a team must assemble people willing to work with her."],"boss_battle_explanation":"Autonomy over team is the least developed of the four T's, but it has a proven tool: the grouplet, a small self-organized team with almost no budget and even less authority that tries to change something within the company. Google engineer Bharat Mediratta's testing grouplet slowly turned the organization on its axis, and research shows people working in self-organized teams are more satisfied than those in inherited teams, while autonomous people make better coworkers and autonomy is contagious.","stage_1_observation":"At a sprawling software company in Redmond, you are an engineer on a stable but skeptical inherited team. Your group discovered a way to test code in a third of the time, but your teammates shrug it off, and the VP of engineering says all rollouts must follow the org chart. On your desk sits a profile of Bharat Mediratta, whose testing grouplet at Google needed almost no budget and even less authority, just an informal band of like-minded coders.","stage_1_question":"A formal mandate from the VP has a better budget and a bigger banner, yet Mediratta's budgetless grouplet moved an entire company. Why do self-organized teams outperform top-down directives for this kind of change?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because ample research shows people working in self-organized teams are more satisfied than those working in inherited teams, and a grouplet built autonomously from shared interest generates commitment that orders from above cannot. Mediratta's testing grouplet, a team built autonomously without direction from the top, slowly turned the organization on its axis, and 20 percent time initiatives slice across the org chart to connect people who share an interest rather than a department.","stage_2_intervention":"You have three choices for spreading the faster testing method: file a proposal up the chain and wait for approval, quietly recruit a few like-minded engineers from other departments into a self-organized grouplet and start implementing, get the VP to mandate the new method with quarterly compliance checks, or resign and freelance. Which do you choose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Recruit a few like-minded engineers across departments into a self-organized grouplet and start implementing. As Mediratta put it, if your 20 percent idea is a new product, it is usually pretty easy to find like-minded people and start coding away, and the science affirms that self-organized teams are more satisfied while intrinsically motivated people are better coworkers. If you want to work with more Type I's, the best strategy is to become one yourself, because autonomy is contagious.","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests autonomy over team through the grouplet mechanism, a self-organized team with almost no budget or authority that makes systemic change from within. The book cites Bharat Mediratta's testing grouplet at Google, the finding that self-organized teams are more satisfied than inherited teams, and the claim that autonomy is contagious.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The ROWE Conversation: Earning Your Own Schedule","visual_frame_description":"The dialogue screen shows Jeff's office on a rainy Friday in Charlottesville: a wall chart of project deadlines, wet umbrellas by the door, and Marcus in a hoodie fidgeting across the desk while Jeff leans back with the calm of a monk. Speech bubbles carrying the exchange appear between the two chairs. The player selects Marcus's side of the conversation.","player_mission":"Play Marcus as he navigates his first results-only work environment with CEO Jeff. Choose responses that accept the autonomy contract, focus on outcomes and help on demand, and resist recreating surveillance or trading the freedom for money.","key_concepts":["A ROWE, or results-only work environment, gives people no schedules; they show up when they want and just have to get their work done.","Jeff Gunther launched the ninety-day ROWE experiment at Meddius at the company's holiday dinner in December 2008.","In the beginning, people did not take to the ROWE: the office filled up around nine A.M. and emptied out in the early evening, just as before.","Some staffers had come out of extremely controlling environments, such as a company where late arrivals had to write an explanation for everyone else to read.","After a few weeks, most people found their groove: productivity rose and stress declined.","Two Meddius employees struggled with the freedom and left, but by the end of the test period Gunther decided to go with ROWE permanently.","People in a ROWE still had specific goals they had to reach, and Gunther was there to assist when they needed help.","Gunther believes that in a ROWE environment, employees are far less likely to jump to another job for a $10,000 or even $20,000 increase in salary.","Gunther says his staffers are not two-by-fours or human resources but partners, and partners need to direct their own lives.","For software developers, designers, and others doing high-level creative work, it is all about craftsmanship, and they need a lot of autonomy."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests autonomy support as documented in the 2004 Deci, Ryan, and Baard investment bank study: bosses who see the employee's point of view, give meaningful feedback and information, and provide ample choice raise satisfaction and performance. It also rehearses Richard Ryan's scaffolding point, because Marcus is transitioning from a controlling environment into his first ROWE.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"You are Marcus, a newly hired developer at Meddius in Charlottesville, and this is week two of the company's ninety-day results-only work environment experiment. Your last employer required everyone to arrive by eight A.M., and latecomers had to write an explanation for the whole staff to read. With no schedule to obey, you feel adrift, fear you are slacking, and have been coming in at eight anyway, then staring at the clock. Jeff, the CEO and founder, calls you into his office on a rainy Friday afternoon.","dialogue":["Marcus: I have to be honest, Jeff. Without the eight o'clock rule, I keep second-guessing myself. How do I even know I am working enough?","Jeff: I expected that. When I announced this experiment at our holiday dinner, most people kept their old hours for the first few weeks. That is how a ROWE starts.","Marcus: But if nobody watches the clock, what stops me from drifting?","Jeff: The work stops you, or it does not, and then we talk about the work. People here still have specific goals, like shipping a project by a date. They just own how they get there.","Marcus: And if I leave at three P.M. to watch my daughter's soccer game, that does not look like slacking?","Jeff: It looks like a father who will come back sharper. My developers are craftspeople, and craftsmanship needs autonomy, not surveillance.","Marcus: So you would rather I trust myself than report to you every morning?","Jeff: I would rather you focus on the work itself, not on whether someone calls you a slacker. That is the whole point of results only.","Marcus: What if I miss a deadline with nobody watching?","Jeff: Then you and I sit down and figure out what help you need. My job is to create conditions for your best work, not to patrol your chair."],"choices":["Promise Jeff you will send him a daily log of hours worked so you both have proof you are pulling your weight","Reach for the results-only deal: focus on completing your projects, ask Jeff for help when you hit a wall, and stop measuring yourself by seat time","Ask Jeff for a personal compromise: keep the old eight A.M. rule just for you so you stop feeling guilty","Ask Jeff for a raise as compensation for the anxiety this freedom is causing you"],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Autonomy support and scaffolding within self-determination theory: bosses who see issues from the employee's point of view, give meaningful feedback and information, and provide ample choice over what to do and how to do it raise satisfaction, performance, and well-being.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it accepts the ROWE contract, judged on outcomes with goals and help on demand, while Jeff's responses embody autonomy support: seeing the employee's viewpoint, offering information and assistance, and never converting goals into compensation, since money is only a threshold motivator. Choice 0 fails because a daily hour log reimposes the input-based surveillance of Motivation 2.0, recreating the very monitoring that makes autonomy meaningless. Choice 2 fails because it surrenders the choice that defines autonomy, behaving with a full sense of volition and choice is the SDT definition of autonomous motivation, and it would leave Marcus dependent on the leash he just escaped. Choice 3 fails because once baseline pay is adequate, dollars and cents do not much affect motivation; the freedom to do great work is worth more than a pay raise and cannot be bought with one."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The 20 Percent Time Pitch: Faith Over Tracking","visual_frame_description":"The dialogue screen shows a glass-walled Austin conference room: Lena holds the CFO's time-tracking proposal in one hand while Priya's whiteboard sketch of the test-suite catcher glows on a monitor behind her. Side-by-side speech bubbles carry the exchange between the two women. The player pilots Priya's arguments through the negotiation.","player_mission":"Play Priya as she negotiates the 20 percent time trial with her manager Lena, holding the line against minute-by-minute logging without sinking the whole initiative. Choose responses that defend results-based accountability and the trust-based case Cannon-Brookes used at Atlassian.","key_concepts":["Atlassian's 20 percent time gave engineers dedicated stack time, of their own direction, for product innovation, features, plugins, fixes, or additions they thought most important.","Cannon-Brookes refused to track engineers' time during 20 percent time because that was too controlling; he wanted to back his engineers and take it on faith that they would do good things.","In a yearlong trial, Atlassian developers launched forty-eight new projects under 20 percent time, and the practice became permanent in 2009.","Most Atlassian employees came in substantially under the 20 percent figure because they did not want to let down their current teammates.","Cannon-Brookes answered Atlassian's finance guy with a long list of things delivered, zero turnover in engineering, and highly motivated engineers always trying to perfect the product.","At Google, in a typical year more than half of the company's new offerings are birthed during the 20 percent period of pure autonomy.","Google News, Gmail, Orkut, Google Talk, Google Sky, and Google Translate all began as 20 percent projects.","3M's 15 percent bootlegging time and Google's 20 percent time trace back to William McKnight's creed: hire good people, and leave them alone.","Motivation 3.0 presumes that people want to be accountable, and that control over their task, time, technique, and team is a pathway to that accountability.","Encouraging autonomy does not mean discouraging accountability; whatever operating system is in place, people must be accountable for their work."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests task autonomy and trust-based accountability, the presumption of Motivation 3.0 that people want to be accountable and that giving them control over task and time is the pathway there. The exchange mirrors Atlassian's real defense of 20 percent time, delivered products, zero engineering turnover, and engineers who perfect the product, against a finance guy's price objection.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"You are Priya, a senior developer at a mid-size software firm in Austin. The company is weighing a six-month 20 percent time trial modeled on Atlassian, and your manager Lena is genuinely interested, but the CFO is demanding quarterly hour reports on exactly what everyone does with the time, calling it guardrails. You want 20 percent time to build an automated tool that catches broken test suites before they reach production, the thing you currently fix by hand every Monday morning.","dialogue":["Lena: The CFO will approve the trial only if we track everyone's 20 percent hours. He calls it guardrails. What do you think?","Priya: Atlassian's engineers got that exact request, and Cannon-Brookes said no. He told them that would be too controlling; he wanted to back his engineers and take it on faith that they would do good things.","Lena: Faith is expensive. He estimated seventy engineers at 20 percent time for six months cost a million dollars.","Priya: And they launched forty-eight new projects in a yearlong trial, with zero turnover in engineering. Our turnover last year cost more than their whole program.","Lena: So what would you build with your 20 percent?","Priya: A tool that finds broken test suites before they hit production. It is not in my job description, but it is the thing I already fix by hand every week.","Lena: If I approve the trial, how do I answer the CFO's spreadsheet?","Priya: The same way Atlassian answered its finance guy: show him the list of things we delivered, the retention numbers, and the engineers who keep perfecting the product.","Lena: And if you blow through six months with nothing to show?","Priya: Then we have learned something real about what motivates us. But I have been shipping fixes in my spare time for a year, and I am asking to do it openly."],"choices":["Offer to submit a weekly timesheet of your 20 percent hours so the CFO gets his guardrails and the trial proceeds","Refuse the trial entirely unless the company promises never to track a single hour of anyone's time","Agree to the trial with results-based reporting: what got delivered and how it helps the product, with no minute-by-minute logs","Ask for a cash bonus tied to the tool's success before you write your first line of code"],"optimal_choice":2,"theoretical_principle":"Task autonomy within the four T's and trust-based accountability, the assumption of Motivation 3.0 that people want to be accountable and that giving them control over task, time, technique, and team is the pathway to that accountability.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 2 works because it keeps autonomy over task and time intact by reporting outcomes, what was delivered and how it helps, rather than inputs, and that is precisely how Atlassian justified the million-dollar program: delivered products, zero engineering turnover, and highly motivated engineers. Choice 0 fails because minute-level reporting reintroduces the billable-hour dynamic, where rewards flow from time and focus shifts from the work to the log, draining intrinsic motivation. Choice 1 fails because all-or-nothing rigidity throws away a valuable trial and the dialogue with Lena, and it treats autonomy as isolation rather than as the partnership Cannon-Brookes built. Choice 3 fails because attaching a cash reward to the project converts it into an if-then deal, reasserting Motivation 2.0 in the one domain, self-directed task choice, where it does the most damage."}]},{"id":"ch10","title":"CHAPTER 5 - Mastery","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Flow, autotelic experience, and mastery","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen animation in Csikszentmihalyi's Claremont research office. On the left, a painter at an easel is so absorbed his coffee cools untouched; on the right, a scroll of Experience Sampling Method booklet entries streams by with timestamps and mood ratings. Floating observation cards drift across the screen and the player must drag each one into one of three labeled bins.","player_mission":"Read each observation card and sort it into the bin it best describes: autotelic experience, flow, or mastery. Complete all seven sorts so the researcher's wall of findings is accurately filed.","key_concepts":["Csikszentmihalyi coined 'autotelic experience' from the Greek auto (self) and telos (goal or purpose); in an autotelic experience the goal is self-fulfilling and the activity is its own reward.","Painters Csikszentmihalyi observed during his Ph.D. research were so enthralled they seemed in a trance, with time passing quickly and self-consciousness dissolving.","Csikszentmihalyi replaced the Greek-derived adjective with the word people used for these optimal moments: flow.","In flow, goals are clear, such as reaching the top of the mountain or molding the clay just right.","In flow, feedback is immediate: the ball sails in or out of bounds and the pot you are throwing comes out smooth or uneven.","The most important flow condition is a perfect relationship between what a person had to do and what he could do.","The flow challenge sits a notch or two beyond current abilities, stretching body and mind so that effort itself becomes the most delicious reward.","In flow, people live so deeply in the moment that their sense of time, place, and even self melts away.","Csikszentmihalyi developed the Experience Sampling Method, paging people eight times a day at random intervals to record what they were doing and how they felt.","Mastery, the desire to get better and better at something that matters, unfolds over a longer time horizon than flow: one happens in a moment, the other over months, years, sometimes decades."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the distinction between autotelic experience, the flow state, and mastery: autotelic activities are their own reward, flow is the moment of perfect challenge with clear goals and immediate feedback, and mastery is the long-horizon pursuit. Csikszentmihalyi's Experience Sampling Method data showed that the highest, most satisfying moments in people's lives occur when they are in flow.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each observation into the concept it best describes: autotelic experience, flow, or mastery.","column_a_label":"Autotelic experience","column_b_label":"Flow","column_c_label":"Mastery","rows":[{"label":"The activity is its own reward; the goal is self-fulfilling.","col":"a"},{"label":"Goals are clear and feedback is immediate.","col":"b"},{"label":"The challenge sits a notch or two beyond your current abilities.","col":"b"},{"label":"The desire to get better and better at something that matters.","col":"c"},{"label":"Can happen tomorrow morning; it lives in a single moment.","col":"b"},{"label":"Unfolds over months, years, sometimes decades.","col":"c"},{"label":"Painters so enthralled that time passes quickly and self-consciousness dissolves.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Any activity that ends in a trophy or medal is autotelic","Flow requires challenges far beyond your current ability so you are pushed to the limit","Flow and mastery operate on the same time horizon"],"explanation":"The matrix contrasts three related concepts from the chapter. Autotelic experiences are activities that are their own reward, which is why the painters in Csikszentmihalyi's research seemed entranced. Flow is the in-the-moment state defined by clear goals, immediate feedback, and a challenge calibrated just beyond ability, so the sense of self melts away. Mastery is the long-horizon pursuit of getting better at something that matters; flow can occur tomorrow morning, whereas mastery takes years."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Entity theory and incremental theory","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen classroom in East Palo Alto. On the left, Ms. Alvarez's fifth-grade class wrestles with word problems; on the right, a think-aloud panel shows each student's inner monologue during the task. Statement cards float across the bottom of the screen, and the player must drag each card to the belief-system column it reflects.","player_mission":"Read each inner-thought card and sort it into the column it reflects: entity theory, incremental theory, or Motivation 2.0 compliance logic. Sort every card so the class's belief map accurately shows which self-theory each voice represents.","key_concepts":["Carol Dweck's signature insight is that what people believe shapes what they achieve; our self-theories determine how we interpret experiences and can set boundaries on what we accomplish.","Entity theorists believe intelligence is an entity that exists within us in a finite supply that we cannot increase.","Incremental theorists believe intelligence, though it varies slightly from person to person, can be increased with effort.","Dweck analogizes intelligence to height for entity theorists, since you cannot make yourself taller, and to strength for incremental theorists, since you can pump iron and grow.","If you believe intelligence is a fixed quantity, every educational and professional encounter becomes a measure of how much you have.","If you believe intelligence can be increased, the same encounters become opportunities for growth: intelligence is something you develop rather than demonstrate.","Dweck says the entity theory is a system that requires a diet of easy successes.","Under an entity view, if you have to work hard, it means you are not very good.","Type X behavior often holds an entity theory of intelligence and disdains effort as a sign of weakness.","Type I behavior holds an incremental theory of intelligence and welcomes effort as a way to improve at something that matters."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests Carol Dweck's self-theory framework: entity theorists treat intelligence as a fixed entity to be measured and demonstrated, while incremental theorists treat it as a capacity that grows through effort. Dweck's research shows the incremental view heads toward mastery, while the entity view demands easy successes and reads hard work as a sign of weakness.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each statement into the belief system it reflects: entity theory, incremental theory, or Motivation 2.0 compliance logic.","column_a_label":"Entity theory","column_b_label":"Incremental theory","column_c_label":"Motivation 2.0 compliance logic","rows":[{"label":"Intelligence is like height; wanting to grow taller does not make it happen.","col":"a"},{"label":"Intelligence is like strength; pumping iron builds more of it.","col":"b"},{"label":"That tough problem just measured how much I have.","col":"a"},{"label":"That tough problem is a chance to develop my ability.","col":"b"},{"label":"If I have to work hard, I must not be very good.","col":"a"},{"label":"Effort is a welcome route to getting better at something that matters.","col":"b"},{"label":"A nice bunch of carrots and the threat of a stick get the job done.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Intelligence is inherited and cannot change under any circumstances","Everyone is born with exactly the same amount of intelligence","Intelligence is nothing more than how fast you finish a timed test"],"explanation":"The matrix sorts statements about ability into Dweck's two self-theories plus the Motivation 2.0 premise. Statements that treat intelligence as fixed, measured, and demonstrated sort to entity theory; statements that treat it as expandable through effort sort to incremental theory, the mastery-oriented column; and statements that treat behavior as a product of carrots and sticks sort to Motivation 2.0 logic."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Performance goals and learning goals","visual_frame_description":"A junior high science classroom in the 1990s where half the students receive a sheet reading 'Goal: score high on the test' and half receive 'Goal: understand the scientific principles.' Later panels show both groups tackling a new set of related problems; the learning-goal students work deeper into the hour, while the performance-goal students stop early. The player sorts goal statements into labeled columns.","player_mission":"Classify each goal statement as a performance goal, a learning goal, or one of the goal features Dweck says both types share. Sort all seven statements so the study's design and findings are correctly mapped.","key_concepts":["Dweck says goals come in two varieties: performance goals and learning goals.","Getting an A in French class is a performance goal; being able to speak French is a learning goal.","Both goal types are entirely normal and pretty much universal, and both can fuel achievement.","Only one of the two goal types leads to mastery.","In Dweck's studies, giving children a performance goal was effective for relatively straightforward problems.","Performance goals often inhibited children's ability to apply the concepts to new situations.","In one study, junior high students given learning goals scored significantly higher when asked to apply scientific principles to new problems.","Students with learning goals also worked longer and tried more solutions.","Dweck writes that with a learning goal, students do not have to feel they are already good at something in order to hang in and keep trying.","Because their goal is to learn rather than prove they are smart, learning-goal students persist through difficulty."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix tests Dweck's distinction between performance goals, which demonstrate existing ability, and learning goals, which develop ability. Her junior high science studies showed that while both goal types fuel achievement, learning goals produce better transfer to novel problems and longer persistence, which is why only they lead to mastery.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each goal statement as a performance goal, a learning goal, or a feature that applies to both goal types according to Dweck.","column_a_label":"Performance goal","column_b_label":"Learning goal","column_c_label":"Both goal types","rows":[{"label":"Getting an A in French class.","col":"a"},{"label":"Being able to speak French.","col":"b"},{"label":"Effective for relatively straightforward problems.","col":"a"},{"label":"Helped junior high students apply learned scientific principles to new problems.","col":"b"},{"label":"Entirely normal, pretty much universal, and capable of fueling achievement.","col":"c"},{"label":"Students do not feel they must already be good at something to keep trying.","col":"b"},{"label":"Inhibited students' ability to apply concepts to new situations.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Performance goals are the only goals that can fuel achievement","Learning goals are abnormal and rarely universal among students","Performance goals lead more reliably to mastery than learning goals"],"explanation":"The matrix separates goals that prove ability from goals that build it. Performance goals, like getting an A, work on straightforward problems but inhibited transfer in Dweck's studies; learning goals, like learning to speak French, helped students apply knowledge to novel problems and keep working. The 'both' column captures Dweck's point that the two goal types are normal, universal, and able to fuel achievement, even though only learning goals lead to mastery."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The conditions of flow in game design","visual_frame_description":"A small indie game studio in Santa Monica, where Nina, a 27-year-old level designer, is absorbed in a surreal underwater scene: a glowing organism devours smaller creatures and slowly evolves. Teammates pass her desk all afternoon and she never looks up; the on-screen creature responds instantly to each tweak she makes. A producer's proposal document is about to land on her desk.","player_mission":"You are the studio's design lead. First, identify the conditions that explain why Nina is clearly in flow. Then, evaluate the producer's plan to 'professionalize' the team and decide whether to approve, revise, or scrap it before it kills the studio's engagement.","key_concepts":["In flow, goals are clear, such as reaching the top of the mountain or molding the clay just right.","In flow, feedback is immediate, so the ball sails in or out of bounds and the pot comes out smooth or uneven.","The most important flow condition is the perfect relationship between what a person had to do and what he could do.","When a challenge is too difficult relative to ability, the result is anxiety.","When a challenge is too easy relative to ability, the result is boredom.","Csikszentmihalyi titled his first book on autotelic experiences Beyond Boredom and Anxiety.","Work can have the structure of autotelic experiences: clear goals, immediate feedback, and challenges well matched to abilities.","Csikszentmihalyi found people are much more likely to reach flow at work than in leisure.","Jenova Chen designed the game flOw so players advance and explore any way they desire and failure pushes the player to a level better matched to her ability.","The free online version of flOw was played more than three million times, and the PlayStation version generated more than 350,000 downloads."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the recipe for flow: clear goals, immediate feedback, and a challenge calibrated just beyond current ability. Csikszentmihalyi's Experience Sampling Method research showed this match produces the deep engagement that the chapter calls central to mastery, and game designer Jenova Chen built flOw around exactly that calibration.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Flow arises when goals are clear, feedback is immediate, and the challenge sits a notch or two beyond the person's current abilities. The chapter applies these conditions to both game design and organizational work, because a challenge that exceeds capability produces anxiety and one that falls short produces boredom.","stage_1_observation":"Nina, a level designer at Comet Games, is playtesting her new underwater level in a quiet corner office. Her glowing organism must eat smaller creatures to evolve, and every mouse gesture produces an immediate visual reaction. She mutters 'the shell pattern is off,' re-tunes a value, watches the creature's behavior smooth out, and beams. Her teammates pass by for hours without breaking her concentration; her sense of time appears to have dissolved.","stage_1_question":"Which combination of conditions best explains why Nina is so obviously in flow?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Nina's work has the three flow ingredients: a clear goal (evolve the organism), immediate feedback (the creature reacts to every tweak), and tuning problems that sit just beyond her current skill, so the effort itself feels rewarding and her sense of time and self melts away.","stage_2_intervention":"The studio's new producer, Malik, announces a plan to 'professionalize' the team: everyone must design from the same fixed template, difficulty ratings must match a company-wide standard, and an hourly red-yellow-green dashboard will grade each designer's progress in real time. Before he sends the memo, decide what to do with the plan.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Scrap or heavily revise the plan, because a fixed template destroys the match between challenge and ability, and an hourly surveillance dashboard replaces immediate task feedback with external grading, which breeds anxiety or boredom rather than flow. Keep goals clear, feedback tied directly to the work, and difficulty individualized so each designer's challenge stays a notch beyond her ability, the way flOw pushed players to levels matched to their skill."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Raising a learning goal instead of a performance goal","visual_frame_description":"A kitchen in Omaha on a Saturday evening. Maya, a 24-year-old grad student, sits across from her sixteen-year-old brother Jordan, whose French II quiz with a C-minus written in red sits face-down on the table. Jordan's phone shows an open email draft to the school counselor requesting to drop the class. Warm light, a half-eaten dinner, and one conversation that could change the draft.","player_mission":"You are Maya. In one conversation, shift Jordan's reading of the failed quiz from a verdict on fixed ability to a guidepost for growth, and steer him toward a learning goal instead of a performance goal before he sends the drop request.","key_concepts":["What people believe shapes what they achieve; beliefs about the nature of ability can set boundaries on accomplishment.","An entity theory treats intelligence as a finite supply inside us that cannot be increased.","An incremental theory treats intelligence as something that, with effort, we can increase.","Getting an A in French class is a performance goal; being able to speak French is a learning goal.","Both performance and learning goals are entirely normal, pretty much universal, and both can fuel achievement.","Only the learning goal leads to mastery.","In Dweck's junior high science study, students with learning goals scored significantly higher when applying knowledge to new problems.","Students with learning goals worked longer and tried more solutions than performance-goal students.","With a learning goal, students do not have to feel they are already good at something in order to hang in and keep trying.","Students with an expansive mindset recognized that setbacks are inevitable on the road to mastery and can be guideposts for the journey."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests Dweck's goal framework under an incremental self-theory: a performance goal asks a student to prove ability, while a learning goal asks her to develop it, and only the learning goal produces persistence and transfer. The failed quiz becomes information about where practice should go instead of a verdict on fixed intelligence.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Jordan, sixteen, just got a C-minus on a French II quiz and announced he is 'just not a languages person' and wants to drop the class. You are his older sister Maya, home for the weekend. Jordan's self-talk is a textbook entity-theory response: a setback read as evidence of a fixed ceiling. You have one conversation to reshape how he reads the failure before he emails the counselor.","dialogue":["Jordan: I don't know why I try. The teacher said my accent is 'developing,' which is code for terrible.","Maya: Developing is literally the point. French is a skill you are building, not an asset you were issued at birth.","Jordan: Easy for you to say. You got A's in Spanish without trying.","Maya: I studied every morning for a year and still butchered the subjunctive. The grade came after the grind, not instead of it.","Jordan: So what do I do? Memorize the verb charts for the retest so I can get a B and look normal?","Maya: That is a performance goal: get the letter, prove you are not dumb. Here is a different one: by June, be able to hold a real conversation at a bakery counter in Paris.","Jordan: A conversation? That is way scarier than a quiz.","Maya: Scarier means it is the right size. A goal that asks you to grow is supposed to stretch you. The C is not a verdict; it is information about where the stretch should go.","Jordan: So retaking the quiz just to check the box is the trap.","Maya: Now you are seeing it. Want me to quiz you on the verb charts tonight, and after that we practice ordering a pain au chocolat?"],"choices":["Exactly. Let us replace 'get an A on the retest' with 'speak French well enough to order lunch in Paris by June' and start tonight with verb charts and an accent drill.","Do not be so hard on yourself, sweetie. You are smart, and I am sure you will get an A on the retest. I believe in you.","You are right that your ceiling may be lower than your classmates'. Let us just try to pass the class and get the credit over with.","Fine, drop the class. Mom and Dad will be upset, but if French makes you feel bad about yourself, protecting your self-image matters more."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Dweck's learning goals versus performance goals operating under an incremental self-theory.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 reframes the setback under an incremental theory: the C becomes guidepost information, and the goal shifts from proving ability to developing it, the pattern Dweck's junior high studies showed produced higher transfer scores and longer persistence. Choice 1 is a performance-goal pep talk that ties Jordan's worth to fixed smartness and pressures him toward easy successes. Choice 2 endorses the entity theory, telling a capable student she has hit her ceiling, which Dweck's data show produces helpless responses to difficulty. Choice 3 opts out of mastery entirely, treating self-image as something to protect instead of competence as something to build."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The three laws of mastery","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom-style evidence wall in a behavioral-science archive. On one wall hang six exhibits: Dweck's classroom data, a West Point summer training photo, a Cézanne landscape, a practice-session time log, a diagram of an asymptote curve approaching a horizontal line, and Tiger Woods at a practice range. The player drags each exhibit under one of three law placards.","player_mission":"Read each finding and sort it under the law of mastery it best supports: mastery is a mindset, mastery is a pain, or mastery is an asymptote. File all seven exhibits correctly on the evidence wall.","key_concepts":["Mastery abides by three laws: mastery is a mindset, mastery is a pain, and mastery is an asymptote.","Flow is essential to mastery but does not guarantee it, because flow happens in a moment while mastery unfolds over months, years, sometimes decades.","Dweck's research shows that beliefs about the nature of ability determine how we interpret experiences and can set boundaries on accomplishment.","Anders Ericsson's research holds that many characteristics once believed to reflect innate talent are actually the results of intense practice for a minimum of ten years.","At West Point, the best predictor of which cadets survived Beast Barracks was grit, defined as perseverance and passion for long-term goals.","Daniel Chambliss, after a three-year study of Olympic swimmers, called excellence's dependence on routine preparation 'the mundanity of excellence.'","The West Point researchers argued that in every field, grit may be as essential as talent to high accomplishment.","An asymptote is a straight line that a curve approaches but never quite reaches.","A critic wrote that for Cézanne, the synthesis of a design was an asymptote toward which he was forever approaching without ever quite reaching it.","Tiger Woods says he can and must become better, and he understands that mastery will always hover beyond his grasp; the joy is in the pursuit more than the realization."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests the chapter's three laws of mastery: mindset, pain, and asymptote. Pink argues that mastery requires a growth-oriented self-theory, grueling decade-scale practice, and acceptance that perfection recedes as we approach it.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each finding into the law of mastery it best supports: mastery is a mindset, mastery is a pain, or mastery is an asymptote.","column_a_label":"Mastery is a mindset","column_b_label":"Mastery is a pain","column_c_label":"Mastery is an asymptote","rows":[{"label":"Dweck: what students believe about intelligence predicted whether they persisted on problems they could not solve.","col":"a"},{"label":"West Point: grit, not intellect or athleticism, predicted which cadets survived Beast Barracks.","col":"b"},{"label":"Cézanne stalked the ultimate synthesis of a design his whole career without ever quite reaching it.","col":"c"},{"label":"Ericsson: traits once believed to reflect innate talent are often the results of intense practice for a minimum of ten years.","col":"b"},{"label":"Fifth- and sixth-graders who believed brainpower was fixed gave up quickly on hard problems and blamed their intelligence.","col":"a"},{"label":"Tiger Woods insists he must become better even as he knows mastery will always hover beyond his grasp.","col":"c"},{"label":"Chambliss: Olympic swimmers who did the best spent the most time on mundane race-readiness activities.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["The three laws operate on the same time horizon as flow itself","Mastery is fully attainable after ten years of consistent practice","Beliefs about ability have no measurable effect on achievement"],"explanation":"The matrix files each finding under one of the three laws. Mindset findings come from Dweck's prediction work and the fifth- and sixth-grader persistence study; pain findings come from Ericsson's ten-year practice research, the West Point grit results, and Chambliss's mundanity-of-excellence observation; asymptote findings come from Cézanne's endless approach to an unreachable synthesis and Tiger Woods's insistence that he can and must improve."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The mindset reset","visual_frame_description":"A Portland accounting firm's break room, where Marcus, a 34-year-old accountant, stares at a mock exam marked 61 percent. His colleague watches as Marcus re-reads his old notes and retakes the same practice exam rather than tackling unfamiliar problems. The failed analytics module is scheduled to be dropped from his plan next week.","player_mission":"You are Marcus's mentor. First, identify the self-theory and goal pattern that is derailing his study plan. Then, one week later, give him guidance grounded in Dweck's findings that can shift his mindset before he withdraws from the module.","key_concepts":["Dweck's signature insight is that what people believe shapes what they achieve.","Entity theorists believe intelligence exists within us in a finite supply that we cannot increase; incremental theorists believe it can increase with effort.","If you believe intelligence is a fixed quantity, every educational and professional encounter becomes a measure of how much you have.","If you believe intelligence can increase, the same encounters become opportunities for growth.","Entity theorists want to look like masters without expending the effort to attain mastery.","In Dweck's study, students who subscribed to fixed brain-power gave up quickly on tough problems and blamed their lack of intelligence.","Students with a more expansive mindset kept working, deployed far more inventive strategies, and did not blame anything for their difficulty.","The young people with an expansive mindset recognized that setbacks are inevitable on the road to mastery and can be guideposts for the journey.","With a learning goal, students do not have to feel they are already good at something in order to hang in and keep trying.","Dweck says effort is one of the things that gives meaning to life because it means you care about something and are willing to work for it."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests Dweck's entity-versus-incremental self-theory distinction: a fixed belief converts failure into evidence of incapacity, while a malleable belief converts failure into guidance. Her studies of fifth- and sixth-graders show the growth-oriented view produces persistence, inventive strategies, and mastery-oriented responses to adversity.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Mastery is a mindset. Dweck's self-theories show that believing intelligence is a fixed entity turns every setback into a verdict on your worth, while believing it can grow turns setbacks into guideposts, and the goal a person chooses follows from whichever belief he holds.","stage_1_observation":"Marcus, a 34-year-old accountant at a mid-sized firm in Portland, just scored 61 percent on the mock exam for the new data-analytics module of the CPA credential. In the break room he tells a colleague, 'I have never been good with software, and clearly I never will be. I would rather stick to what I am already good at.' His colleague notices that Marcus keeps re-reading his old notes and re-taking the same practice exam instead of solving unfamiliar problems.","stage_1_question":"What belief-and-goal pattern is holding Marcus back?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Marcus holds an entity theory of intelligence: he treats the module as a measure of a fixed ability he cannot increase. His behavior shows a shift toward performance goals that demand easy successes he can already hit, which is why he repeats familiar practice exams; Dweck's research shows this pattern converts the setback into proof of incapacity and drives helpless responses.","stage_2_intervention":"One week later Marcus tells you he is dropping the analytics module. Which guidance, grounded in Dweck's findings, should you give him to change course?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Recast the goal from performance to learning: reframe the module as a chance to develop a new capability, like building strength, treat the failed mock exam as a guidepost, and have him practice on problems just beyond his current level. Dweck's studies show learning-goal students hang in, try more solutions, and outscore performance-goal students on novel problems, so the module becomes an opportunity for growth instead of a verdict."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Grit at Beast Barracks","visual_frame_description":"Week six of Cadet Basic Training at West Point: a dusty parade ground at dusk, cadets in gray T-shirts running the same footwork drill for the fifth time. Cadet Rivera, a star athlete, slumps against a wall during a rest break; Cadet Osei, unremarkable on paper, quietly keeps drilling. Behind them, the long gray line of the Academy library rises against the Hudson River.","player_mission":"You serve on the Academy's retention research team. First, predict which cadet profile the West Point grit study says will persist, and why. Then, in week seven, recommend how to respond when the promising cadet wants to quit.","key_concepts":["Each summer about 1,200 young American men and women arrive at West Point, and before any classroom work they endure seven weeks of Cadet Basic Training known as Beast Barracks.","By the time the summer ends, one in twenty of these talented, dedicated young adults has dropped out.","The West Point study team included scholars from West Point, the University of Pennsylvania, and the University of Michigan.","The best predictor of cadet success was their rating on grit, defined as perseverance and passion for long-term goals.","Physical strength, intellect, leadership ability, and well-roundedness were not the predictors that distinguished those who stayed.","The same researchers found that grittiness, rather than IQ or standardized test scores, is the most accurate predictor of college grades.","The researchers wrote that working longer without switching objectives may be less perceptible than working harder, and that grit may be as essential as talent to high accomplishment.","Ericsson's research holds that many characteristics once believed to reflect innate talent are actually the results of intense practice for a minimum of ten years.","Daniel Chambliss's three-year study of Olympic swimmers found that the best spent the most time and effort on the mundane activities that readied them for races.","Moments of flow in the course of pursuing excellence can help people through the rough parts of mastery."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the second law of mastery, that mastery is a pain. The West Point study showed that grit, defined as perseverance and passion for long-term goals, predicted which cadets endured Beast Barracks better than strength, intellect, or leadership, and Ericsson's expert-performance research links achievement to years of effortful, often unglamorous practice.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Mastery is a pain. The path to becoming ever better at something you care about requires difficult, all-consuming effort over a long time, and the West Point evidence shows the trait that carries people through is grit: perseverance and passion for long-term goals.","stage_1_observation":"It is week six of Beast Barracks. Cadet Rivera, a gifted athlete with a 4.0 GPA, is wilting under the routine of marching drills, uniform inspections, and demerit paperwork. Cadet Osei, average on every physical and academic metric so far, wakes early, drills the same footwork nightly, and has never missed a study session. On the latest fitness test the two nearly match. The retention committee must predict who is more likely to be standing in the long gray line in four years.","stage_1_question":"Which cadet does the grit research predict will persist, and why?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Cadet Osei, because the best predictor of staying was not physical strength, intellect, leadership, or well-roundedness, but grit. Osei shows the perseverance and passion for long-term goals that keeps her working the same routine without switching objectives, the trait the same researchers found predicted college grades better than IQ or test scores.","stage_2_intervention":"Week seven: Cadet Rivera, facing her first demerit, says, 'Maybe the Army just is not for me.' The retention team wants your recommendation on how to respond, given that mastery hurts.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep Rivera oriented toward the long-term goal rather than the immediate setback: acknowledge that difficulty is normal on the path to mastery, since expert performance grows out of intense practice over a minimum of ten years. Help her identify which moments put her in flow so she knows what she is working toward, and set the expectation that progress comes from working longer without switching objectives, not from sudden talent."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The mastery asymptote","visual_frame_description":"A busy pastry kitchen in San Francisco the night of a regional competition. Elena, a 41-year-old pastry chef, stares at her winning mille-feuille on the pass counter while the trophy sits untouched beside it. In the corner of the frame, a math textbook diagram shows a curve approaching a horizontal asymptote that it never touches, and a Cézanne landscape hangs on the wall.","player_mission":"You are Elena's mentor. First, explain to her sous-chef why Elena's dissatisfaction is not a failure of appreciation. Then, recommend which of two career paths she should choose and what she should expect to feel along it.","key_concepts":["An asymptote is a straight line that a curve approaches but never quite reaches.","University of Chicago economist David Galenson, who studied the careers of artists, linked Cézanne's most enduring late paintings to his endless attempts to realize his best work.","A critic wrote that for Cézanne the ultimate synthesis of a design was never revealed in a flash but approached with infinite precautions, forever approaching without ever quite reaching it.","Mastery is impossible to realize fully: you can approach it, home in on it, and get really, really close, but never touch it.","Tiger Woods has said flatly that he can, and must, become better, whether as an amateur, after his best outing, or at the end of his finest season.","Woods understands that mastery will always hover beyond his grasp.","The mastery asymptote is a source of frustration because it asks why you would reach for something you can never fully attain.","The mastery asymptote is also a source of allure because the joy is in the pursuit more than the realization.","In the end, mastery attracts precisely because mastery eludes.","Robert Reich advises figuring out what you want to be really good at, knowing you will never really satisfy yourself that you have made it, and accepting that that is okay."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the third law of mastery, the asymptote: the goal recedes as you approach it, generating frustration and allure in equal measure. Pink grounds the law in Cézanne's endless stalking of an unreachable synthesis and Tiger Woods's insistence that he can and must improve even though mastery will always hover beyond his grasp.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Mastery is an asymptote: like a curve approaching a horizontal line, you can home in on mastery but never touch it. Cézanne stalked his ideal synthesis for decades without reaching it, and Tiger Woods knows his best will always hover beyond his grasp; the frustration and the allure come from the same unattainability.","stage_1_observation":"Elena, a 41-year-old pastry chef, has just won a regional competition for her signature mille-feuille. At the celebration she looks uneasy and tells her sous-chef, 'It is good, but I can already see the next version. The caramel needs one more degree of patience.' Her sous-chef is baffled: 'You just won first place. Why do you look like you lost?'","stage_1_question":"Is Elena's dissatisfaction a failure of appreciation or the signature of the mastery asymptote?","stage_1_correct_answer":"It is the mastery asymptote at work. Elena's joy is in the pursuit more than the realization, because mastery attracts precisely because mastery eludes. Cézanne approached his ultimate synthesis forever without quite reaching it, and Tiger Woods says he can and must become better even as he knows mastery will always hover beyond his grasp.","stage_2_intervention":"Elena's mentor offers two paths: cap her ambition and compete at a comfortable level where she always wins, or keep pushing toward a 'perfect' mille-feuille she concedes she will never wholly produce. Which path should she choose, and what should she expect to feel along the way?","stage_2_correct_answer":"She should choose the second path and accept the asymptote as both frustration and allure: find the joy in the pursuit rather than completion, and let the unattainable perfect version keep her engaged. That is the position Robert Reich describes: figure out what you want to be really good at, know you will never fully make it, and accept that that is okay."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Goldilocks tasks and the positive Sawyer Effect","visual_frame_description":"A hospital corridor where a cleaner in navy scrubs stops to chat with a recovering patient and then straightens a nurse's supply cart; a second panel shows an Ericsson manager in a one-on-one meeting, and a third shows a calibration dial labeled 'challenge' being tuned between 'boredom' and 'anxiety.' Tactic cards float onto the screen for sorting.","player_mission":"Classify each workplace tactic as a Goldilocks task, positive-Sawyer-Effect job sculpting, or broader flow-friendly structuring. Sort all seven tactic cards into the correct columns on the strategy board.","key_concepts":["Organizations that increase flow-friendliness typically use Goldilocks tasks: challenges that are neither too hot nor too cold, neither overly difficult nor overly simple.","A frequent source of workplace frustration is the mismatch between what people must do and what people can do.","When what people must do exceeds their capabilities, the result is anxiety.","When what people must do falls short of their capabilities, the result is boredom.","Csikszentmihalyi titled his first book on autotelic experiences Beyond Boredom and Anxiety.","The positive side of the Sawyer Effect runs the current in the other direction, turning work into play.","Amy Wrzesniewski and Jane Dutton studied hospital cleaners, nurses, and hairdressers, finding that some cleaners took on tasks such as chatting with patients and helping nurses' jobs go more smoothly.","Adding more absorbing challenges increased the cleaners' satisfaction and boosted their own views of their skills.","Wrzesniewski and Dutton write that even in low-autonomy jobs, employees can create new domains for mastery.","A study of 11,000 industrial scientists and engineers in the United States found that the desire for intellectual challenge was the best predictor of productivity."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This matrix tests two organizational tactics the chapter endorses: Goldilocks tasks that match challenge to capability, and the positive Sawyer Effect of sculpting jobs to bring flow into mundane duties. The chapter's evidence, including Wrzesniewski and Dutton's studies and the 11,000-scientist study, links both tactics to flow, productivity, and mastery.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each workplace tactic as a Goldilocks task, positive-Sawyer-Effect job sculpting, or broader flow-friendly structuring of work.","column_a_label":"Goldilocks tasks","column_b_label":"Positive Sawyer Effect","column_c_label":"Flow-friendly structuring","rows":[{"label":"A task that is neither too hot nor too cold, neither overly difficult nor overly simple.","col":"a"},{"label":"A hospital cleaner takes on chatting with patients and helping nurses' jobs go more smoothly.","col":"b"},{"label":"Managers configure work assignments so employees have clear objectives and a way to get quick feedback.","col":"c"},{"label":"Teams adjust assignments so that what people must do matches what they can do.","col":"a"},{"label":"Hairdressers reframe aspects of their duties to make work more playful and more fully their own.","col":"b"},{"label":"Managers meet staff monthly to sense who is overwhelmed and who is underwhelmed, then adjust assignments.","col":"c"},{"label":"Even in low-autonomy jobs, employees create new domains for mastery.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["Goldilocks tasks require challenges far beyond current capability to stretch employees","Job sculpting is only possible in high-autonomy positions","Revamping work assignments has no measurable effect on company profitability"],"explanation":"The matrix separates the chapter's two named tactics plus broader structuring. Goldilocks tactics calibrate the difficulty of a task so it neither exceeds nor falls short of capability; positive-Sawyer-Effect tactics let employees sculpt mundane duties, as the hospital cleaners did when they added absorbing challenges; and flow-friendly structuring refers to systemic design, such as Ericsson's clear objectives with quick feedback and Green Cargo's monthly overwhelmed-underwhelmed check-ins."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Forty-eight hours without flow","visual_frame_description":"A 1970s psychology laboratory at the University of Chicago: participants sit in a drab waiting room filling out Experience Sampling Method booklets. One man rubs a headache beneath fluorescent lights, a woman stares at the clock, and a handwritten sign on the door reads 'No Play Allowed.' A DSM-IV symptom checklist sits beside the researcher's notebook.","player_mission":"You are the lab's attending researcher. First, diagnose what is happening to the participants of Csikszentmihalyi's two-day experiment. Then, propose a workplace package that prevents the same danger signs in employees.","key_concepts":["Generalized anxiety disorder is a mental illness that afflicts roughly 3 percent of the adult population.","According to the DSM-IV, the presence of any three of six symptoms, such as restlessness, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance, indicates a potentially serious problem.","In the early 1970s Csikszentmihalyi asked people to record all the noninstrumental things they did, small activities undertaken not out of obligation but because they enjoyed them.","He then instructed participants to act in a normal way while doing nothing that was play or noninstrumental.","One woman enjoyed washing dishes because it gave her something constructive to do and time to fantasize free of guilt, but she could wash dishes only when absolutely necessary.","Even at the end of the first day, participants noticed an increased sluggishness about their behavior.","Participants complained of headaches, difficulty concentrating, and thoughts that wandered round in circles without getting anywhere.","Some felt sleepy while others were too agitated to sleep.","After just two days of deprivation, the general deterioration in mood was so advanced that prolonging the experiment would have been unadvisable.","Flow is not a nicety; it is a necessity, the oxygen of the soul."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests the chapter's 'oxygen of the soul' experiment: scrubbing every play-like, noninstrumental activity from daily life produced headaches, irritability, concentration trouble, and sleep disruption within two days, a mood profile resembling generalized anxiety disorder. The finding supports Pink's claim that flow is a psychological necessity rather than a luxury.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"Flow is the oxygen of the soul. In Csikszentmihalyi's early-1970s experiment, removing all 'play' and noninstrumental activity produced within two days a deterioration in mood eerily similar to a serious psychiatric disorder, showing that engagement is a necessity for psychological survival.","stage_1_observation":"It is day two of an experiment in a psychology lab in Chicago in the early 1970s. Participants were told to act normally but to do nothing that is play or noninstrumental. In their booklets, one writes 'Slept badly, listless, more nervous, more guarded'; another reports feeling 'tense, more hostile, angry, and irritated'; a third notes difficulty concentrating, with thoughts that wander round in circles without getting anywhere. A woman who enjoyed washing dishes has been told she may wash only when absolutely necessary. The study team consults the DSM-IV checklist.","stage_1_question":"What is happening to these participants, and why?","stage_1_correct_answer":"They are suffering flow deprivation, not generalized anxiety disorder. Measured against the DSM-IV, people can show three or more symptoms such as irritability, difficulty concentrating, sleep disturbance, and fatigue, but these healthy participants were stripped of all play-like activity, and after just two days the general deterioration in mood was so advanced that prolonging the experiment would have been unadvisable.","stage_2_intervention":"The lab director asks a business leader to observe the results and propose how a workplace could avoid producing the same danger signs. Which package of changes, grounded in the chapter, should you propose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Restore the conditions of flow that the experiment removed: offer Goldilocks tasks matched to people's abilities, let employees sculpt mundane duties through the positive Sawyer Effect, and build flow-friendly practices such as clear objectives, quick feedback, and regular check-ins on whether people are overwhelmed or underwhelmed, as Ericsson and Green Cargo did."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Building engagement through flow-friendly check-ins","visual_frame_description":"A small meeting room at Nordwind Logistics in Gothenburg, Sweden, where Priya, a 29-year-old customs-documentation specialist, sits across from her manager at a table holding two coffee cups and a scheduling calendar. A wall of green shipping containers glows through the window. The monthly one-on-one is on the agenda.","player_mission":"You are Priya's manager at Nordwind Logistics, a Swedish freight company trying to break a 125-year losing streak by rebuilding work around flow. Conduct the monthly one-on-one so that you discover whether Priya is overwhelmed or underwhelmed, and adjust her assignments toward the challenge-ability match.","key_concepts":["A frequent source of frustration in the workplace is the mismatch between what people must do and what people can do.","When what people must do falls short of their capabilities, the result is boredom.","When what people must do exceeds their capabilities, the result is anxiety.","Goldilocks tasks are challenges that are neither overly difficult nor overly simple.","Fast Company has noted that companies including Microsoft, Patagonia, and Toyota have created flow-friendly environments that help people move toward mastery.","At Ericsson, Stefan Falk persuaded managers to configure work assignments so employees had clear objectives and a way to get quick feedback.","Falk replaced once-a-year performance reviews with one-on-one meetings six times a year, often as long as ninety minutes, to discuss level of engagement and path toward mastery.","At Green Cargo, managers met staff once a month to get a sense of whether people were overwhelmed or underwhelmed and adjusted assignments to help them find flow.","After two years of managerial revamping, state-owned Green Cargo became profitable for the first time in 125 years, with executives citing flowcentricity as a key reason.","When work has the structure of autotelic experiences, clear goals, immediate feedback, and well-matched challenges, we do not just enjoy it more, we do it better."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue simulates the flow-calibrated check-in that Stefan Falk ran at Ericsson and Green Cargo: the manager probes whether an employee is overwhelmed or underwhelmed, then adjusts assignments toward the Goldilocks zone. The mechanism is flow's core condition, which the chapter ties to engagement and mastery rather than compliance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"You are Priya's manager at Nordwind Logistics, a Swedish freight company whose leadership is replacing 125 years of losses with a flow-based management overhaul. Monthly one-on-ones now replace annual reviews, and leadership has asked you to sense whether people are overwhelmed or underwhelmed and adjust assignments so they can find flow. Priya, a perceptive 29-year-old customs-documentation specialist, has been watched the clock for weeks, and this meeting is where the change happens or stalls.","dialogue":["Priya: I am not sure how to say this, but I am honestly bored most days. The customs forms are the same fifteen fields, over and over. I finish my batch by noon and then I am just watching the clock.","You: That is exactly what this meeting is for. Walk me through a normal week.","Priya: I file, I check the registry, I file some more. Nothing asks anything of me. When my colleague Anders retired, his exception queue, the tricky shipments that need human judgment, just sat there waiting for the system to time out.","You: The exception queue is technically mine to assign. If I moved the hard cases to you, what would that change?","Priya: Honestly, that is the work I actually like. Nobody has looked at it since Anders left, so I would be figuring it out from scratch. It is a puzzle. It might take me a while, and I would probably need someone to tell me when I am getting it right.","You: So the goal would be clear, the feedback would be immediate, and the challenge would sit slightly beyond your current skill. That is the profile of work people get lost in.","Priya: I want that. I want to get genuinely good at this, even if it is ugly in the middle.","You: Then let us set it up. The exception queue is yours weekly, and every Friday we review what you shipped, what stumped you, and what you figured out, and we tune the difficulty as you improve."],"choices":["I hear you, but leadership needs throughput. I will give you the exception queue only if you promise to keep your error rate under half a percent, tracked daily on the dashboard.","Agreed. The exception queue is yours weekly, and every Friday we review what you shipped, what stumped you, and what you figured out, tuning the difficulty as you improve.","Boredom is part of any job, Priya. Let us focus on making the routine forms 100 percent accurate, and I will note your patience in your annual review.","I will put you on a special project team for a month. It is more interesting, but it means working nights, and I cannot promise the arrangement will stick."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Goldilocks tasks and flow-friendly management as practiced by Stefan Falk at Ericsson and Green Cargo.","psychological_breakdown":"The optimal choice converts Priya's work into a properly scaled challenge with a clear goal, quick feedback, and difficulty calibrated just beyond her current ability, plus regular recalibration check-ins, which mirrors Falk's structure at Ericsson and Green Cargo. Choice 0 adds a performance-goal dashboard that turns the work into a compliance test, which the chapter links to anxiety rather than flow. Choice 2 denies the problem and preserves the boredom-inducing mismatch between what Priya must do and what she can do. Choice 3 trades engagement for overtime and treats mastery as a temporary favor instead of a sustained path."}]},{"id":"ch11","title":"CHAPTER 6 - Purpose","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Purpose Maximization vs. Profit Maximization vs. Checklist Ethics","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen sorting board glows in three columns, each labeled with one operating system or approach: purpose maximization, profit maximization, and checklist compliance. Small orange evidence tiles — a shoe icon for TOMS, a stat card reading 800 million, a gavel for compliance rulings — hover as the player drags each one into its column.","player_mission":"Read each evidence tile about real companies, pledges, and policies, then drag it into the column it best matches. Place at least 5 of 6 tiles correctly to pass the audit screen.","key_concepts":["Motivation 3.0 does not reject profits but places equal emphasis on purpose maximization.","TOMS Shoes describes itself as a for-profit company with giving at its core, launched by Blake Mycoskie in 2006.","Motivation 2.0 relegates purpose to the status of an ornament that must not get in the way of profits.","Gary Hamel argues that wealth maximization lacks the power to fully mobilize human energies.","Worldwide co-op membership doubled to 800 million people in the last three decades, per writer Marjorie Kelly.","Purpose provides activation energy for living, according to psychologist Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi.","The MBA Oath is a voluntary code of conduct pledging managers to serve the greater good.","Ethics guidelines can unintentionally move purposeful behavior from Type I to Type X when reduced to checklists.","Max Bazerman observed that weak ethical standards turn doing the right thing into checking off boxes."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests self-determination theory's claim that purpose is a basic source of activation: people perform and persist best when their goals serve a cause larger than themselves. Pink's evidence is the divergence between falling compensated engagement and rising volunteerism, showing purpose nourishes people in ways pay alone does not.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each organizational impulse below. Specifically, where does the TOMS Shoes model — a for-profit company with giving at its core — belong?","column_a_label":"Purpose maximization (Motivation 3.0)","column_b_label":"Profit maximization (Motivation 2.0)","column_c_label":"Checklist compliance (Type X ethics policies)","rows":[{"label":"TOMS Shoes sells a pair and gives a pair away, calling itself a for-profit company with giving at its core","col":"a"},{"label":"Gary Hamel: as an emotional catalyst, wealth maximization lacks the power to fully mobilize human energies","col":"b"},{"label":"A firm reduces affirmative action to a list of requirements to prove it isn't discriminating","col":"c"},{"label":"The MBA Oath pledges managers to serve the greater good rather than only the bottom line","col":"a"},{"label":"Worldwide co-op membership doubled to 800 million people over roughly three decades","col":"a"},{"label":"Bazerman's warning: a weak set of ethical standards turns doing the right thing into checking off boxes","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b — TOMS is simply a charity that finances its operations through shoe sales","c — TOMS must meet ethics checklists so it won't get sued or fined","a — but only because TOMS chases profit and treats giving as a marketing ornament"],"explanation":"TOMS is neither a pure charity nor a profit-first business: Pink describes it as a for-profit company with giving at its core, the hallmark of purpose maximization, where purpose is the objective and profit is the catalyst. Checklist ethics sorts to column C because it shifts workers from intrinsic motivation to box-checking for legal safety, and Hamel's remark anchors the Motivation 2.0 view that wealth maximization cannot mobilize human energies."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Good Life: Purpose Goals vs. Profit Goals at Rochester","visual_frame_description":"A college quad at sunset with a graduation cap floating above the gates. Two dossier cards labeled Profit Goals and Purpose Goals unfold on screen, each with a happiness meter that slides in opposite directions as the player inspects the follow-up data.","player_mission":"Classify each graduate aspiration as intrinsic, extrinsic, or neutral, then identify which goal profile the Rochester study found to increase anxiety and depression even when attained.","key_concepts":["Deci, Ryan, and Niemiec surveyed Rochester seniors about their life goals and followed up one to two years into their careers.","Extrinsic aspirations include becoming wealthy or achieving fame; Pink calls them profit goals.","Intrinsic aspirations include helping others improve their lives, learning, and growing; Pink calls them purpose goals.","Purpose-goal attainers reported higher satisfaction and subjective well-being than when they were in college.","Profit-goal attainers reported satisfaction, self-esteem, and positive affect no higher than when they were students.","Profit-goal attainers showed increases in anxiety, depression, and other negative indicators.","The researchers concluded that attainment of profit goals has no impact on well-being and actually contributes to ill-being.","Richard Ryan noted that profit-goal high attainers are busy making money, leaving less room for love, attention, caring, and empathy.","Satisfaction depends not merely on having goals, but on having the right goals."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests self-determination theory's distinction between extrinsic and intrinsic aspirations: attaining material goals fails to lift well-being and can worsen it. The longitudinal Rochester study found purpose-goal attainers happier and less anxious while profit-goal attainers were worse off despite reaching their goals.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Deci, Ryan, and Niemiec tracked University of Rochester graduates one to two years into their careers. Which aspiration profile showed rises in anxiety and depression even while attaining its goals?","column_a_label":"Intrinsic aspirations (purpose goals)","column_b_label":"Extrinsic aspirations (profit goals)","column_c_label":"Neutral aims (neither category)","rows":[{"label":"A graduate wants to help others improve their lives","col":"a"},{"label":"A graduate wants to become wealthy","col":"b"},{"label":"A graduate wants to achieve fame","col":"b"},{"label":"Attainers of these goals reported satisfaction no higher than when they were students","col":"b"},{"label":"Attainers reported higher subjective well-being and quite low anxiety and depression","col":"a"},{"label":"A graduate wants to learn and grow","col":"a"},{"label":"Attainers showed increases in anxiety and depression even while attaining their goals","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["a — purpose goals raised anxiety and depression in the follow-up data","c — both profiles showed identical well-being after attainment","b — but only among students who failed to attain their goals"],"explanation":"The Rochester follow-up found that graduates with extrinsic aspirations (wealth, fame) who attained their goals reported satisfaction, self-esteem, and positive affect no higher than in college — and showed more anxiety and depression, so attaining profit goals contributed to ill-being. Purpose-goal attainers, by contrast, reported higher well-being and low anxiety, which is why those rows sort to column A and the target row to column B."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Three Realms of the Purpose Motive: Goals, Words, and Policies","visual_frame_description":"A three-chambered museum exhibit: Goal Hall shows shoe boxes multiplying into donations, Word Hall shows speech bubbles morphing into honest dialogue, and Policy Hall shows a wall calendar with one glowing day per week reserved for meaningful work.","player_mission":"Sort each example from the chapter into the realm where the purpose motive stirs first — goals, words, or policies — and pinpoint exactly where the Mayo Clinic experiment belongs.","key_concepts":["The purpose motive first appears in three realms of organizational life: goals, words, and policies.","Physicians at the Mayo Clinic who spent one day a week on the aspect of their job most meaningful to them had half the burnout rate of those who did not.","Dunn and Norton propose shifting part of a company's charitable-giving budget so individual employees donate chosen sums.","Pro-social spending — spending money on other people or on a cause — can increase subjective well-being.","Sylvia Hewlett found that boomers and Gen Y are redefining success and accepting a radically remixed set of rewards.","Neither boomers nor Gen Y rate money as the most important form of compensation.","Robert Reich's pronoun test gauges organization health by whether employees refer to the company as we or they.","Gary Hamel says goals described as efficiency and advantage lack the power to rouse human hearts.","We companies and they companies are very different places, and in Motivation 3.0 we wins.","The MBA Oath urges managers to create value that no single individual can create alone."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario maps the purpose motive onto the three realms Pink identifies — goals, words, and policies — testing whether the learner can place the Mayo Clinic trial and pro-social giving in the policy realm rather than the goals realm. The evidence base spans Hewlett's generational research, pro-social spending studies, and the halved burnout finding at Mayo.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Pink maps the early stirrings of the purpose motive onto three realms of organizational life. Where does the Mayo Clinic experiment — letting doctors spend one day a week on their most meaningful work — belong?","column_a_label":"Goals — remixed rewards and purpose maximizers","column_b_label":"Words — the purpose dialect in corporate language","column_c_label":"Policies — autonomy enlisted in service of purpose","rows":[{"label":"TOMS Shoes' give-one-for-one business model","col":"a"},{"label":"The MBA Oath's opening pledge to serve the greater good","col":"b"},{"label":"The Mayo Clinic trial letting doctors spend one day a week on the most meaningful part of the job","col":"c"},{"label":"Dunn and Norton's proposal that employees be given sums to donate to charities they choose","col":"c"},{"label":"Sylvia Hewlett's finding that Gen Y wants a great team and the ability to give back to society","col":"a"},{"label":"Hamel's call to infuse mundane business activities with honor, truth, love, justice, and beauty","col":"b"},{"label":"Robert Reich's pronoun test — listening for we versus they","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"c","distractors":["a — it is a goals shift like the TOMS model","b — it is a words shift like the MBA Oath","c — but only for physicians, not as a model other organizations could adopt"],"explanation":"The Mayo Clinic field trial is a policy: deliberately restructuring work time so physicians spend a day a week on the aspect of the job most meaningful to them, cutting burnout in half — Pink calls it 20 percent time with a purpose. TOMS and the Gen Y remixed rewards belong to the goals realm, while the MBA Oath, Hamel's vocabulary, and Reich's pronoun test belong to the words realm."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Purpose Motive and the Type I Tripod","visual_frame_description":"A boardroom with two glowing pitch decks on opposite walls: one covered in bar charts and margin curves, the other showing a map of small towns dotted with building icons. Warren, the sixty-two-year-old CEO, leans back in a leather chair while a third screen shows a slogan factory stamping words.","player_mission":"Choose which corporate plan actually carries the purpose motive, then defend that choice by redesigning the incentives and language so profit talk does not crowd purpose out of the firm.","boss_battle_explanation":"Purpose is the third leg of the Type I tripod: autonomy and mastery are essential, but purpose provides the context that lets the other two legs accomplish more. Autonomous people working toward mastery perform at very high levels, and those who do so in the service of some greater objective can achieve even more; Motivation 3.0 pursues purpose maximization while Motivation 2.0 treated purpose as an ornament.","stage_1_observation":"You are a strategy consultant at a retreat for Harlow & Reed, a mid-sized engineering firm. Two teams pitch the next decade. Team Argus presents a financial plan to expand market share and lift margins by 12 percent, citing efficiency, advantage, and differentiation. Team Meridian presents a plan to anchor the firm's work in rebuilding public infrastructure for underserved towns, promising steady profit as a by-product. The CEO, Warren, says he is too old to chase butterflies.","stage_1_question":"Which plan best embodies what Pink calls the purpose motive?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Team Meridian's plan — it makes purpose the objective and treats profit as the catalyst or happy by-product of attaining it, exactly the Motivation 3.0 stance in which purpose maximization, not profit maximization, supplies the activation energy.","stage_2_intervention":"The board accepts Meridian's pitch, but CFO Priya wants to incentivize the engineers with a 20-percent bonus pool tied to quarterly profit targets and a slogan wall reading Efficiency is honor. You have one edit to the implementation. What do you change?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Drop the profit-tied bonus and the slogan — profit-contingent if-then incentives and efficiency language push purpose back to ornament status and cannot mobilize human energies; instead, keep pay fair and out of the way and let engineers spend one day a week on the community projects, following the Mayo Clinic model of 20 percent time with a purpose.","key_concepts":["Purpose is the third leg of the Type I tripod, providing context for autonomy and mastery.","Autonomous people working toward mastery perform at very high levels.","Those who work toward mastery in the service of a greater objective can achieve even more.","Motivation 2.0 relegated purpose to the status of an ornament.","Motivation 3.0 places equal emphasis on purpose maximization, without rejecting profits.","Gary Hamel: wealth maximization lacks the power to fully mobilize human energies.","The most deeply motivated people hitch their desires to a cause larger than themselves.","At the Mayo Clinic, one day a week on meaningful work halved physician burnout.","Compensated engagement is falling while volunteerism is rising in the United States.","Volunteer work is nourishing people in ways that paid work simply does not."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"The battle tests self-determination theory's claim that purpose, alongside autonomy and mastery, is a core need that supplies activation energy for high performance. Pink's evidence is the boomers' demographic surge of purpose-seeking, rising volunteerism, and the Mayo Clinic burnout finding — though the broad social sweep is his synthesis rather than a single controlled experiment.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"TOMS Shoes and the Rise of Purpose Maximizers","visual_frame_description":"A sunlit storefront where canvas flat-soled shoes are stacked on crates beside a world map dotted with delivery pins. A scale floats above the counter, one pan holding a new shoe and the other holding a child's sneaker, balanced perfectly.","player_mission":"Diagnose what kind of enterprise TOMS is in Pink's framework, then defend its giving-at-the-core model against a private-equity deal that would downgrade giving to an ornament.","boss_battle_explanation":"Purpose maximizers are operations that make purpose the goal and profit the catalyst rather than the objective: for-profit companies with giving at their core, B corporations, and cooperatives. Pink argues such ventures are so incompatible with Motivation 2.0 logic that they would seize up and crash under that operating system.","stage_1_observation":"Step into a bright storefront in the summer of 2006. Blake Mycoskie has just launched TOMS Shoes: hip, canvas, flat-soled shoes on display beside a wall chart showing that every purchase ships a second pair to a child in a developing country. A shopper asks the clerk: Is this a charity that sells shoes, or a business that gives shoes away?","stage_1_question":"How does Pink classify ventures like TOMS?","stage_1_correct_answer":"TOMS is neither simply a charity nor a conventional profit-maximizing business — the company itself answers that it is both and describes itself as a for-profit company with giving at its core, a purpose maximizer where purpose is the goal and profit is the catalyst.","stage_2_intervention":"Two years later, a private-equity investor offers Mycoskie a deal: double margins by dropping the give-one model to one pair per ten sold and rebrand the donation as an annual charity event. He gives you one recommendation to deliver.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Turn the offer down — cutting giving into a box-ticking annual event turns purpose into an ornament, exactly the move that would flatten TOMS into the socially responsible but rarely delivering template Pink criticizes; the venture's identity depends on purpose remaining at the core of every sale.","key_concepts":["Blake Mycoskie launched TOMS Shoes in 2006.","Every time TOMS sells a pair of shoes, it gives away another pair to a child in a developing country.","TOMS describes itself as a for-profit company with giving at its core.","TOMS is neither a charity that finances itself with shoe sales nor a business that sacrifices earnings to do good — it is both.","Purpose maximizers pursue purpose and use profit as the catalyst rather than the objective.","B corporations, for-benefit organizations, and low-profit LLCs recast the goals of the traditional business enterprise.","Worldwide co-op membership doubled to 800 million people over three decades.","Not-only-for-profit enterprises differ from the socially responsible businesses that have rarely delivered on their promise.","Under Motivation 2.0 logic, a venture like TOMS would seize up and crash."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the purpose motive: organizations that put giving at the core of the business model rather than as a side program. Pink's evidence is TOMS, the surge of co-ops and benefit corporations, and the demographic shift of boomers and Gen Y toward remixed, nonmonetary rewards — a popular-science synthesis of emerging business trends.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Checklist Ethics vs. Autonomy in Service of Purpose","visual_frame_description":"A desk with a laminated 40-item ethics checklist beside a glass jar overflowing with folded donation cards and a small sign reading Your 200 dollars, your pick. Overhead, a graph splits into a red falling line labeled intrinsic and a green rising line labeled satisfaction.","player_mission":"Predict the motivational side effect of a compliance checklist, then pick the single policy that converts the credit union from box-checking back to purpose-driven giving.","boss_battle_explanation":"Corporate ethics guidelines, however well-intentioned, can backfire: by giving people a fairly weak set of standards to meet, they replace intrinsic motivation — do it because it's right — with extrinsic box-checking — make sure we don't get sued or fined. Bazerman's warning is that compliance can crowd out purpose, and the better path is enlisting autonomy in the service of purpose maximization.","stage_1_observation":"At Verano Credit Union, a loan officer scandal has just hit the local news. General counsel Denise drafts a 40-item ethics checklist, mandating quarterly sign-offs and compliance training for every employee. You watch Dana, a conscientious loan officer who once refused a bonus for pushing a risky product, spend her Friday reading the checklist aloud and asking peers whether each item counts.","stage_1_question":"What does Max Bazerman's analysis predict about Dana's motivation after the checklist arrives?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Her motivation shifts from intrinsic to extrinsic: instead of doing the right thing because it is right, she now meets the minimal ethical standard to avoid punishment, because the guidelines handed her an alternate set of standards — do this so you can check off all these boxes.","stage_2_intervention":"Denise wants to restore the credit union's moral energy and asks you to pick one lever: (a) double the checklist items and add fines for missed sign-offs; (b) give each employee a 200-dollar budget each quarter to donate to any cause they choose; (c) pay a compliance bonus for perfect sign-off records. Which lever do you choose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Choose the donation budget — Dunn and Norton's pro-social spending research shows that handing employees control over how the organization gives back improves their satisfaction, which is exactly the model of enlisting autonomy in the service of purpose instead of adding another layer of extrinsic control.","key_concepts":["Corporate ethics guidelines can unintentionally move purposeful behavior out of the Type I schema and into Type X.","Bazerman: giving people a weak set of ethical standards replaces doing it because it's right with checking off all these boxes.","Checklist ethics can turn affirmative action into a set of requirements to show the organization isn't discriminating.","Workers may meet the minimal ethical standards to avoid punishment while the guidelines inject no purpose.","The better approach is to enlist the power of autonomy in the service of purpose maximization.","The correlation between money and happiness is weak past a certain, quite modest level.","Pro-social spending — on other people or on a cause — can increase subjective well-being.","Dunn and Norton propose giving employees sums to donate to the charities of their choice.","Handing employees control over corporate giving may do more for satisfaction than another if-then financial incentive.","Instances of unethical behavior have not declined despite years of crafting corporate ethics guidelines."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests self-determination theory's crowding-out effect: extrinsic standards displace intrinsic moral motivation, a mechanism Bazerman describes in the book. The corrective per Pink is autonomy in service of purpose, supported by Aknin, Dunn, and Norton's pro-social spending findings that giving employees control over charitable dollars raises well-being.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"What Happens When You Attain Profit Goals","visual_frame_description":"A quiet coaching session in a glass-walled office at an accounting firm: Marcus, twenty-six, sits across from Priya the leadership coach while Sana, a nonprofit auditor, leans in from a side chair. A subtle on-screen graph shows two happiness curves crossing in opposite directions as the conversation unfolds.","player_mission":"Coach Marcus through the paradox of attaining profit goals without gaining happiness — use the Rochester study and Ryan's explanation to redirect his ambition toward purpose goals without abandoning his ambition.","scenario_setup":"You are Priya, a leadership coach at a Big Four accounting firm. Twenty-six-year-old senior analyst Marcus just made partner track with a six-figure raise and booked a session to figure out why he feels hollow. Across the room, his colleague Sana, who took a lower-paying role at a nonprofit audit shop, reports feeling more alive than ever. Your goal is to help Marcus read his own results with self-determination theory — and keep him from doubling down on the wrong goal.","dialogue":["Marcus: I hit the numbers. One-eighty hours of billable work this month, top-bracket bonus, name on the partner list. And I feel... nothing. What is wrong with me?","Priya: Nothing is wrong with you. A University of Rochester study followed graduates one to two years into their careers — people who attained their wealth and fame goals weren't any happier than they were in college.","Marcus: So I should make more money? Maybe the goal was just too small.","Sana: I make half of what you do, Marcus. But I audited a school district's books and they got laptops for every classroom. I sleep better.","Priya: That's the striking part of the research — profit-goal attainers showed increases in anxiety and depression, even while winning. Richard Ryan's explanation: they're busy making money and attending to themselves, leaving less room for love, attention, caring, and the things that truly count.","Marcus: So what do I put in the goals column instead? I can't exactly write feel good on a performance review.","Priya: Stay ambitious — but hitch the ambition to purpose goals: helping others, learning, growing. The study found purpose-goal attainers reported higher satisfaction and lower anxiety than in college.","Sana: Try this: before your next raise conversation, ask what a cause larger than yourself would have you build. Then let that sentence steer the work."],"choices":["Tell Marcus to keep the money but redirect his drive: keep one day a week for meaningful work, and set a purpose goal alongside the profit goal.","Agree that the goal was too small and advise doubling the targets — more money and a bigger title will finally scratch the itch.","Suggest Marcus quietly transfer to Sana's nonprofit firm so he can escape profit goals entirely.","Advise Marcus to separate his feelings from his career — feelings are unreliable, but the bonus is real."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Intrinsic versus extrinsic aspirations (Deci, Ryan, and Niemiec)","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 works because it keeps the attained profit goal harmless while adding intrinsic aspirations: the Rochester follow-up found purpose-goal attainers reported higher satisfaction and lower anxiety, while profit-goal attainers were worse off even when successful. Choice 1 is the trap Ryan describes — expanding the goal that is already failing, a road of further unhappiness mistaken for the road to happiness. Choice 2 abandons the autonomy Marcus clearly values in his work, and choice 3 discounts real subjective evidence, which is exactly how high attainers wind up with the ill-being the research documented.","key_concepts":["Deci and Ryan's Rochester follow-up tracked graduates one to two years after college.","Extrinsic aspirations seek wealth and fame; Pink calls these profit goals.","Intrinsic aspirations seek to help others, learn, and grow; Pink calls these purpose goals.","Attaining profit goals did not raise satisfaction, self-esteem, or positive affect.","Profit-goal attainers showed increases in anxiety, depression, and other negative indicators.","Purpose-goal attainers reported higher subjective well-being and low anxiety and depression.","Richard Ryan: high profit-goal attainers are busy making money, leaving less room for love, attention, caring, and empathy.","Satisfaction depends on having the right goals, not merely on having goals.","Chasing more money after attaining profit goals can drive people down a road of further unhappiness."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests self-determination theory's aspiration distinction: attaining extrinsic goals fails to improve well-being and can worsen it, while intrinsic aspirations support it. The Rochester follow-up and Ryan's empathy explanation ground the coaching advice the player must deliver.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Words and Policies: Keeping Volunteerism Alive","visual_frame_description":"An executive office at Pillar Health where CEO Greta gestures at a chart of volunteer hours climbing steeply; Malik, the chief operating officer, holds a clipboard with the word mandate crossed out. A window shows employees heading out to a community garden on a Saturday.","player_mission":"Steer the CEO away from a mandatory volunteer program with sign-in sheets and promotion credit, and toward a purpose-preserving design that keeps volunteerism intrinsically motivated.","scenario_setup":"You are Malik, chief operating officer of Pillar Health, a 300-person insurance company. Employee volunteer participation has tripled this year — uncompensated effort is rising even as paid engagement surveys fall. CEO Greta sees an opportunity to formalize it: she wants a mandated four-hour monthly Community Service Block with sign-in sheets, credit toward promotion, and a compliance officer. Your mission is to steer the conversation so the policy does not extinguish the very intrinsic flame it aims to fan.","dialogue":["Greta: Malik, great news — volunteerism is through the roof. Let's institutionalize it: four mandatory hours a month, sign-in sheets, and it counts toward your promotion rubric.","Malik: Slow down. The rising volunteer line in our data is nourishing people precisely because paid work isn't. If we make it an obligation, we hand people an alternate set of standards to check off.","Greta: But how do we scale good deeds without structure? Where's the accountability?","Malik: Bazerman's caution cuts both ways: give people a weak set of standards to meet and they stop asking what's right and start asking what's required. We would convert intrinsic motivation into extrinsic motivation.","Greta: Fine — then what does a purpose-conscious policy actually look like?","Malik: Give people control. Earmark a giving budget and let each employee donate their share to any cause they choose, the way Dunn and Norton suggest. Or let teams pick their own volunteer projects and their own time.","Greta: Hmm. And the promotion credit?","Malik: Drop it. The moment volunteerism feeds the bonus calculus it becomes another if-then reward. The Mayo Clinic found letting people spend one day a week on what matters halved burnout — that's the direction of travel."],"choices":["Propose replacing the mandate with a per-employee donation budget and self-chosen volunteer projects, with no sign-in sheets and no promotion credit.","Support the mandate but soften the optics: call it an opportunity and keep the sign-in sheets quietly.","Compromise at two mandatory hours a month with a small community stars badge system to keep morale up.","Advise Greta to drop volunteering as a company matter entirely — volunteers will keep doing it anyway."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Autonomy in service of purpose maximization (the crowding-out effect)","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 preserves what the science says volunteers are feeding on — self-direction and a cause larger than themselves — while adding pro-social control that, per Dunn and Norton, raises well-being. Choices 1 and 2 smuggle extrinsic contingencies in the back door, recreating the box-checking that Bazerman warns turns doing the right thing into checking off boxes. Choice 3 abandons organizational support entirely, forfeiting the policies realm where purpose can be scaled beyond individual effort.","key_concepts":["Employee volunteerism is rising in the United States even as compensated engagement falls.","Volunteer work is nourishing people in ways that paid work simply does not.","Motivation 2.0 does not recognize purpose as a motivator.","Ethics guidelines can unintentionally move purposeful behavior from Type I into Type X.","Bazerman: weak ethical standards turn doing the right thing into checking off boxes.","Giving employees control over how the organization gives back can improve their satisfaction.","Dunn and Norton propose giving individual employees sums to donate to the charities of their choice.","The Mayo Clinic trial let physicians spend one day a week on the aspect of their job most meaningful to them.","Mayo Clinic doctors in the trial had half the burnout rate of those who did not participate.","Handing employees control over giving back may beat one more if-then financial incentive for satisfaction."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the crowding-out mechanism of self-determination theory: formalizing intrinsically rewarding activity with mandates converts it into extrinsic box-checking. The corrective is autonomy in service of purpose — Dunn and Norton's pro-social giving and the Mayo Clinic's purpose day, both presented in the chapter as purpose-centered policy.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}}]},{"id":"ch12","title":"Type I for Individuals: Nine Strategies for Awakening Your Motivation","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Flow Test, Oblique Cards, and Deliberate Practice","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory bench holds three instruments side by side: a pager buzzing at random intervals beside a notepad, a deck of black cards with cryptic phrases, and a stopwatch hovering over a practice schedule. Each instrument illuminates as the player classifies the evidence tiles.","player_mission":"Classify each technique described on the evidence tiles — the pager-based flow test, the Oblique Strategies deck, and deliberate practice — into its correct instrument column.","key_concepts":["Csikszentmihalyi equipped research participants with electronic pagers to sample their mental states at random intervals.","The pager studies paged participants approximately eight times a day for a week.","Real-time reports proved far more honest and revealing than previous methods.","The flow test uses forty random reminders in a week, recording what you're doing, how you feel, and whether you're in flow.","Flow reports can reveal which times of day are most flow-friendly.","Oblique Strategies is a set of one hundred cards published by Brian Eno and Peter Schmidt in 1975.","Oblique cards contain a single, often inscrutable question or statement designed to push you out of a mental rut.","Deliberate practice has one objective: to improve performance, per Anders Ericsson.","Deliberate practice is mentally and physically exhausting, which is why so few people commit to it."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The scenario contrasts three mastery tools from the book: experience sampling (the flow test), associative re-framing (Oblique cards), and structured skill-building (deliberate practice). Csikszentmihalyi's pager method stands out because it captures flow in real time rather than through memory, an evidence-based technique Pink recommends for discovering your true source of intrinsic motivation.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Csikszentmihalyi's research team paged participants about eight times a day for a week, asking about their mental state at that moment. Which technique does the self-administered version — forty random checks in a week — belong to?","column_a_label":"The flow test","column_b_label":"Oblique Strategies cards","column_c_label":"Deliberate practice","rows":[{"label":"Set a reminder to go off at forty random times in a week and record what you're doing and feeling","col":"a"},{"label":"A card reading: Your mistake was a hidden intention","col":"b"},{"label":"Shoot five hundred free throws rather than ten at the end of practice","col":"c"},{"label":"Look for which times of day are more flow-friendly and restructure your day accordingly","col":"a"},{"label":"Pull a card when you're stymied to push yourself out of a mental rut","col":"b"},{"label":"Seek constant, critical feedback on your performance","col":"c"},{"label":"Published by Brian Eno and Peter Schmidt in 1975 as a set of one hundred cards","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b — the pager method comes from Eno and Schmidt","c — it is a deliberate-practice drill with five hundred repetitions","a — but only for athletes and artists, not knowledge workers"],"explanation":"The flow test is Csikszentmihalyi's methodological innovation: random real-time paging turned out to be far more honest and revealing than retrospective reports, and Pink adapts it to forty random self-checks per week. Oblique cards and deliberate practice are separate tools: the cards pry open stuck thinking with cryptic prompts, while deliberate practice is Ericsson's painful, feedback-heavy method for improving performance."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Big Question, the Small Question, and Webber's Card Pair","visual_frame_description":"A nightstand at bedtime holds three artifacts: a plaque engraved with a one-sentence life summary, a journal open to the question Was I better today than yesterday?, and two blank three-by-five-inch cards. A bedside lamp casts warm light over each as the player sorts them.","player_mission":"Classify each question and practice — the sentence question, the daily improvement question, and Webber's morning-and-night card pair — into its correct column.","key_concepts":["Clare Boothe Luce told President Kennedy in 1962 that a great man is one sentence.","Abraham Lincoln's sentence: He preserved the union and freed the slaves.","Franklin Roosevelt's sentence: He lifted us out of a great depression and helped us win a world war.","Contemplating your purpose begins with the big question: What's your sentence?","The small question — Was I better today than yesterday? — is asked before sleep each night.","Small measures include learning ten vocabulary words, making eight sales calls, or writing four pages.","Reminding yourself you don't need to be a master by day 3 is the best way to ensure you will be one by day 3,000.","Alan Webber's exercise uses blank three-by-five-inch cards.","Webber asks: What gets you up in the morning? and What keeps you up at night?"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the motivational power of goal framing: a purpose-level sentence supplies direction while a daily proximal question sustains progress, reminding the learner that motivation is fueled by meaningful aims plus visible improvement. Pink's sources are Luce's famous advice to Kennedy and Webber's card-pair exercise, supported by the mastery science that improvement signals feed intrinsic motivation.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which question is meant to be asked every night before sleep, looking for small measures of improvement such as learned vocabulary words or completed pages?","column_a_label":"The big question (What's your sentence?)","column_b_label":"The small question (Was I better today than yesterday?)","column_c_label":"Webber's morning-and-night card pair","rows":[{"label":"Offered by Clare Boothe Luce to President John F. Kennedy in 1962","col":"a"},{"label":"Lincoln's sentence: He preserved the union and freed the slaves","col":"a"},{"label":"Ask whether you learned your ten vocabulary words, made your eight sales calls, or wrote your four pages","col":"b"},{"label":"Pare each answer to a single sentence, and toss the card if you can't live with it","col":"c"},{"label":"From Alan Webber's book Rules of Thumb","col":"c"},{"label":"A great man is one sentence","col":"a"},{"label":"You don't have to be flawless each day; look for small measures of improvement","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["a — Luce's advice is meant to be a nightly ritual","c — Webber's cards track daily improvement quotas","b — but it only applies to work goals, not health habits"],"explanation":"The small question, Was I better today than yesterday?, is a nightly self-check for incremental gains, built on the insight that real achievement comes from grinding through tough tasks rather than basking in applause. Luce's one-sentence advice and Webber's paired morning-and-night cards serve different ends: the first orients your life's purpose, while the second keeps a living compass you can update whenever an answer stops ringing true."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Taking a Sagmeister: Snip Five Years from Retirement","visual_frame_description":"The door of a New York graphic design studio swings shut for the last time; the sign flips to Back in one year. Inside the window, sketches of travel posters, a stack of passports, and a calendar with all 365 days colored green are visible. A frantic client's assistant hovers at the door.","player_mission":"Explain Sagmeister's time-restructuring strategy in the book's terms, then counsel him through an offer that would cut the sabbatical short.","boss_battle_explanation":"Stefan Sagmeister snips five years from the traditional retirement period and sprinkles them into his working years: every seven years he closes his graphic design shop for a 365-day sabbatical of travel and experimentation, and the ideas generated during the year off often provide his income for the next seven years. It requires planning and saving — a Type I life investment.","stage_1_observation":"It is opening day at Sagmeister's New York graphic design studio. The door lock turns for the last time; the sign flips to Back in one year. Inside, you see sketches of travel posters, a stack of passports, and a calendar with all 365 days colored green. A client's assistant rushes up, desperate: he can't just walk away from the work!","stage_1_question":"What is Sagmeister's strategy, in the book's terms?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Every seven years he takes a full 365-day sabbatical — snipping five years from the traditional twenty-five-year retirement and sprinkling them into his working years — and the unconventional timeline is deliberate, not reckless.","stage_2_intervention":"Ten months into the sabbatical, Sagmeister gets a seven-figure re-engagement offer from a tech giant, on condition that he cut the trip short and return to the studio. He asks you, his friend, for counsel. What do you advise?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Advise him to complete the year — his stated evidence is that the ideas generated during the year off often provide the income for the next seven years, and cutting it short forfeits the un-get-backable, once-per-seven-years window on which his whole plan depends.","key_concepts":["People typically spend the first twenty-five or so years of life learning, about forty working, and the final twenty-five in retirement.","Sagmeister wondered why not snip five years from retirement and sprinkle them into the working years.","Every seven years Sagmeister closes his shop and takes a 365-day sabbatical.","He uses the year to travel, live in places he's never been, and experiment with new projects.","The ideas generated during the year off often provide his income for the next seven years.","Taking a Sagmeister requires a fair bit of planning and saving.","Forgoing the big-screen TV is a small price for an unforgettable year of personal exploration, Pink argues.","Pink says this idea is more realistic than many of us realize."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The scenario tests temporal autonomy: restructuring the traditional life timeline to inject self-directed exploration into working years, an autonomy play in the service of renewal and creative mastery. The evidence base is Sagmeister's practice as reported by Pink, not a controlled study — the book presents it as an entrepreneurial experiment worth emulating.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Deliberate Practice at the Cellist's Stand","visual_frame_description":"A warm rehearsal hall where Dana, forty-two, plays the same Bach cello suites from a stack of worn sheet music; conductor Tomas holds a baton and a clipboard. The sheet-music pages are identical year after year, but one page glows where a new challenging passage is about to be written in.","player_mission":"Diagnose why Dana's decade of weekly practice produced no improvement, then choose the retraining program that matches Ericsson's deliberate-practice principles.","boss_battle_explanation":"Mastery follows deliberate practice — Ericsson's lifelong period of effort to improve performance in a specific domain. It is not playing the same week at the same level: deliberate practice means changing your performance, setting new goals, repeating, seeking constant critical feedback, working ruthlessly on weaknesses, and accepting that the process is mentally and physically exhausting.","stage_1_observation":"At the Eastside Community Orchestra, forty-two-year-old accountant Dana warms up with the same three Bach cello suites she has played at the same tempo since college. After rehearsal, the conductor, Tomas, notes that she has not improved in a decade. Dana shrugs: I practice weekly — that's the commitment, right?","stage_1_question":"Why does Dana's decade of weekly practice produce no improvement, per Anders Ericsson?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Because it is not deliberate practice: she plays the same pieces the same way, while deliberate practice is about changing your performance, setting new goals, and straining to reach a bit higher each time — people who play tennis once a week for years do not get better if they do the same thing each time.","stage_2_intervention":"Dana agrees to restructure. Tomas offers two programs: Program A is a monthly play-along with a metronome at her current tempo, plus recording her rehearsals; Program B is weekly sessions where she targets her weakest passages (shifting and thumb position), repeats tough runs fifty times each, and gets honest critique from Tomas every session, even when it stings. Which program does the mastery science favor?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Program B — it hits every element of deliberate practice: purpose-focused work on weaknesses, repetition at scale (basketball greats shoot five hundred free throws, not ten), constant critical feedback, and honest acceptance that the process should be exhausting, which is exactly why so few commit to it and why it works.","key_concepts":["Anders Ericsson of Florida State University calls the key to mastery deliberate practice.","Deliberate practice is a lifelong period of effort to improve performance in a specific domain.","Deliberate practice has one objective: to improve performance.","People who play tennis once a week for years don't get better if they do the same thing each time.","Basketball greats don't shoot ten free throws at the end of practice; they shoot five hundred.","Seek constant, critical feedback — if you don't know how you're doing, you won't know what to improve.","Those who get better work on their weaknesses, not only on what they're already good at.","Deliberate practice is mentally and physically exhausting.","Few people commit to deliberate practice, which is precisely why it works."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"The battle tests Ericsson's deliberate-practice model: improvement requires goal-raising, repetition, critical feedback, and deliberate strain rather than mere time-on-task. The book's evidence is Ericsson's research on expert performance, applied here to everyday mastery pursuits like Dana's cello.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Flow Test and a Career in Doubt","visual_frame_description":"A coffee shop at golden hour: Dev, a UX designer, slides his phone across the table to Nia, a marketing manager surrounded by sticky notes and a half-empty latte. On the phone screen, a timer icon labeled 40 random pings glows softly between them.","player_mission":"Convince a skeptical friend to run Csikszentmihalyi's flow test and to treat the resulting patterns as real evidence about her intrinsic motivation.","scenario_setup":"You are Dev, a UX designer at a payments startup, in a coffee shop with your friend Nia, a marketing manager who has been questioning her career. A mentor once told her to find your passion, but she doesn't know where to look. You remember Csikszentmihalyi's pager method and realize you can run a miniature version with her phone. She is skeptical but willing to try.","dialogue":["Nia: Everyone says find your passion, Dev. I've been waiting for a lightning bolt for three years. It hasn't come.","Dev: That's the wrong instrument. Csikszentmihalyi didn't ask people to remember their best moments — his team paged them about eight times a day for a week and asked what they were doing right then.","Nia: And random beeps found flow better than memory?","Dev: Yes — the real-time reports were far more honest and revealing than the old retrospective questionnaires. You can run the same experiment alone: set your phone to ping you at forty random moments this week.","Nia: And each ping, I answer what I'm doing, how I'm feeling, and whether I'm in flow?","Dev: Exactly. Then look at the patterns: which moments produced flow, where you were, who you were with, and whether certain times of day are flow-friendly.","Nia: What if the data says my best moments are all at my side project and none at work?","Dev: Then the exercise has told you something about your true source of intrinsic motivation — which is precisely the point. From there you can restructure your day, or have a very different conversation with your manager."],"choices":["Set up the forty-ping week with her tonight, and schedule a data-review session to plan a real change based on the patterns.","Suggest she quit on the spot — if she has any doubt about her job, the flow test will just confirm it.","Warn her that self-reported mood data is too subjective to trust, so she should stick with her mentor's intuition instead.","Have her write one long retrospective essay about her happiest work moments instead — richer data than random pings."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Experience sampling and the flow test (Csikszentmihalyi)","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 mirrors Csikszentmihalyi's method: random real-time sampling beats retrospective memory for honesty, and the book's guiding questions — which moments, where, with whom, flow-friendly times — are designed to surface the true source of intrinsic motivation. Choice 1 overreads a diagnostic tool as a resignation trigger, choice 2 inverts the book's empiricism in favor of folk intuition, and choice 3 repeats the very retrospective method the pager studies showed was inferior.","key_concepts":["Csikszentmihalyi's team paged participants about eight times a day for a week.","Real-time reports proved far more honest and revealing than previous methods.","The flow test sets forty random reminders in a week.","At each ping, record what you're doing, how you're feeling, and whether you're in flow.","The patterns reveal which moments produced flow, where you were, and who you were with.","Some times of day are more flow-friendly than others.","The data can show whether your job matches your true source of intrinsic motivation.","Flow is the state of optimal challenge, discovered and named by Csikszentmihalyi.","You can restructure your day based on flow findings to add optimal experiences."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests experience sampling as a diagnostic: random in-the-moment reports reveal flow patterns that memory distorts, letting the learner locate her true intrinsic motivation. The method is Csikszentmihalyi's pager technique, which Pink presents as evidence-based and easily self-administered.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}}]},{"id":"ch13","title":"Type I for Organizations: Nine Ways to Improve Your Company, Office, or Group","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Autonomy Audit vs. Purpose Cards vs. Pronoun Test","visual_frame_description":"A manager's desk holds three diagnostic tools: a printed survey with four sliders labeled task, time, team, and technique; a stack of blank three-by-five-inch cards; and an ear listening to hallway chatter while the words we and they drift past like labeled balloons.","player_mission":"Classify each organizational diagnostic — the autonomy audit, the purpose-card game, and the pronoun test — into its correct tool column.","key_concepts":["The autonomy audit asks employees four questions covering task, time, team, and technique.","Each autonomy answer is ranked on a scale of 0 to 10, with 0 meaning almost none and 10 meaning a huge amount.","Autonomy audit responses are anonymous.","The employee average falls somewhere on a 40-point autonomy scale.","0 on that scale is a North Korean prison and 40 is Woodstock.","A healthy overall average can mask a problem in one area, such as low time autonomy.","Whose purpose is it anyway? asks each person to write a one-sentence company purpose on a card.","Reading the purpose cards aloud reveals whether the organization is aligned or scattered.","Reich's pronoun test listens for we versus they when employees describe the company.","They suggests disengagement; we suggests employees feel part of something significant and meaningful."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The scenario contrasts three diagnostics of organizational health: quantitative autonomy measurement, purpose alignment sampling, and linguistic belonging detection. Pink grounds them in the autonomy science of self-determination theory and in Reich's practical diagnostic — tools designed to close the perception-reality gap that keeps leaders from knowing how much control they actually exert.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Which tool asks for anonymous 0-to-10 ratings on task, time, team, and technique?","column_a_label":"The autonomy audit","column_b_label":"Whose purpose is it anyway?","column_c_label":"Reich's pronoun test","rows":[{"label":"Four questions with numerical rankings from 0 (almost none) to 10 (a huge amount)","col":"a"},{"label":"The average lands on a 40-point scale running from a North Korean prison to Woodstock","col":"a"},{"label":"Hand everyone a blank three-by-five-inch card asking for a one-sentence answer about the company's purpose","col":"b"},{"label":"Listen to employees in hallways and at lunch for whether they say we or they","col":"c"},{"label":"An exercise designed to close the gap between perception and reality","col":"b"},{"label":"Read the answers aloud to check whether the team is aligned on a common purpose","col":"b"},{"label":"Devised by former U.S. labor secretary Robert B. Reich","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b — the ranking exercise is the purpose-card game","c — the 40-point scale measures pronoun frequency","a — it was designed by Clay Shirky for internet forums"],"explanation":"The autonomy audit is the only one of the three that quantifies the four freedoms — task, time, team, and technique — on 0-to-10 scales, with averages plotted on the 40-point scale so leaders discover gaps between perceived and actual autonomy. The card game targets purpose alignment, and the pronoun test gauges organizational belonging through the everyday language workers use."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Now-That Peer Rewards vs. If-Then Carrots vs. Time Autonomy","visual_frame_description":"Three vending-machine-style panels display different reward systems: one dispenses 50-dollar certificates triggered by a peer's thumbprint, one dispenses a carrot whenever a sales number hits a bullseye, and one unlocks a fifth of the weekly calendar as free time. The panels light up as each evidence tile drops into place.","player_mission":"Classify each reward practice — the Kimley-Horn peer bonus, contingent commissions, and 20 percent time — into its correct category, and identify where the Kimley-Horn system belongs.","key_concepts":["At Kimley-Horn and Associates, a civil engineering firm in Raleigh, North Carolina, any employee can award a 50-dollar bonus to a colleague at any point without asking permission.","The rewards work because they're real-time and not handed down from any management.","Any employee who does something exceptional receives recognition from their peers within minutes.","The bonuses are noncontingent now-that rewards.","Noncontingent now-that rewards avoid the seven deadly flaws of most corporate carrots.","A reward from a colleague carries a different, perhaps deeper, meaning than one from a boss.","20 percent time encourages employees to spend one-fifth of their hours working on any project they want.","Gmail and Google News are cited as products of 20 percent time.","20 percent time with training wheels starts with 10 percent time — one afternoon of a five-day workweek — for six months.","An island of autonomy helps people act on their great ideas and converts downtime into more productive time."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario discriminates reward architectures: noncontingent peer recognition preserves intrinsic motivation while contingent if-then incentives trigger the seven deadly flaws of corporate carrots. Pink's evidence is Kimley-Horn's working peer-bonus system and the Google 20 percent time lineage, illustrating how autonomy-friendly structures outperform contingency-based pay.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Kimley-Horn's HR director told Fast Company the 50-dollar bonus system works because it's real-time and it's not handed down from any management. Which column does the Kimley-Horn system belong in?","column_a_label":"Peer-to-peer now-that rewards","column_b_label":"If-then contingency rewards","column_c_label":"Time-autonomy policies (20 percent time)","rows":[{"label":"At any point, without asking permission, anyone at Kimley-Horn can award a 50-dollar bonus to a colleague","col":"a"},{"label":"A commission paid only after hitting next quarter's sales number","col":"b"},{"label":"Encourages employees to spend one-fifth of their hours on any project they want","col":"c"},{"label":"The system that carries the seven deadly flaws of most corporate carrots","col":"b"},{"label":"Because the bonuses are noncontingent, they avoid the seven deadly flaws","col":"a"},{"label":"Gmail and Google News are cited as products of this practice","col":"c"},{"label":"A 50-dollar bonus lands within minutes of exceptional work, from a colleague rather than a boss","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b — the bonus is contingent on a manager's review cycle","c — it is a time-autonomy policy covering one-fifth of working hours","a — it is a boss-given award handed out at quarterly reviews"],"explanation":"Kimley-Horn's peers can award 50 dollars to anyone at any time without permission — a real-time, noncontingent now-that reward from a colleague rather than a boss, which is exactly why it dodges the seven deadly flaws and carries deeper meaning than a manager's carrot. Contingent commissions are classic if-then rewards, and 20 percent time is a separate autonomous-time policy whose fruits include Gmail and Google News."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Goldilocks for Groups: Fixing a Stuck Team","visual_frame_description":"An agile sprint board at Nordlicht Games shows six names: the same three people repeatedly assigned the glowing regression passes, while Elena's avatar sits buried under grey data-entry cards. In the corner, a contest poster reading Best QA Tester hangs crookedly.","player_mission":"Diagnose the flow-hoarding and competition problems on this QA team, then choose the intervention that spreads Goldilocks tasks and protects intrinsic motivation.","boss_battle_explanation":"Goldilocks tasks — neither too easy nor too hard — deliver flow, but teams often defeat them: people repeat the jobs they have proven they can do, while an unfortunate few get saddled with the flow-free tasks nobody else wants. The fix is diversity (per Teresa Amabile), a no-competition zone, task-shifting, and animating the group with purpose rather than rewards.","stage_1_observation":"You are an agile coach at Nordlicht Games watching Maya's QA team of six. On the sprint board, the same three people always pull the regression passes they have closed a hundred times; Elena, the newest member, is stuck with data-entry scripts she finished in week one. Meanwhile, the sales department is quietly running an internal Best QA Tester cash contest.","stage_1_question":"What is the root problem on this team, per the Goldilocks-for-groups analysis?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Flow is being hoarded, not shared: people settle into the jobs they have proven they can do, Elena is saddled with the flow-free work nobody else wants, and the contest pits coworkers against one another — which rarely works and almost always undermines intrinsic motivation.","stage_2_intervention":"Maya asks you to pick one intervention for the next sprint: (a) rotate tasks so Elena gets the regression passes and veterans get her scripts; (b) add a public leaderboard for the QA contest to make the reward visible; (c) run a task-shifting pass in which bored veterans train Elena on regression skills and then take on aspects of a more experienced member's work, while keeping the sprint free of rankings. Which one do you choose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option (c) — it follows the chapter's recipe: cross-train people into new challenges (task-shifting), avoid pitting coworkers head-to-head, and keep the group animated by a shared mission rather than rewards; a leaderboard would institutionalize competition, and a bare rotation ignores the mastery growth that task-shifting provides.","key_concepts":["A Goldilocks task is neither too easy nor too hard and delivers a sense of flow.","Teamwork often leaves people doing the jobs they always do because they've proven they can do them well.","An unfortunate few get saddled with the flow-free tasks nobody else wants.","Teresa Amabile advises diverse work groups so people stimulate and learn from each other.","Homogeneous groups in background and training fail to cross-fertilize each other's ideas.","Pitting coworkers against one another rarely works and almost always undermines intrinsic motivation.","Task-shifting lets someone bored train others in skills they've mastered and take on a more experienced member's work.","Competition is a c-word to avoid; collaboration and cooperation are the words to use.","Nothing bonds a team like a shared mission — animate with purpose, don't motivate with rewards."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"The battle tests flow distribution and Amabile's intrinsic-motivation design principles: homogeneous assignments and competition drain the Goldilocks experience, while diversity, cooperation, and task-shifting spread it. The evidence is expert recommendation and the flow science of Csikszentmihalyi rather than a controlled workplace experiment.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"20 Percent Time with Training Wheels","visual_frame_description":"A town-hall screen at Bernal Analytics shows a slide titled 20% INNOVATION TIME — FOREVER with a giant question mark; CEO Priya stands at the podium as CFO Omar types on his phone. In the next scene, junior engineer Leo's desk glows with a prototype reporting tool he built in his free afternoons.","player_mission":"Advise a skittish CEO on how to launch 20 percent time, then defend keeping the autonomy island once the pilot produces a working prototype.","boss_battle_explanation":"20 percent time lets employees spend one-fifth of their hours on any project they want, and its fruits include Gmail and Google News. For skittish leaders, Pink recommends training wheels: start with 10 percent time — one afternoon of a five-day workweek — for six months, rather than committing forever, creating an island of autonomy where downtime becomes productive time.","stage_1_observation":"In a town-hall meeting at Bernal Analytics, CEO Priya unveils a slide titled 20% INNOVATION TIME — FOREVER. The room goes quiet; CFO Omar texts you: Cost? Slippage? Forever? You watch Priya's confidence deflate as three directors roll their eyes.","stage_1_question":"Pink has a specific answer for leaders who love the idea but feel skittish. What does he propose?","stage_1_correct_answer":"20 percent time with training wheels: start with 10 percent time — one afternoon of a five-day workweek — and commit to it for six months instead of forever, converting slack that is already being wasted at work into focused creative time.","stage_2_intervention":"Six months in, the pilot works: junior engineer Leo used his afternoons to prototype an internal reporting tool that saves the firm nine hours a week — and he is pleading to keep the afternoon, while Omar wants to redeploy those hours to billable work. Priya turns to you for the final call.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep the island of autonomy — the pilot is exactly what Pink predicted: people acting on their great ideas and converting downtime into more productive time; the next Post-it note will not emerge from a billing plan, and helping Leo's idea grow is the entire purpose of the exercise.","key_concepts":["20 percent time encourages employees to spend one-fifth of their hours working on any project they want.","Gmail and Google News are cited as benefits the world has reaped from 20 percent time.","A more modest version starts with 10 percent time — one afternoon of a five-day workweek.","Leaders are encouraged to try 10 percent time for six months rather than committing forever.","Most people have wasted at least that amount of time at work anyway.","Creating an island of autonomy helps people act on their great ideas.","Autonomy time converts downtime into more productive time.","Someone in your operation just might invent the next Post-it note.","A FedEx Day is a related practice: a day to work on anything, with one rule — deliver something the next day."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests organizational autonomy provision: even a partial, time-boxed grant of self-directed work redirects wasted time into productive innovation without the risk of a permanent mandate. Pink's evidence is the 20 percent time lineage behind Gmail and Google News, scaled down for risk-averse leaders.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Three Steps Toward Giving Up Control","visual_frame_description":"A director's office at Alder & Finch where Rafael leans back at a cluttered desk and deputy director Simone stands by a whiteboard listing three steps: involve in goal-setting, use noncontrolling language, hold office hours. A calendar on the wall shows blocked off weekly slots labeled open door.","player_mission":"Persuade a control-oriented director to test all three control-relinquishing steps for one quarter, and frame the success metrics so engagement counts alongside throughput.","scenario_setup":"You are Rafael, operations director at Alder & Finch, a 120-person design studio. Deputy director Simone has shadowed you for a month and is convinced your team is over-controlled — you write goals for everyone, your emails are full of must and should, and your office door is a fortress. You are skeptical but willing to test her three proposals for one quarter, with the CEO watching for results.","dialogue":["Simone: Rafael, your people aren't rebelling — they're just complying. Type X bosses relish control; Type I bosses relinquish it.","Rafael: If I stop assigning goals, how will anything ship? That's a recipe for chaos.","Simone: Start by involving people in goal-setting. Research shows individuals are far more engaged when they're pursuing goals they had a hand in creating — and people often have higher aims than the ones you assign.","Rafael: Fine. And the must and should in my emails? I'm not going to write in poetry.","Simone: Try think about or consider instead. A small change in wording can promote engagement over compliance — it might even reduce some people's urge to defy.","Rafael: And what about the flow of people into my office? I need those five-minute summons to stay ahead.","Simone: Hold office hours — one or two hours a week when your schedule is clear and any employee can come talk to you about anything. You'll learn something, and they'll stop dreading the summons.","Rafael: Okay. One quarter, all three. If throughput drops, we go back to control.","Simone: That's a fair experiment — but watch the engagement metrics, not just throughput. Compliance looks efficient until it quietly strangles the work."],"choices":["Implement all three steps — co-created goals, noncontrolling language, and weekly open office hours — and review engagement alongside throughput at quarter's end.","Implement only office hours — the other two steps risk too much scope creep for one quarter.","Keep assigned goals but soften the language, since engagement is really about tone.","Decline the experiment and commission an external audit to prove your control style is optimal."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"Autonomy support via goal participation, noncontrolling language, and open access (self-determination theory)","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 applies all three of the chapter's control-relinquishing steps: people are far more engaged pursuing goals they helped create, think-about language trades compliance for engagement, and office hours open channels of trust — the complete package self-determination theory predicts. Choice 1 undercuts the package, choice 2 mistakes tone for structure, and choice 3 is a delaying tactic that bureaucratizes the very problem.","key_concepts":["Type X bosses relish control; Type I bosses relinquish control.","Individuals are far more engaged when they're pursuing goals they had a hand in creating.","People often have higher aims than the goals you assign them.","Saying think about or consider instead of must or should promotes engagement over compliance.","Noncontrolling language might reduce some people's urge to defy.","Office hours set aside one or two hours a week when any employee can talk about anything on her mind.","Involving people in goal-setting draws on a considerable body of research.","Summoning people into your office should sometimes be replaced by letting them come to you.","Colleagues might benefit and the leader might learn something from open office hours."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests autonomy support in management: goal participation, noncontrolling language, and open access convert compliance into engagement. The chapter's three concrete steps operationalize self-determination theory's autonomy need for leaders reluctant to give up control.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}}]},{"id":"ch14","title":"The Zen of Compensation: Paying People the Type I Way","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Internal Fairness, External Fairness, and Paying Above Average","visual_frame_description":"Two adjoining cubicles sit side by side, each with a nameplate — yours and Fred's — and a pay envelope glowing on each desk. Above them, a cityscape of Fortune 200 towers appears whenever external-fairness evidence is inspected, followed by a rising wage curve labeled above market.","player_mission":"Classify each compensation situation as an internal fairness issue, an external fairness issue, or the pay-more-than-average strategy, and identify which principle the cubicle gap violates.","key_concepts":["The most important aspect of any compensation package is fairness.","Internal fairness means paying people commensurate with their colleagues.","External fairness means paying people in line with others doing similar work in similar organizations.","A violation of internal fairness makes motivation plummet.","A violation of external fairness produces a largely irreversible motivation dip.","Paying people the Type I way doesn't mean paying everyone the same amount.","If Fred has a harder job or contributes more, he deserves a richer deal — and studies show most people accept that.","Getting internal and external equity right isn't itself a motivator.","Equity done right avoids putting the issue of money back on the table as a de-motivator.","George Akerlof and Janet Yellen discovered companies that overpaid their workers, and higher wages could reduce a company's costs."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests equity theory's role in self-determination: fairness violations shift attention to money and crush motivation, while fair baseline pay lets people focus on the work itself. Pink's framework derives from Deci's finding that rewards like money are most demotivating when organizations use them to control staff.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"You and Fred have adjoining cubicles, equivalent responsibility and experience — and Fred makes scads more money than you. Which principle is being violated?","column_a_label":"Internal fairness","column_b_label":"External fairness","column_c_label":"Pay more than average","rows":[{"label":"Paying people commensurate with their colleagues","col":"a"},{"label":"Fred, in the adjoining cubicle with equivalent responsibility, earns scads more","col":"a"},{"label":"Paying people in line with others doing similar work in similar organizations","col":"b"},{"label":"Auditors with ten years' experience discover peers at other Fortune 200 firms make double","col":"b"},{"label":"Akerlof and Yellen found companies paying a little more than the market demands","col":"c"},{"label":"Higher wages attract better talent, reduce turnover, and boost productivity and morale","col":"c"},{"label":"Fred with a harder job or bigger contribution deserves a richer deal — that's fair","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b — cubicle neighbors are compared across firms","c — the gap should be closed by raising everyone to the market rate","a — but only after external fairness is established"],"explanation":"Internal fairness means pay commensurate with colleagues, so Fred's cubicle advantage violates it and drops your motivation. External fairness compares like work across similar organizations — the double-salary Fortune 200 discovery. Paying above average is the separate Akerlof-Yellen strategy, and the addendum clarifies that unequal pay is fair when job demands or contributions differ."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Wide-Ranging, Relevant, Hard-to-Game Metrics","visual_frame_description":"A compensation consultant's dashboard at Meridian Medical Devices shows one giant quarterly sales number ballooning in red while smaller dials for customer satisfaction, new product ideas, and peer evaluations stay greyed out. A second screen shows a web of balanced metrics glowing green.","player_mission":"Predict where a single-number bonus plan drives a product manager's behavior, then choose the metric design that converts if-then rewards into now-that rewards.","boss_battle_explanation":"Performance metrics become less combustible when they're wide-ranging, relevant, and hard to game: a pay plan resting on a single quarterly sales number narrows focus and invites corner-cutting, while a varied metric set — current and future sales, company revenue, customer satisfaction, new product ideas, peer evaluations — transforms if-then rewards into now-that rewards. The payoff for reaching metrics should be modest, not massive.","stage_1_observation":"You are the compensation consultant at Meridian Medical Devices. Product manager Theo's bonus rides almost entirely on hitting next quarter's sales number. You watch the data: his new feature requests for the quarter after next have flatlined, he has stopped visiting two happy but slow-paying customers, and his team logs overtime re-negotiating deal terms to land bookings before the deadline.","stage_1_question":"What does Pink's metric analysis predict Theo's behavior will spiral into?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Narrowing: Theo won't concern himself with the quarter after this one, the health of the company, or whether the firm is investing in research and development — and nervous people in his position cut corners to reach quarterly goals, exactly the pattern in the chapter's product-manager example.","stage_2_intervention":"The CEO hands you two new pay plans: Plan A — salary plus a massive one-time bonus if the quarterly number beats plan by 20 percent; Plan B — salary plus modest targets across current-quarter sales, current-year sales, two-year company revenue and profit, customer satisfaction, new-product ideas, and coworker evaluations. You recommend one.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Plan B — varied metrics are harder to finagle, modest gains rather than massive payoffs are less likely to narrow people's focus or encourage the low road, and a range of measures reflecting the totality of great work transforms counterproductive if-then rewards into less combustible now-that rewards.","key_concepts":["A pay plan resting on one quarterly sales goal makes a product manager ignore the quarters after it.","Nervous managers may cut corners to reach a quarterly goal.","Good metrics include sales, customer satisfaction, new product ideas, and coworker evaluations.","When metrics are varied, they're harder to finagle.","The gain for reaching metrics shouldn't be too large.","Modest payoffs are less likely to narrow people's focus or encourage the low road.","The right mix of metrics varies considerably across organizations.","Some people will find a way to game even the most carefully calibrated system.","A variety of measures can transform counterproductive if-then rewards into less combustible now-that rewards.","Effective organizations compensate people in ways that let them mostly forget about compensation."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests narrow-goal dynamics as presented in the book: single-number contingencies narrow attention and invite corner-cutting, while broad, moderate-stakes metric portfolios protect intrinsic engagement. Pink's evidence is the contrast between narrow and wide metric designs in the compensation chapter, grounded in Deci's warning that contingent money is most demotivating.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Zen of Compensation: A Raise Conversation","visual_frame_description":"An HR conference room at Bluepeak Logistics: Deniz, the HR director, stands before a whiteboard showing a pay scale with the words fair base marked above the market line, while CEO Anya tosses a glossy perks catalog onto the table with a printing of a giant quarterly bonus trophy.","player_mission":"Redirect the CEO's pay redesign — base-pay cuts funding a giant bonus pool and a perks catalog — toward fair, slightly-above-average base pay that takes money off the table.","scenario_setup":"You are Deniz, HR director at Bluepeak Logistics, a 400-person courier network. CEO Anya is redesigning pay to light a fire: she wants to cut base salaries 8 percent to fund a giant quarterly top dispatcher bonus pool, add a perks catalog of office espresso machines and swag, and celebrate pay in every all-hands. You believe this will backfire. Your mission is to redirect the plan toward the Zen of compensation before Friday's board vote.","dialogue":["Anya: Deniz, money is the language people understand. Shrink the base, supercharge the bonuses, and let's talk about salary at every all-hands.","Deniz: That's precisely backwards, Anya. Pink's reading of Deci: the more prominent salary, perks, and benefits are in someone's work life, the more they inhibit creativity and unravel performance.","Anya: So you want us to pay less? That can't be the Type I way.","Deniz: The Type I way isn't bargain-basement wages — it's the Zen of compensation: in Motivation 3.0, the best use of money is to take the issue of money off the table.","Anya: And the equity questions? If two dispatchers in adjacent hubs earn wildly different amounts, people notice.","Deniz: Internal fairness means paying people commensurate with their colleagues; external fairness means matching similar work at similar organizations. Getting equity right isn't itself a motivator — it's how we stop money from becoming a de-motivator.","Anya: So what makes people actually feel paid well?","Deniz: Two moves: pay a little above average — Akerlof and Yellen found it attracts better talent, reduces turnover, and boosts productivity and morale — and if we keep any metrics, keep them wide-ranging, relevant, and hard to game, with modest payoffs."],"choices":["Abandon the base-pay cut and bonus pool; set fair, slightly-above-market base pay, keep perks modest, and let money recede into the background so the work itself leads.","Keep the bonus pool but double the base cut to make the fire bigger — more cash on the table means more motivation.","Keep pay flat and invest the savings in an espresso machine catalog; perks are the modern salary.","Compromise: equalize everyone's pay to the same number so no fairness complaint can ever recur."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The Zen of compensation: fairness and adequate base pay that takes money off the table","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 0 executes the chapter's plan: internal and external fairness, slightly-above-average base pay per Akerlof and Yellen, modest wide-ranging metrics, and compensation designed to disappear from attention — the condition under which people focus on the work itself. Choice 1 amplifies exactly the salience that unravels performance, choice 2 mistakes perks for pay, and choice 3 confuses equal pay with fair pay, which the book rejects outright.","key_concepts":["In Motivation 3.0, the best use of money is to take the issue of money off the table.","The more prominent salary, perks, and benefits are, the more they can inhibit creativity and unravel performance.","Edward Deci: when organizations use rewards like money to motivate staff, that's when they're most demotivating.","Effective organizations compensate people so they can mostly forget about compensation and focus on the work itself.","The most important aspect of any compensation package is fairness.","Internal fairness: pay commensurate with colleagues; external fairness: pay in line with similar work in similar organizations.","Akerlof and Yellen found paying a little more than the market could attract better talent, reduce turnover, and boost productivity and morale.","Higher wages could actually reduce a company's costs.","High base pay does more for performance and commitment than an attractive bonus structure.","Wide-ranging, relevant, and hard-to-game metrics transform if-then rewards into now-that rewards."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests the Zen of compensation: fair, adequate, slightly-above-average base pay that takes the issue of money off the table so the work itself can motivate. The Akerlof-Yellen efficiency-wage finding and Deci's demotivation warning anchor the policy choices the player must steer through.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."}}]},{"id":"ch15","title":"Type I for Parents and Educators: Nine Ideas for Helping Our Kids","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"If-Then Bribes vs. Type I Engagement vs. Standardization","visual_frame_description":"A bright school hallway splits into three labeled corridors on screen. Left corridor: a cash register churns out pizza boxes, iPods, and dollar bills for students. Middle corridor: kids build experiments and lead student council votes with proud grins. Right corridor: identical desks where every student writes the same workbook page on the same day. A banner overhead reads: Bribing into compliance or challenging into engagement?","player_mission":"Classify each family or school practice into one of three categories: Motivation 2.0 if-then bribes, Type I engagement practices, or standardization with routines and right answers. Drag each practice card to the corridor it belongs to, then answer which category the featured practice belongs to.","key_concepts":["All kids start out as curious, self-directed Type I's, yet many end up as disengaged, compliant Type X's.","The adults running schools and heading families may be the cause of the Type I-to-Type X slide.","Science shows that promising a preschooler a fancy certificate for drawing a picture can make the child draw - and then lose further interest in drawing.","There is a mismatch between what science knows about motivation and what schools actually do.","Too many schools are redoubling their emphasis on routines, right answers, and standardization.","Schools haul out a wagon full of if-then rewards such as pizza for reading books, iPods for showing up to class, and cash for good test scores.","Reward schemes of this kind bribe students into compliance instead of challenging them into engagement.","The world economy increasingly demands nonroutine, creative, conceptual abilities from workers.","Raising Type I kids requires helping them move toward autonomy, mastery, and purpose at school and at home.","Breaking Motivation 2.0's grip on education and parenting matters for the new world of work and for leading satisfying lives."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the extrinsic-reward effect at the heart of Motivation 2.0: contingent if-then incentives buy short-term compliance while undercutting intrinsic interest, as with the preschooler who loses interest in drawing after being promised a certificate. Deci and Ryan's self-determination research, synthesized in Drive, holds that contingent rewards can crowd out the autonomy, mastery, and purpose that sustain genuine engagement.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each family or school practice: a) a Motivation 2.0 if-then reward that bribes for compliance, b) a Type I practice that challenges children into engagement through autonomy, mastery, or purpose, or c) an emphasis on routines, right answers, and standardization.","column_a_label":"Motivation 2.0 if-then bribe into compliance","column_b_label":"Type I challenge into engagement","column_c_label":"Routines, right answers, standardization","rows":[{"label":"A middle school pays students $10 to attend Saturday tutoring sessions.","col":"a"},{"label":"Principal Okafor lets the student council choose the community service projects for the whole term.","col":"b"},{"label":"The district mandates that every fourth-grade class cover the same workbook page on the same calendar day.","col":"c"},{"label":"A PTA offers a new iPad to any kid who shows up to school every day for a month.","col":"a"},{"label":"Mrs. Igwe's science students design their own experiments around questions they are genuinely curious about.","col":"b"},{"label":"Parents pay their daughter $20 for every A on her report card.","col":"a"},{"label":"A school replaces free reading time with a scripted test-prep workbook drill held every morning.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - the payment is a positive reinforcer that builds engagement because attendance leads to better grades","c - the reward standardizes attendance, so it belongs in the standardization category","a - but only if the money is framed as a salary for schoolwork rather than a bonus"],"explanation":"The cash-for-attendance deal is a textbook if-then reward: the chapter describes schools hauling out a wagon full of if-then rewards, including iPods for showing up to class and cash for good test scores, practices that bribe students into compliance instead of challenging them into engagement. The chapter also notes that a promised reward can make a child perform the behavior and then lose further interest in it, which is why the middle school category describes a compliance bribe rather than genuine engagement."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Reward-Undermining Effect: Cash for Test Scores","visual_frame_description":"A school board meeting room glows under fluorescent lights. Superintendent Rivera stands before a projector showing a slide titled Incentive Plan: $50 per passing test grade, with a prize ladder below it listing new iPods for attendance and pizza for reading logs. Board members lean forward across a long oak table, calculators out, while a stack of report cards sits untouched in the corner.","player_mission":"Watch the scene, predict what the motivation science described in the chapter says will happen to the students, then choose the intervention that moves the district from bribing compliance toward raising Type I kids. Defeat the if-then reward trap at both stages.","key_concepts":["All kids start out as curious, self-directed Type I's, yet many end up as disengaged, compliant Type X's.","A promised reward can produce the behavior and then drain interest out of it, as with the preschooler promised a certificate for drawing.","Science knows one thing while schools often do another: there is a mismatch between the evidence and classroom practice.","Too many schools are redoubling their emphasis on routines, right answers, and standardization.","If-then rewards seen in schools include pizza for reading books, iPods for showing up to class, and cash for good test scores.","Such schemes bribe students into compliance instead of challenging them into engagement.","The world economy now demands nonroutine, creative, conceptual abilities from workers.","Raising Type I kids means helping them move toward autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","Breaking Motivation 2.0's grip on education and parenting is needed to equip young people for the new world of work.","Satisfying lives, not just compliant behavior, are the goal the chapter argues parents and educators should pursue."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the reward-undermining mechanism of Motivation 2.0: contingent cash incentives produce the rewarded behavior but can strip the intrinsic interest from the activity itself, exactly as a promised certificate made the preschooler draw but lose interest in drawing. The chapter's argument is that adults can do better by challenging children into engagement through autonomy, mastery, and purpose rather than bribing them into compliance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is the if-then reward trap and the compliance-versus-engagement distinction: extra-credit cash and prizes get students to perform, but they convert learning into a transaction and predict lost interest afterward, so the fix is to shift the school's practices toward autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","stage_1_observation":"Superintendent Rivera stands at the board meeting holding a draft incentive plan that would pay ninth graders $50 for every passing test grade. She walks the room through the prize ladder: cash for test scores, iPods for showing up to class, pizza for completed reading logs. Board members nod approvingly as she asks what the research predicts will happen to these students' love of learning under the plan.","stage_1_question":"Based on the chapter's account of the science, what does the evidence predict for students paid $50 per passing test grade?","stage_1_correct_answer":"They will comply, showing up and performing on the tests, but many will lose further intrinsic interest in the learning itself, because contingent rewards convert an engaging activity into a transaction - the same effect the chapter describes for the preschooler promised a certificate for drawing.","stage_2_intervention":"A parent on the committee proposes scrapping the cash plan entirely and asks you to put forward the replacement. The board wants a concrete move. Which intervention most closely follows the chapter's recipe for raising Type I kids?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Redeploy the budget toward autonomy, mastery, and purpose: let students choose learning projects that genuinely interest them, give them real voice in setting their own goals, and connect the work to purposes beyond grades - challenging them into engagement rather than bribing them into compliance."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Family Reading-Reward Debate","visual_frame_description":"A warm evening living room. Nine-year-old Maya curls up on the couch holding a library book about dragons while a glass jar labeled Reading Money sits half-empty on the shelf behind her. Her parents, Tom and Lena, face each other across the coffee table in a tense but civil standoff, and grandma Ada knits in the armchair, watching them all.","player_mission":"Join the family conversation as the voice of wisdom. Listen to each argument, then choose the response that reflects what the chapter's science says about if-then rewards and about raising Type I kids. Your choices steer whether Maya keeps loving reading or starts negotiating book prices.","key_concepts":["A promised certificate for drawing made the preschooler draw - and then lose further interest in drawing.","If-then rewards such as pizza for reading books buy compliance rather than engagement.","Kids start out as curious, self-directed Type I's and can end up disengaged, compliant Type X's.","The problem may be the adults who run schools and head families.","Mismatch: science knows, and schools and families often do otherwise.","Raising Type I kids means moving them toward autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","Breaking Motivation 2.0's grip on parenting is part of the chapter's program.","Satisfying lives, not just compliant behavior, are the goal for parents and educators.","Routines, right answers, and standardization push children in the wrong direction for the new world of work.","The world economy demands nonroutine, creative, conceptual abilities."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The scenario pressures the overjustification-style effect: a contingent if-then payment per book turns reading into a commercial exchange, and the chapter's evidence says the child complies now but loses interest later, as the preschooler-certificate study showed. The chapter's remedy is to restore autonomy, mastery, and purpose, which is what reconnects Maya to reading for its own sake.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Maya used to devour books. Last semester her parents, Tom and Lena, started paying her $2 per book read, and now Maya reads only the thinnest titles to collect her allowance and asks to skip reading night. Tom defends the deal as a fair wage for homework; Lena worries the love of reading is gone; grandma Ada remembers her teaching years. Stakes: whether Maya keeps reading after the money stops.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Tom","line":"I don't see the problem. Maya used to be a reluctant reader, and now she finishes four books a month. The two dollars per book is working."},{"speaker":"Lena","line":"Is it? She has been choosing the thinnest books on the shelf since we started paying her. Last night she asked if she could be paid twice for re-reading."},{"speaker":"Maya","line":"If I read The Hobbit again, that counts as one book. But it takes me three weeks. I can read four picture books in one night for the same money."},{"speaker":"Tom","line":"Hey, a deal is a deal. Motivation is motivation. She is reading more than she did in September, and that was the whole point."},{"speaker":"Ada","line":"When I taught first grade we handed out gold stars for reading. The children who needed the stars never read once the stars stopped. The ones who chose their own books kept going for years."},{"speaker":"Lena","line":"So the real question is whether we are teaching Maya to love reading or teaching her to negotiate book prices."},{"speaker":"Maya","line":"Actually... the dragon book Ms. Chen gave me? I do not need money for that one. Can I just read it and not get paid?"},{"speaker":"Tom","line":"...Huh. So you would rather read the dragon book for free than earn two dollars for a picture book. That changes things."}],"choices":["Keep the $2-per-book deal firmly in place - it measurably increased reading, and the fix is simply to pay more for harder books.","End the cash deal and restore choice: let Maya pick books that genuinely interest her, talk about them together, and treat reading as an adventure rather than a job.","Compromise: keep paying, but only for classic literature, so she develops worthwhile taste.","Switch to a family leaderboard with a bigger prize for whoever reads the most books by Friday."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"The reward-undermining effect of contingent if-then rewards under Motivation 2.0, and the chapter's alternative of moving children toward autonomy, mastery, and purpose to keep them intrinsically engaged.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it removes the contingent payment that converted reading into a transaction and restores autonomy - Maya already signals intrinsic interest with the dragon book she would read for free - which is exactly the autonomy, mastery, and purpose prescription for keeping Type I kids engaged. Choice 0 fails because it doubles down on the if-then deal and even adds per-difficulty pricing, deepening the negotiation mindset; choice 2 keeps the bribe alive while adding external taste-control, which is still Motivation 2.0; choice 3 merely swaps one extrinsic contest for another, and leaderboards predictably drive the same gaming behavior of chasing volume over meaning."}]},{"id":"ch16","title":"The Type I Reading List: Fifteen Essential Books","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Fixed Mindset vs. Growth Mindset vs. Deliberate Practice","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen mind gallery. Left panel: a stone statue of a person with a chiseled face, cracks running through it, labeled Fixed. Center panel: the same statue in clay, hands reshaping it, labeled Growth. Right panel: a glowing forge where a runner hammers herself through repeated drills, labeled Deliberate Practice. Thought-bubbles from a row of office workers drift toward each panel.","player_mission":"Sort each person's belief or behavior into the correct panel: fixed mindset, growth mindset, or deliberate practice. Read each scenario carefully, then choose the column each evidence item belongs to and answer the featured classification.","key_concepts":["Carol Dweck's research distinguishes a fixed mindset, where talents and abilities are carved in stone, from a growth mindset, where talents can be developed.","Fixed mindsets see every encounter as a test of worthiness, while growth mindsets see the same encounters as opportunities to improve.","Dweck offers concrete steps for moving from fixed to growth, including listening for the fixed mindset voice that hurts resiliency.","Growth-minded language sounds like: I'm not sure I can do it now, but I think I can learn with time and effort.","Geoff Colvin's Talent Is Overrated argues the difference between the merely good and the masters is deliberate practice.","Deliberate practice is highly repetitive, mentally demanding work that is often unpleasant but undeniably effective.","Colvin's Type I insight: if you set a goal of becoming an expert in your business, you immediately start doing all kinds of things you do not do now.","Interpret challenges not as roadblocks but as opportunities to stretch yourself.","Autonomy, mastery, and purpose are integral to the human condition, which is why the reading list exists.","Mastery is one of the three elements the reading list explores through fifteen essential books."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"The scenario tests Dweck's mindset pair: fixed-mindset people treat every encounter as a verdict on their worth, while growth-mindset people treat the same encounters as chances to improve - and it contrasts both with Colvin's deliberate practice, the repetitive, mentally demanding work that separates masters from the merely good. The book presents both as core tools for cultivating a Type I life of autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Sort each belief and behavior: a) fixed mindset - talents and abilities carved in stone, every encounter a test of worthiness; b) growth mindset - talents can be developed, encounters are opportunities to improve; or c) deliberate practice - highly repetitive, mentally demanding work aimed at getting better.","column_a_label":"Fixed mindset","column_b_label":"Growth mindset","column_c_label":"Deliberate practice","rows":[{"label":"After a rejected proposal, Priya concludes the feedback proves she was never any good at design.","col":"a"},{"label":"Dev tells his review panel: I'm not sure I can do it now, but I think I can learn with time and effort.","col":"b"},{"label":"Nadia spends ninety minutes a day on repetitive, mentally demanding drills that target her weakest product skills.","col":"c"},{"label":"Tom hears a colleague's critique as another verdict on his worth as a person.","col":"a"},{"label":"Aisha treats each new client pitch as a chance to stretch skills she has never tried before.","col":"b"},{"label":"Marcus schedules daily practice sessions that feel effortful and unpleasant but measurably raise his accuracy.","col":"c"},{"label":"After a demotion, Elena tells herself the setback proves her abilities are fixed and stops volunteering for challenges.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - Priya is interpreting feedback, and growth mindset also involves interpreting feedback","c - the rejection caused her to plan harder work the next time, which is deliberate practice","a - but only if Priya also changes her portfolio, otherwise the situation shows a learning orientation"],"explanation":"Priya's response is a textbook fixed-mindset move: Dweck says people with a fixed mindset believe their abilities are carved in stone and see every encounter as a test of their worthiness, so a rejection becomes permanent proof of inadequacy rather than a signal to develop. A growth mindset would treat the same rejection as an opportunity to improve, and deliberate practice would show up as repetitive, mentally demanding training sessions - neither of which appears in this item."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Flow, Hidden Advantages, or Reward Undermining","visual_frame_description":"Three stage sets on one screen. Set one: a runner on a moonlit bridge, eyes closed, wholly in the zone, clock frozen. Set two: a hockey rink where a child's January birthday quietly changes his path while older, bigger teammates loom. Set three: an office where a gleaming bonus trophy sits, and beside it a worker's desk grows dim and empty as interest drains away. A caption asks: which book's core idea is at work?","player_mission":"Attribute each workplace or sports scenario to the book that explains it: Csikszentmihalyi's flow, Gladwell's hidden advantages, or Kohn's indictment of rewards. Drag each story to its source book and answer the featured classification.","key_concepts":["Csikszentmihalyi's Flow describes optimal experiences when we feel in control, full of purpose, and in the zone.","The best moments usually occur when a person's body or mind is stretched to the limits in a voluntary effort to accomplish something difficult and worthwhile.","Flow shows how people turn even the most unpleasant tasks into enjoyable, rewarding challenges.","Malcolm Gladwell's Outliers takes a hammer to the idea of the self-made man.","High achievers are often products of hidden advantages of culture, timing, demographics, and luck.","Gladwell's insight: it is not how much money we make but whether our work fulfills us that makes us happy between nine and five.","Alfie Kohn's Punished by Rewards indicts B. F. Skinner's do-this-and-you'll-get-that behaviorism.","Kohn's line: do rewards motivate people? Absolutely. They motivate people to get rewards.","Kohn's 1993 book ranges across school, work, and private life in its indictment of extrinsic motivators.","Autonomy, mastery, and purpose underpin the whole reading list, including these three books."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This matrix contrasts three mechanisms from the reading list: Csikszentmihalyi's flow, the optimal experience of being fully in control and in the zone; Gladwell's hidden advantages, the culture, timing, demographics, and luck that quietly separate achievers; and Kohn's reward critique, in which extrinsic motivators motivate people to get rewards rather than to love the activity. Each scenario in the matrix tests which mechanism the book's text actually claims.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Attribute each scenario: a) flow - Csikszentmihalyi's optimal experience where a person is stretched to the limits and in the zone; b) hidden advantages - Gladwell's culture, timing, demographics, and luck behind success; or c) reward undermining - Kohn's case that extrinsic motivators motivate people to get rewards.","column_a_label":"Flow (Csikszentmihalyi)","column_b_label":"Hidden advantages (Gladwell)","column_c_label":"Reward undermining (Kohn)","rows":[{"label":"A marathon runner loses track of time mid-race, feeling fully in control and in the zone.","col":"a"},{"label":"A junior analyst who loved modeling data goes quiet and disengaged after her firm starts paying a per-report bonus.","col":"c"},{"label":"A skilled hockey player owes a good slice of his career to a January birthday that landed him in the older league as a child.","col":"b"},{"label":"An accountant turns a dull reconciliation into a personal best-time challenge and feels thrilled by the work.","col":"a"},{"label":"A coding bootcamp offers a scholarship prize for each completed module, and voluntary projects stop the week the prizes end.","col":"c"},{"label":"Two equally gifted interns end up with different careers because one was mentored by a senior partner and the other was not.","col":"b"},{"label":"A potter spends an entire evening throwing imperfect bowls, absorbed, discovering that the effort itself is the reward.","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"b","distractors":["a - the birthday gave the player an early advantage that felt like being in the zone when he practiced","c - the league placement functions as a reward that motivated his early participation","b - but only if the player also trained deliberately, otherwise the case shows effort alone"],"explanation":"The January birthday is a pure hidden-advantage item: Gladwell shows how young Canadian hockey players who are old for their cohort enjoy the timing-and-demographics edge of being bigger and more developed in the same league, an accident of the calendar that feeds mastery. It is not about losing oneself in a voluntary stretching effort, as in flow, and no extrinsic motivator is being indicted, as in Kohn's reward critique."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Team of Rivals: Leading from Autonomy and Purpose","visual_frame_description":"A glass-walled design studio at dusk. Around a central table sit Megan, the sharp CFO who opposed the new CEO's promotion; Ira, the award-winning lead engineer; and Sasha, the record-breaking head of sales. The new CEO, in the corner, studies a portrait-like briefing on Abraham Lincoln's cabinet while the board's instructions - Sideline the threats - glows faintly on a wall screen.","player_mission":"Study how Doris Kearns Goodwin describes Lincoln's Type I leadership, predict how he treated rivals who outshone him, then make the two-stage decision about how to run your own firm. Beat the rival-marginializing boss battle by leading from mastery, autonomy, and purpose.","key_concepts":["Doris Kearns Goodwin's Team of Rivals shows Abraham Lincoln as an exemplar of Type I behavior.","Lincoln worked mightily to achieve mastery in law and politics.","Lincoln gave his staunchest rivals power and autonomy.","Lincoln developed a leadership style rooted in a higher purpose: ending slavery and keeping the union intact.","Lincoln was self-confident enough to surround himself with rivals who excelled in areas where he was weak.","Lincoln genuinely listened to other people's points of view, which helped him form more complex opinions of his own.","Lincoln gave credit where it was due and was not afraid to take the blame.","Autonomy, mastery, and purpose are integral to the human condition, the theme of the reading list.","Gladwell's Outliers adds that hidden advantages of culture, timing, and luck help people become masters.","Dweck's growth mindset interprets encounters as opportunities to improve rather than tests of worth."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the Type I leadership pattern Goodwin documents: mastery through relentless work, granting real power and autonomy even to staunchest rivals, and anchoring leadership in a purpose larger than the leader. The counter-intuitive mechanism is that surrounding yourself with rivals who outshine your weaknesses produces better decisions and loyalty than neutralizing threats.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is Type I leadership as documented in Goodwin's Team of Rivals: high mastery, genuine autonomy granted to capable rivals, and a higher purpose binding the team together, all of which beat the control-and-neutralize playbook of Type X management.","stage_1_observation":"You have inherited a crisis at your design firm. Like Lincoln's cabinet, your leadership team is full of brilliant rivals: Megan, the CFO who openly opposed your promotion, Ira, the award-winning engineer, and Sasha, the top-selling head of sales. The previous CEO neutralized threats by marginalizing them. The board watches. Study what Goodwin records about how Lincoln, facing an even more divided group, actually behaved toward his staunchest rivals.","stage_1_question":"According to Goodwin, what was Lincoln's documented approach to rivals who excelled in areas where he was weak?","stage_1_correct_answer":"He was self-confident enough to surround himself with rivals who excelled in areas where he was weak, and he gave them real power and autonomy - his cabinet included his fiercest opponents, and he genuinely listened to their points of view.","stage_2_intervention":"The board now expects you to sideline Megan, the CFO who opposed you, and install a loyal deputy in her place. Stage 2: which move best reproduces Lincoln's Type I leadership pattern with your own team?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Give Megan genuine authority over the budget despite her opposition, genuinely listen to her contrary points of view to form more complex opinions, and give credit where it is due while taking blame yourself - the combination Goodwin documents as Lincoln's: mastery, autonomy for rivals, and a higher purpose."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Semco Experiment: Autonomy Over Control","visual_frame_description":"An airy factory floor with no titles on any door. Workers set their own clocks, a voting board covers one wall, and a salary whiteboard shows pay set partly by the workers themselves. Ricardo Semler, sleeves rolled up, grins next to a twenty-year growth chart that climbs steadily past 20 percent, while his old corner office sits empty and small.","player_mission":"Understand Semler's radical Semco transformation, answer what the documented results were, then lead your own mid-size firm through the same autonomy overhaul when your CEO pushes bonuses instead. Win the battle by trusting self-sufficiency over control.","key_concepts":["Ricardo Semler transformed the Brazilian manufacturing firm Semco through a series of radical autonomy steps.","Semler canned most executives and eliminated job titles at Semco.","Semler let the company's three thousand employees set their own hours.","Semler gave everyone at Semco a vote in big decisions.","Semler even let some workers determine their own salaries.","Under Semler's non-command, Semco has grown 20 percent a year for the past two decades.","Semler wants everyone at Semco to be self-sufficient, not dependent on any individual, especially himself.","Maverick and its sequel The Seven-Day Weekend show how to put the iconoclastic philosophy into action.","Autonomy, mastery, and purpose are the elements the reading list explores across all fifteen books.","Senge's The Fifth Discipline adds that learning organizations deem autonomous thinking vital to their health."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The scenario tests radical autonomy as an organizational mechanism: Semler removed the control apparatus - executives, titles, fixed hours, centralized votes - and employees became self-sufficient principals, and the documented outcome was two decades of 20 percent annual growth. The book presents Semco as proof that trusting workers with real power outperforms the control-freak model of management.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is ownership through autonomy: Semler's Semco playbook - eliminate titles and executives, let employees set hours, vote on big decisions, and even set some salaries - produced 20 percent annual growth for two decades, challenging the assumption that firms need control structures to perform.","stage_1_observation":"You study Semler's transformation of Semco. He canned most executives, eliminated job titles, let the company's three thousand employees set their own hours, gave everyone a vote in big decisions, and even let some workers determine their own salaries. His office, he jokes, kept getting moved and shrinking. Watch the outcomes, then answer what Semler's experiment actually produced.","stage_1_question":"What was the documented result of Semler's autonomy experiment at Semco?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Under Semler's non-command, Semco grew 20 percent a year for the past two decades - the radical autonomy structure coexisted with relentless growth rather than undermining it.","stage_2_intervention":"You are the COO of a mid-size appliance maker with high turnover and quiet, rule-bound factories. You propose eliminating job titles and letting teams set their own hours and vote on major decisions. The CEO counters with a large cash bonus program instead. Stage 2: which plan do you advance?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Advance the autonomy package - strip the titles, let teams set their own hours and vote on major decisions, and eventually let teams weigh in on pay structures - because Semler's documented playbook treats employees as self-sufficient principals, not controllable agents, and produced two decades of 20 percent growth at Semco."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Flow at the Desk: The Deliberate-Practice Conversation","visual_frame_description":"A quiet cafe at 6 a.m. Lena, a 34-year-old data analyst, stares at a glowing laptop full of dashboards while her friend Marcus, a competitive rower in a damp training jacket, demonstrates a stroke technique with his coffee cup. Steam rises between them; a whiteboard menu shows the day's specials beneath a small poster of a river regatta.","player_mission":"Steer the conversation as Marcus helps Lena escape her dreary dashboard routine. Listen for the mechanisms from the reading list - flow, deliberate practice, finite versus infinite games - then choose the advice that matches what the books actually say about mastery.","key_concepts":["Csikszentmihalyi's Flow describes moments when we feel in control, full of purpose, and in the zone.","The best moments come when the body or mind is stretched to its limits in a voluntary effort to accomplish something difficult and worthwhile.","Geoff Colvin shows the difference between the good and the masters is deliberate practice: highly repetitive, mentally demanding, often unpleasant but undeniably effective work.","Colvin's insight: set a goal of becoming an expert and you immediately start doing all kinds of things you do not do now.","James Carse distinguishes finite games, which have a winner and an end, from infinite games, which have no winner and no end - the goal is to keep playing.","Nonwinnable games are more rewarding than the win-lose ones we are accustomed to playing at work and in relationships.","Finite players play within boundaries; infinite players play with boundaries.","John L. Parker's Once a Runner shows mastery's toll and thrill: being faster by a tenth of a second than he was the week before.","Steven Pressfield's The War of Art argues the truly free individual is free only to the extent of his own self-mastery.","Edward Deci's Why We Do What We Do: motivation is something people do, not something that gets done to them."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This dialogue pits the mastery mechanisms of the reading list against routine: deliberate practice is repetitive and demanding yet effective, flow comes when skills are stretched voluntarily, and Carse's infinite game - getting better without end - is what keeps people playing rather than finishing and stopping. The optimal advice redirects Lena from deadline-winning toward improvement-chasing, the pattern Colvin, Csikszentmihalyi, and Carse all describe.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Lena is good but stuck: every week she produces the same dashboard, nobody asks her to improve, and she dreads the repetition. Marcus, a competitive rower training for a regatta, keeps talking about how his hardest workouts are his favorite. He wants to show her that mastery and flow are available at her desk, not only on the water. Stakes: whether Lena treats her career as a finite weekly contest or an infinite game of improvement.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Marcus","line":"Honestly, the best part of my week is the 5 a.m. ergo session - the one I used to dread most. It is brutal, but I am chasing my own split times."},{"speaker":"Lena","line":"That makes no sense to me. If a workout is unpleasant, why would it be the highlight of your week?"},{"speaker":"Marcus","line":"Csikszentmihalyi got it right - these are the moments you feel in control and in the zone. I am not repeating last year's work. I deliberately practice my weakest stroke segments until they are fast."},{"speaker":"Lena","line":"My job is the opposite. Every week I produce the same dashboard. Nobody asks me to get better at it - they just want the numbers by Friday."},{"speaker":"Marcus","line":"Then set your own bar the way I do. Pick the report that annoys you most, add a skill you are bad at, and try to be one percent faster each time you build it."},{"speaker":"Lena","line":"But my manager grades me on the deliverable, not on improvement. If I spend time drilling, I miss the deadline."},{"speaker":"Marcus","line":"That is a finite game - you are trying to win the week. Carse's whole point is that the infinite game, getting better without an end, is the one that actually keeps you playing. Infinite players play with boundaries, not just within them."},{"speaker":"Lena","line":"So I should treat my own development as the goal... and let the dashboard take care of itself."},{"speaker":"Marcus","line":"Exactly. The masters are not made by the weekly win. They are made by the tenth of a second they shave off their own best, week after week."}],"choices":["Keep the dashboard exactly as it is graded - deadlines are graded and improvement is not, so mastery is a luxury you cannot afford.","Carve out a deliberate-practice slot: pick one weak skill per quarter, drill it in repetitive, demanding chunks, and track your own improvement against last quarter, not against the deadline.","Ask your manager for a raise as compensation for the boring work - money is the real solution to a motivation problem.","Transfer to a less demanding team so the work feels easy and pleasant again."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Deliberate practice (Colvin) plus flow (Csikszentmihalyi) and the infinite-game frame (Carse): mastery comes from repetitive, mentally demanding improvement work voluntarily undertaken, and optimal experience emerges when skills are stretched toward a goal of getting better.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it installs Colvin's deliberate practice - repetitive, mentally demanding work on a weak skill - inside Lena's own schedule, tracks improvement against her own prior performance the way Parker's miler races his own tenth of a second, and shifts the frame from Carse's finite weekly contest to the infinite game of getting better. Choice 0 surrenders the infinite game and keeps the deadline as the sole standard, which is exactly the routine that drains Lena; choice 2 assumes money fixes motivation, the error Deci and Kohn both attack, since the work itself remains unfulfilling; choice 3 trades challenge for comfort, but flow requires voluntary stretching, and easy work produces boredom rather than optimal experience."}]},{"id":"ch17","title":"Listen to the Gurus: Six Business Thinkers Who Get It","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Theory X, Theory Y, or Self-Management Culture","visual_frame_description":"Three conference rooms side by side. Room one: a manager's camera feed watches workers two levels down, keystrokes scrolling on a wall monitor. Room two: a supervisor hands the hardest problem to the newest hire while teammates nod. Room three: a postmortem circle where people examine a failed launch with a banner reading NO BLAME, and a red flag button sits on every desk.","player_mission":"Classify each management practice by its underlying assumption about human behavior: Theory X control, Theory Y trust, or the self-motivation culture of the gurus. Drag each scenario to its room and answer the featured classification.","key_concepts":["Douglas McGregor's 1960 book The Human Side of Enterprise gave the practice of management a badly needed shot of humanism.","The Human Side of Enterprise is a key ancestor of Motivation 3.0.","Theory X assumes people avoid effort, work only for money and security, and therefore need to be controlled.","Theory Y assumes work is as natural for human beings as play or rest, initaitive and creativity are widespread, and committed people seek responsibility.","McGregor noted that managers who keep constant surveillance over subordinates are often the same ones complaining that subordinates will not take responsibility.","Peter Drucker, who coined the term knowledge worker, called self-management the next frontier and a revolution in human affairs.","Drucker argued demanding knowledge workers define their own task and results is necessary because knowledge workers must be autonomous.","Jim Collins wrote that expending energy trying to motivate people is largely a waste of time.","Collins's four practices: lead with questions not answers, engage in dialogue and debate not coercion, conduct autopsies without blame, and build red flag mechanisms.","The gurus' guidance designs organizations that promote autonomy, mastery, and purpose."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This matrix tests McGregor's two competing assumptions about human behavior - Theory X's control of effort-avoiding, money-driven people versus Theory Y's trust in people who naturally seek responsibility - and contrasts both with the self-motivation culture built by Drucker and Collins. The chapter presents Theory Y as the more accurate and ultimately more effective approach, with self-management as the frontier Drucker described.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each management practice: a) Theory X - people avoid effort, work only for money and security, and need to be controlled; b) Theory Y - work is as natural as play, initiative and creativity are widespread, and committed people seek responsibility; or c) self-motivation culture - questions over answers, dialogue over coercion, autopsies without blame, red flag mechanisms, and self-defined tasks.","column_a_label":"Theory X control","column_b_label":"Theory Y trust","column_c_label":"Self-motivation culture","rows":[{"label":"A plant manager installs software to monitor keystrokes of staff two levels below him, claiming they will not work without it.","col":"a"},{"label":"After a failed product launch, the team runs a postmortem about causes while explicitly forbidding blame.","col":"c"},{"label":"A shift supervisor tells the crew: this job should come as naturally as rest to you - decide how to run the line and own the result.","col":"b"},{"label":"A VP replaces her answer-first briefings with questions and requires every team to debate rather than coerce.","col":"c"},{"label":"A regional head assumes his staff is lazy and will only respond to bonuses and surveillance.","col":"a"},{"label":"A team lead hands the hardest strategic problem to the newest hire and trusts commitment to spark responsibility.","col":"b"},{"label":"A senior analyst is asked to think through her own work plan and submit the results and deadlines she should be held accountable for.","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - the manager trusts the software to do the monitoring, which is a form of delegation","c - keystroke tracking creates accountability, the same mechanism as self-defined results","a - but only if the workers also receive bonuses, otherwise the motive is unclear"],"explanation":"The keystroke surveillance is pure Theory X: McGregor's Theory X manager assumes people avoid effort and work only for money and security, and therefore need to be controlled - and McGregor specifically observed that managers complain subordinates will not take responsibility while keeping constant surveillance over their day-to-day performance. Theory Y would hand over decisions and trust commitment to breed responsibility, and the self-motivation culture would ask for self-defined work plans rather than monitor keystrokes."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"ROWE at Best Buy: The Results-Only Revolution","visual_frame_description":"A Best Buy-style campus where nobody clocks in. One employee works from a beanbag with headphones, another holds a video standup from a park bench, and a wall screen shows only one metric: results. Two former HR professionals, Cali and Jody, present their ROWE charter - Every meeting is optional - above a placard reading NO WORK SCHEDULES.","player_mission":"Study the results-only work environment Ressler and Thompson built at Best Buy, answer what its tenets actually are, then defend ROWE against a schedule-and-surveillance counter-proposal in your own division. Win by letting results - not presence - define work.","key_concepts":["Cali Ressler and Jody Thompson, former human resources professionals at Best Buy, persuaded their CEO to experiment with a radical new approach to organizing work.","They wrote about their experiences in Why Work Sucks and How to Fix It.","ROWE, the results-only work environment, affords employees complete autonomy over when, where, and how they do their work.","In ROWE the only thing that matters is results.","ROWE tenet: people at all levels stop doing any activity that is a waste of their time, the customer's time, or their company's time.","ROWE tenet: employees have the freedom to work any way they want.","ROWE tenet: every meeting is optional.","ROWE tenet: there are no work schedules.","McGregor's Theory Y, a key ancestor of Motivation 3.0, assumes committed people seek responsibility rather than needing control.","The gurus offer blueprints for organizations that promote autonomy, mastery, and purpose."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the results-only work environment, ROWE: Ressler and Thompson removed schedules, required meetings, and control over when, where, and how employees work, leaving results as the only measure. The mechanism is autonomy, the same principle behind McGregor's Theory Y - trust committed people and they seek responsibility instead of needing surveillance.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is ROWE: complete autonomy over when, where, and how work happens, with results as the only thing that matters - no schedules, optional meetings, free choice of working style, and permission to kill any activity that wastes time for employees, customers, or the company.","stage_1_observation":"You study the ROWE experiment that Ressler and Thompson persuaded Best Buy's CEO to run. In the results-only work environment, employees get complete autonomy over when, where, and how they do their work, and the only thing that matters is results. Observe the charter they posted, then answer which practices actually belong to the model.","stage_1_question":"Which set of practices belongs to the ROWE model tested at Best Buy?","stage_1_correct_answer":"There are no work schedules; every meeting is optional; employees have the freedom to work any way they want; and people at all levels stop doing any activity that wastes their time, the customer's time, or their company's time - results are the only thing that matters.","stage_2_intervention":"You now run a claims division of 400 people. Your VP proposes attendance tracking badges and mandatory daily stand-ups to lift productivity. You propose rolling out ROWE instead. Stage 2: which move do you make?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Roll out ROWE as Ressler and Thompson designed it: drop the work schedules and mandatory meetings, give teams complete freedom over when, where, and how they work, judge only results, and let employees at every level eliminate activities that waste their time, the customer's time, or the company's time."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Culture Debate: Collins's Four Practices","visual_frame_description":"A call-center floor at 8 a.m. VP Dana, recently hired to turn the operation around, stands with a whiteboard marker before Director Theo, arms crossed. Behind them, agents at rows of desks avoid eye contact while a wall speaker crackles with the daily answer-key briefing. A single red flag sits unused in a corner box.","player_mission":"Mediate the culture debate between Dana and Theo. Guide Theo away from answer-keys and blame storms toward Collins's four practices for self-motivated culture, and pick the response that matches what the gurus actually prescribe.","key_concepts":["Jim Collins: expending energy trying to motivate people is largely a waste of time.","If you have the right people on the bus, they will be self-motivated, and the real question becomes how to manage so as not to de-motivate them.","Collins practice one: lead with questions, not answers.","Collins practice two: engage in dialogue and debate, not coercion.","Collins practice three: conduct autopsies, without blame.","Collins practice four: build red flag mechanisms that make it easy for employees and customers to speak up when they identify a problem.","Drucker argued knowledge workers must be autonomous and should think through their own work plans.","McGregor's Theory Y: work is as natural as play or rest and committed people seek responsibility.","The gurus' blueprints promote organizations built around autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","Hamel adds that management tools invented in the 19th century deserve radical overhaul."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This dialogue tests Jim Collins's self-motivation culture: with the right people on the bus, the manager's job is not to motivate but to avoid de-motivating, by leading with questions, debating instead of coercing, running blame-free autopsies, and building red flag mechanisms. The mechanism is autonomy - treating employees as capable principals whose speaking up and owning mistakes is engineered into the culture rather than suppressed.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"VP Dana is brought in to turn around a call center where managers deliver answer keys every Monday, failures end in blame storms, and nobody dares flag problems. She has read Jim Collins and wants to change the culture; Director Theo, a sixteen-year veteran, believes people here need direction. Stakes: whether the center shifts from a coercion machine to a culture where self-motivation can flourish.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Dana","line":"Collins wrote that expending energy trying to motivate people is largely a waste of time. Our problem is not lazy agents - it is a culture that de-motivates them."},{"speaker":"Theo","line":"We tried team building, we tried bonuses. People here need direction. Every week starts with an answer key from me, and call quality depends on it."},{"speaker":"Dana","line":"So you hand them answers instead of questions. Collins's first practice is to lead with questions, not answers - let them think the work through."},{"speaker":"Theo","line":"And when a project fails? I hold the postmortem. Someone has to own the mistake, or nothing ever changes."},{"speaker":"Dana","line":"Conduct autopsies, without blame. If a failed launch becomes a blame storm, the only thing people learn is how to hide problems."},{"speaker":"Theo","line":"You want me to ask agents what is wrong and then do nothing when they say it?"},{"speaker":"Dana","line":"Build red flag mechanisms - make it easy for employees and customers to speak up when they identify a problem. Then actually act on what they tell you."},{"speaker":"Theo","line":"...So my job becomes asking questions, absorbing the blame, and listening to flags?"},{"speaker":"Dana","line":"That is the job. If you have the right people on the bus, they will be self-motivated - your job is to manage in such a way as not to de-motivate them."}],"choices":["Reassure Theo the answer key stays, but add a suggestion box so agents feel heard.","Commit to Collins's four practices: open every meeting with questions, replace monologues with genuine debate, run blame-free autopsies, and institute a real red flag channel that feeds decisions.","Replace the answer key with a weekly awards ceremony for the best call numbers.","Outsource problem detection to an external consultant so managers keep their authority intact."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Collins's self-motivation culture: lead with questions not answers, engage in dialogue and debate not coercion, conduct autopsies without blame, and build red flag mechanisms - grounded in the claim that right people on the bus are self-motivated and the manager's task is not to de-motivate them.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it installs all four mechanisms Collins prescribes, each aimed at preserving autonomy: questions invite agents to own the work, debate replaces coercion, blame-free autopsies make failure a learning event rather than a threat, and red flag mechanisms engineer speaking up into the culture. Choice 0 fails because it keeps the answer-key authority structure and adds only a passive suggestion box, which does not change the de-motivating dynamic Dana diagnosed; choice 2 replaces one extrinsic ritual with another, praising outputs without changing how work is owned; choice 3 outsources the listening, which removes the very autonomy Collins says employees need and tells them their voice has no direct route into action."}]},{"id":"ch18","title":"The Type I Fitness Plan: Four Tips for Getting (and Staying) Motivated to Exercise","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Extrinsic Goals, Intrinsic Goals, or If-Then Self-Bribes","visual_frame_description":"Three exercise scenes on one screen. Scene one: a wedding photo album looms over a grim runner on a treadmill counting calories. Scene two: a parent and child sprint through a park, laughing, mid-tag-game. Scene three: a shopping cart with a new jacket floats above a notebook reading IF I WORK OUT FOUR TIMES, THEN I BUY. A caption asks: what drives this person?","player_mission":"Classify each exerciser's goal as extrinsic, intrinsic, or an if-then self-bribe, matching the chapter's research findings. Drag each fitness story to the right category and answer the featured classification.","key_concepts":["People who seek to lose weight for extrinsic reasons - to slim down for a wedding or look better at a class reunion - often reach their goals.","Extrinsic goal-setters then gain the weight back as soon as the target event ends.","People who pursue intrinsic goals - to get fit in order to feel good or stay healthy for their family - make slower progress at first.","Intrinsic goal-setters achieve significantly better results in the long term.","The Type I way is to set your own goals rather than accept a standardized, cookie-cutter exercise plan.","You can work with a professional on your plan, but you make the final calls.","If-then self-bribes - if I exercise four times this week, then I'll buy myself a new shirt - can backfire.","Occasional now-that rewards are not a problem: if you have swum the distance you hoped to this week, a massage afterward will not hurt.","Ample research in behavioral science supports the extrinsic-versus-intrinsic goal finding.","Running can contain all the elements of Type I behavior: autonomy, mastery, and purpose."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the goal-origin effect documented in the chapter: event-driven extrinsic goals such as a wedding deadline produce impressive short-term progress and rebound once the event passes, while intrinsic goals of feeling good or staying healthy for family progress more slowly at first yet win in the long term. The mechanism is the familiar contrast between compliance-oriented and autonomy-oriented motivation applied to exercise.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Classify each exerciser's drive: a) extrinsic goal - slimming down for an event like a wedding or class reunion; b) intrinsic goal - feeling good or staying healthy for family; or c) if-then self-bribe - a contingent promise to buy something after completing workouts.","column_a_label":"Extrinsic event goal","column_b_label":"Intrinsic goal","column_c_label":"If-then self-bribe","rows":[{"label":"Rafael resolves to run five miles by June so he photographs well at his brother's wedding.","col":"a"},{"label":"Mei trains so she can keep playing tag with her kids without getting winded.","col":"b"},{"label":"Leo promises himself: if I hit the gym four times this week, I will order a new jacket.","col":"c"},{"label":"Sam wants to drop ten pounds before the class reunion he has dreaded since graduation.","col":"a"},{"label":"Priya lifts weights so she can feel stronger, sleep better, and stay healthy for her family.","col":"b"},{"label":"Nina tells herself: if I swim three mornings this week, I will treat myself to a latte every single day next week.","col":"c"},{"label":"Dana books a massage after swimming the distance she hoped for this week, with no condition promised in advance.","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["b - wedding training made Rafael feel good about himself, which is an intrinsic benefit","c - the race deadline is a promise Rafael made to himself, like an if-then deal","a - but only if the wedding photos are for his own album, otherwise the goal is intrinsic"],"explanation":"Rafael's June deadline is a textbook extrinsic event goal: the chapter says people who seek to lose weight to slim down for a wedding or look better at a class reunion often reach their goals and then gain the weight back once the target event ends, exactly the rebound pattern the research describes. It is not an intrinsic goal of feeling good or staying healthy for family, and no contingent shopping deal was promised in advance, so it does not qualify as an if-then self-bribe."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Treadmill Rebellion: Flow Over Obligation","visual_frame_description":"A dim gym at 9 p.m. Omar, in a faded t-shirt, trudges on a treadmill staring at a wall clock while a glowing screen beside him shows three alternate worlds: a tennis court with laughing friends, a park trail at golden hour, and a living room dance session with kids. A caption reads: dreary obligation or intoxicating flow?","player_mission":"Help Omar escape his dreary gym obligation, predict what the chapter prescribes, and then keep his motivation alive six months later. Beat the obligation treadmill at both stages by applying the Sawyer Effect, flow, and the Goldilocks rule to his fitness.","key_concepts":["If trudging to the gym feels like a dreary obligation, find a form of fitness you enjoy that produces those intoxicating moments of flow.","The chapter suggests gathering friends for an informal game of tennis or basketball, joining an amateur league, or going for walks at a local park.","Dancing for a half-hour or playing with your kids are also recommended flow-producing options.","Use the Sawyer Effect to your advantage and turn your work(out) into play.","Getting better at something provides a great source of renewable energy.","Pick an activity in which you can improve over time.","By continually increasing the difficulty of what you take on - think Goldilocks - you can renew that energy and stay motivated.","Set more audacious challenges for yourself as time passes.","Running can have all the elements of Type I behavior: autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","People who keep at exercise and enjoy it most often run toward a greater purpose - testing their limits or staying healthy and vital."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the obligation-versus-flow mechanism in fitness: an activity pursued as a dreary duty drains motivation, while a self-chosen form producing flow - informal games, leagues, park walks, dancing, play - sustains it through the Sawyer Effect that turns work into play. The second stage tests mastery as renewable energy, with the Goldilocks rule of continually rising difficulty keeping engagement alive.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The central concept is choosing flow over obligation: ditch the dreary gym trudge for a form of fitness that produces flow, use the Sawyer Effect to make the workout feel like play, and then keep mastery in mind by raising difficulty in Goldilocks-sized steps so improvement becomes renewable energy.","stage_1_observation":"Omar drags himself to the gym treadmill three nights a week, stares at the wall clock, and has hated every minute for two years - yet he keeps the routine out of duty. He tells you the gym feels like a dreary obligation and asks what the chapter's plan would have him do instead. Watch his options appear on the glowing screen.","stage_1_question":"According to the chapter's tips, what should Omar switch to when his treadmill routine feels like a dreary obligation?","stage_1_correct_answer":"A form of fitness he actually enjoys that produces those intoxicating moments of flow - the chapter recommends informal tennis or basketball with friends, an amateur league, walks at a local park, dancing for a half-hour, or playing with his kids, using the Sawyer Effect to turn his workout into play.","stage_2_intervention":"Six months later Omar loves his weekly tennis games and is getting bored with casual rallies, so he asks how to keep the motivation going now that the novelty has faded. Stage 2: which move does the chapter recommend next?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Keep mastery in mind: pick a concrete skill within the sport to improve, continually raise the difficulty - the Goldilocks rule, not too hard and not too easy - and set progressively audacious challenges over time, so improving itself becomes the renewable energy that sustains his motivation."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Motivation Swap: If-Then vs. Now-That Rewards","visual_frame_description":"A park bench at sunrise. Amara, a marketing manager, sits hunched over a training spreadsheet titled Half-Marathon Countdown while her colleague Ben jogs in place beside her, high-fiving his young daughter who bikes a lap around the fountain. A cracked watch ad banner flutters in the breeze behind them.","player_mission":"Guide Amara through her fitness burnout. Listen for the contrast between her event-driven if-then deal and Ben's intrinsic, now-that approach, then choose the response that follows the chapter's research and its reward guidance.","key_concepts":["People who pursue weight goals for extrinsic reasons - a wedding or a class reunion - often reach the goals and then gain the weight back when the event ends.","People who pursue intrinsic goals - to get fit to feel good or stay healthy for their family - make slower progress at first but achieve significantly better long-term results.","If-then self-bribes - if I exercise four times this week, then I'll buy myself a new shirt - can backfire.","Occasional now-that rewards are not a problem: a massage after swimming the distance you hoped for will not hurt.","Set your own goals: create a plan tailored to your needs and fitness level, and you make the final calls.","Find a form of fitness you enjoy that produces those intoxicating moments of flow.","The Sawyer Effect turns your workout into play.","Keep mastery in mind: pick an activity in which you can improve over time.","Continually increase the difficulty of what you take on - think Goldilocks - and set more audacious challenges as time passes.","People who keep at exercise and enjoy it most often run toward a greater purpose: testing their limits or staying healthy and vital."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"The dialogue forces the goal-origin mechanism: Amara's event-driven half-marathon contract follows the extrinsic pattern of reaching the goal and rebounding, while Ben's intrinsic purpose - staying healthy and vital for his daughter - follows the slower-but-lasting trajectory the chapter's research describes. The reward layer tests the if-then versus now-that distinction, where contingent promises backfire and unconditional treats afterward do no harm.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"Amara, a marketing manager, has signed up for a half-marathon tied to a brand event and promised herself a new watch if she completes every scheduled run until then. She has missed two runs this week and is burning out. Ben, a colleague who trains to keep up with his daughter, invites her on park runs. Stakes: whether Amara keeps bouncing from event to event or builds motivation that outlasts the finish line.","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Amara","line":"Six weeks until the brand-event half-marathon. If I hit every scheduled run until then, I have promised myself a new watch. I have already missed two runs this week."},{"speaker":"Ben","line":"A watch? You are negotiating with yourself. What happens after the half-marathon?"},{"speaker":"Amara","line":"Then the deal resets. I did the same thing for my cousin's wedding last year - trained hard, hit the goal, and put everything back on in three months."},{"speaker":"Ben","line":"That is exactly the pattern the research warns about. Wedding, reunion - event-driven goals get you to the event. Then the engine switches off."},{"speaker":"Amara","line":"So why do I keep setting those goals?"},{"speaker":"Ben","line":"Because they are easy to measure. But the people who stay with it train because the run makes them feel good, or keeps them healthy for the people who depend on them. Slower start, better results in the long term."},{"speaker":"Amara","line":"So I should just... run because I like it? My job made me forget I ever did. Actually - that park loop we did with your daughter on Sunday? I finished feeling like I had won something."},{"speaker":"Ben","line":"There you go. And drop the watch deal - those if-then promises backfire. But if you swim the distance you hoped for this week, a massage afterwards? That is a now-that reward. It will not hurt. It might feel good."}],"choices":["Keep the if-then watch deal - it is the only thing that has ever gotten you to six planned runs in a week.","Reframe your goal: train to feel strong and stay healthy for your daughter, keep the race as a challenge rather than a contract, and celebrate each week's distance with a now-that reward like a massage instead of an if-then prize.","Quit the scheduled runs entirely and only run when you feel like it, so there is never any obligation again.","Move the goalpost: defer the reward until after the event and double the jackpot if you finish."],"optimal_choice":1,"theoretical_principle":"Intrinsic versus extrinsic goal pursuit, plus the if-then versus now-that reward distinction: event-driven extrinsic goals produce rebounds once the event ends, intrinsic goals of feeling good and staying healthy progress slower but win long-term, contingent if-then promises backfire, and unconditional now-that rewards are harmless.","psychological_breakdown":"Choice 1 works because it moves Amara from the extrinsic event contract to the intrinsic purpose the research favors - feeling good and staying healthy for family - while keeping the race as a self-chosen challenge, and it swaps the backfiring if-then watch for a now-that massage, which the chapter explicitly endorses as not a problem. Choice 0 keeps the contingent deal that the chapter says can backfire and has already failed her once before, at the wedding; choice 2 confuses structure with obligation, discarding the scheduled training that gives her progress and mastery; choice 3 doubles down on the contingent jackpot, making the reward bigger without changing the extrinsic engine, and postponing gratification strengthens the very deal structure most likely to collapse after the event."}]},{"id":"ch19","title":"Drive: The Recap","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Three Elements: Autonomy, Mastery, and Purpose","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen sorting board appears, with three labeled crates across the top: Autonomy, Mastery, and Purpose. Six statement cards drift in from the bottom, each carrying either a verbatim definition from the recap or a concrete everyday moment, such as a nurse volunteering for extra training. The player drags each card into the crate it belongs to while a progress bar tracks correct sorts.","player_mission":"Read each statement card and classify it as autonomy, mastery, or purpose exactly as the recap defines them. Sort every card correctly to complete the matrix and unlock the feedback panel.","key_concepts":["Drive's recap defines autonomy as the desire to direct our own lives.","Drive's recap defines mastery as the urge to get better and better at something that matters.","Drive's recap defines purpose as the yearning to do what we do in the service of something larger than ourselves.","The recap presents autonomy, mastery, and purpose as the three essential elements of the upgraded approach to motivation.","The recap argues there is a gap between what science knows about motivation and what business does.","The recap claims the current business operating system, built around external, carrot-and-stick motivators, doesn't work and often does harm.","Drive's one-line summary brands carrots and sticks as so last century for twenty-first-century work.","The book offers multiple summaries — a Twitter-length version and a cocktail-party version — as talking points for recall.","The three-element upgrade is offered as the replacement operating system for twenty-first-century work.","Choosing where to focus one's own effort is an everyday expression of the desire to direct one's own life."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This classification task tests the three named elements of intrinsic motivation from Drive's recap — autonomy, mastery, and purpose — each carrying distinct psychological content: self-direction, improving at something that matters, and serving something larger than oneself. The distinctions come from the book's popular-science synthesis of Deci and Ryan's self-determination research rather than from controlled experiments in this chapter.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Read each statement from the recap or from everyday life and sort it into the element it best describes: autonomy, mastery, or purpose.","column_a_label":"Autonomy — the desire to direct our own lives","column_b_label":"Mastery — the urge to get better and better at something that matters","column_c_label":"Purpose — the yearning to serve something larger than ourselves","rows":[{"label":"The desire to direct our own lives","col":"a"},{"label":"The urge to get better and better at something that matters","col":"b"},{"label":"The yearning to do what we do in the service of something larger than ourselves","col":"c"},{"label":"Priya chooses which open-source bug to fix tonight instead of taking a pre-assigned ticket","col":"a"},{"label":"Marcus records his daily Spanish practice to watch himself improve at something that matters to him","col":"b"},{"label":"The night-shift nurses volunteer for extra training because it helps their patients recover faster","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Mastery is the desire to direct our own lives, since self-direction is a skill you get better at.","Getting better at Spanish is an expression of purpose because improvement serves the larger goal of fluency.","Purpose is simply another word for job satisfaction and carries no motivational content of its own."],"explanation":"Rows one to three are the recap's verbatim definitions, so each sorts to its own column. Rows four to six are behavioral translations: choosing your own work expresses the desire to direct your own life (autonomy), practicing to improve at something that matters expresses the urge to get better (mastery), and working to help patients recover expresses service to something larger than yourself (purpose). The correct answer for the lead row is 'a' because desiring to direct our own lives is the recap's exact definition of autonomy."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Carrot-and-Stick Operating System vs. the Upgrade","visual_frame_description":"A warehouse floor at Meridian Logistics scrolls into view: digital bonus boards glow above the sorting lines, penalty notices flash on forklift screens, and workers move fast but robotic. A voice-over from the CEO, Dana Whitfield, walks the player through her metrics dashboard while damage reports pile up on the quality desk in the foreground.","player_mission":"Diagnose why the carrot-and-stick incentive system is damaging work quality, then choose the operating-system upgrade the recap actually recommends. Get both stages right to defeat the boss.","key_concepts":["The recap claims the current business operating system is built around external, carrot-and-stick motivators.","The recap argues that this operating system doesn't work and often does harm.","The recap says there is a gap between what science knows about motivation and what business does.","Autonomy is defined as the desire to direct our own lives.","Mastery is defined as the urge to get better and better at something that matters.","Purpose is defined as the yearning to do what we do in the service of something larger than ourselves.","The upgrade replaces external motivators with autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","Contingent incentives focus effort on measurable targets rather than on the judgment a task actually demands.","Fair baseline pay plus self-direction is the shape of the upgrade the recap describes."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the recap's central claim that an operating system built on external, carrot-and-stick motivators doesn't work and often does harm, and that the science-backed upgrade is autonomy, mastery, and purpose. The claim is a popular-science synthesis of self-determination research rather than a controlled experiment reported in this chapter.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the recap's central argument: the current business operating system is built around external, carrot-and-stick motivators, and by the science's account it doesn't work and often does harm — the upgrade being autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","stage_1_observation":"You are a work-design consultant called into Meridian Logistics, a mid-sized shipping firm in Columbus. CEO Dana Whitfield runs the floor on commission targets, a public bonus board, and penalty clauses for missed deliveries. Watch the warehouse for a week: workers hit the numbers, but fragile boxes arrive damaged, the easiest parcels are cherry-picked first, and two veteran employees quietly put out feelers to competitors.","stage_1_question":"Dana's metrics improved on paper, yet quality collapsed. Which diagnosis best matches the recap's argument about why the carrot-and-stick approach fails?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The bonuses and fines are exactly the external, carrot-and-stick motivators the recap describes; an operating system built on them doesn't work and often does harm — here it focused effort on gamed, measurable targets while eroding the judgment and care the work actually needs. The correct diagnosis is that the reward system itself, not the workers, is the malfunctioning component.","stage_2_intervention":"Dana demands an upgrade and is weighing three options: (A) double the bonus pool and add steeper fines to reset behavior; (B) keep pay at a fair baseline, then give teams freedom over what they work on, when, and how, while tying the work to a larger mission such as delivering time-critical medicine; (C) launch a mandatory engagement seminar with pay tied to attendance. Which intervention do you recommend?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option B. The recap's upgrade is the three elements — autonomy (the desire to direct our own lives), mastery (the urge to get better at something that matters), and purpose (doing the work in service of something larger) — built on a fair baseline and self-direction. Option A just adds more of the carrot-and-stick dynamic that damaged quality, and option C attaches a contingent reward to attendance, which is another if-then-style control rather than an upgrade."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Explaining the Science-to-Business Gap at a Party","visual_frame_description":"A rooftop party in Austin at dusk: string lights, a skyline behind the railing, and your neighbor Raoul, an operations director in a rumpled blazer, gesturing with a glass of sangria while his wife Marta watches with amusement. He leans in, clearly wanting a straight answer he can use on Monday.","player_mission":"Explain Drive's upgrade to Raoul in terms he can bring to work: name what the science says, what the old operating system gets wrong, and what he should actually change Monday morning. Choose the reply that matches the recap's claims.","key_concepts":["There is a gap between what science knows about motivation and what business does.","Our current business operating system is built around external, carrot-and-stick motivators.","The recap claims the old operating system doesn't work and often does harm.","Autonomy is the desire to direct our own lives.","Mastery is the urge to get better and better at something that matters.","Purpose is the yearning to do what we do in the service of something larger than ourselves.","The upgrade replaces external motivators with autonomy, mastery, and purpose.","Carrots and sticks are described in the book's one-line summary as so last century for twenty-first-century work.","Contingent incentives on complex, judgment-driven work tend to shift effort onto gamed targets.","The recap is offered as talking points for exactly this kind of conversational summary."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the shift from Motivation 2.0's external carrot-and-stick operating system to the three-element intrinsic framework of autonomy, mastery, and purpose that the recap prescribes. The recap's claim that the old system doesn't work and often does harm rests on a popular-science synthesis of Deci and Ryan's self-determination research.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"At a rooftop party in Austin, your neighbor Raoul — an operations director at a consumer-goods company — has just heard a podcast claiming the science of motivation has left business behind. Intrigued but skeptical, he corners you with a glass of sangria and asks what a psychology book would tell his firm about why its bonus scheme keeps backfiring. The stakes: he is genuinely curious, but he will dismiss anything that does not sound implementable by Monday morning.","dialogue":["Raoul: HQ doubled the quarterly bonus after we missed target three months straight. Now my team games the pipeline instead of fixing it. Bigger carrot, same rot — that's the thing I can't explain.","Marta: Raoul, he's the one who reads the behavioral-science stuff. This is exactly the conversation you've been dodging.","Raoul: Fine. If the old carrot-and-stick operating system is broken, what does the upgrade actually look like?","Marta: Keep it short — he has a deadline in forty-eight hours.","Raoul: And don't hand me be nicer — I need mechanics.","Raoul: So: what do I change Monday morning, and why would the science say it works? Give me the real version, not the brochure."],"choices":["The book's upgrade has three pieces: autonomy — let people direct their own work; mastery — give them real chances to get better at something that matters; and purpose — connect the work to something larger than the pipeline. Start by dropping the fine-grained controls and see what they do with the freedom.","Keep the bonus but add a second, bigger bonus at the end of the year — the modern version of the system is just more of it, layered.","There's nothing wrong with carrots and sticks. Your real problem is a lazy team — double down with daily audits and public rankings.","Buy everyone a copy of the book and make attendance at a weekly book club mandatory with a quiz; that's the upgrade the author recommends."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The recap's three-element upgrade — autonomy, mastery, and purpose — replaces an external, carrot-and-stick operating system that doesn't work and often does harm.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 0 names all three elements the recap identifies — autonomy, mastery, and purpose — directly answering Raoul's question about mechanics while connecting it to the gap between what science knows and what business does. Option 1 stays inside the carrot-and-stick operating system, layering more contingent rewards of the same type the recap indicts. Option 2 blames the workers rather than the system, ignoring the recap's claim that the external system itself often does harm. Option 3 turns a learning resource into a mandatory, quiz-graded obligation, reintroducing the contingent control that the upgrade is designed to remove."}]},{"id":"ch20","title":"Drive: The Glossary","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Sorting the Glossary by Operating System","visual_frame_description":"A digital whiteboard shows three long shelves labeled Motivation 2.0, Motivation 3.0, and Compensation Baseline. Glossary definition cards float up one at a time — a FedEx Day, an if-then reward, a results-only work environment — and the player must shelve each under the category the book's operating-system vocabulary supports.","player_mission":"Read each glossary term and sort it into the category that best represents the motivational operating system it belongs to, or the compensation baseline where it sits. Classify all six terms to finish the matrix.","key_concepts":["Motivation 2.0 presumes that humans respond to rewards and punishments in their environment.","Motivation 3.0 presumes that humans also have a third drive — to learn, to create, and to better the world.","If-then rewards are rewards offered as contingencies: if you do this, then you'll get that.","If-then rewards can sometimes be effective for routine tasks.","For creative, conceptual tasks, if-then rewards invariably do more harm than good.","Now-that rewards are offered after a task is complete and are less perilous for nonroutine tasks, though still tricky.","Baseline rewards — salary, contract payments, benefits, and a few perks — represent the floor for compensation.","If baseline rewards are inadequate or inequitable, motivation of any sort becomes extremely difficult.","FedEx Days, created by Atlassian, are one-day bursts of autonomy in which employees show results within twenty-four hours.","A results-only work environment keeps no schedules; employees just have to get their work done.","20 percent time lets employees spend one day in five working on any project they choose."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This sorting task tests the glossary's operating-system vocabulary: Motivation 2.0 relies on contingent external rewards and punishments, while Motivation 3.0 presumes an intrinsic third drive to learn, create, and better the world — with baseline compensation as the floor that must be met before either operates. The scheme is a popular-science synthesis of Deci and Ryan's self-determination research.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Each glossary term belongs to one of three categories: the Motivation 2.0 operating system, the Motivation 3.0 operating system, or the compensation baseline. Sort each term into its best-fitting category.","column_a_label":"Motivation 2.0 — the external rewards-and-punishments operating system","column_b_label":"Motivation 3.0 — the intrinsic third-drive operating system","column_c_label":"Compensation baseline — the floor for pay, benefits, and perks","rows":[{"label":"If you do this, then you'll get that — rewards offered as contingencies","col":"a"},{"label":"Atlassian's FedEx Days, where employees tackle any problem they want and show results within twenty-four hours","col":"b"},{"label":"Salary, contract payments, benefits, and a few perks that represent the floor for compensation","col":"c"},{"label":"A results-only work environment in which employees have no schedules and just have to get their work done","col":"b"},{"label":"20 percent time, in which employees can spend one day in five on any project they choose","col":"b"},{"label":"Now that you've done such a great job, let's acknowledge the achievement — rewards offered after the fact","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["A FedEx Day belongs under Motivation 2.0 because its name demands overnight delivery, just like a deadline bonus.","Baseline rewards belong under Motivation 3.0 because the book says to pay people the Type I way.","20 percent time belongs in the compensation baseline because it is a form of paid work time."],"explanation":"Motivation 2.0 presumes humans respond to rewards and punishments, so its currency is contingent rewards: if-then rewards, which work sometimes on routine tasks and harm creative work, and now-that rewards, which are less perilous for nonroutine tasks but remain external incentives. Motivation 3.0 presumes a third drive to learn, create, and better the world, which is what FedEx Days, 20 percent time, and a ROWE all serve by granting self-direction. Baseline rewards are neither operating system; they are the compensation floor that must be adequate and equitable before motivation of any sort becomes possible. The correct answer for the lead row is 'a' because an if-then contingency is the defining tool of the Motivation 2.0 operating system."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"If-Then Rewards on Creative Work: The Quota Trap","visual_frame_description":"A design studio at a software firm: whiteboards covered in feature sketches, a burndown chart on the wall, and VP Priya Raman staring at a dashboard that reads Sixteen Shipped Features This Quarter. A designer at her desk splits a feature card into slivers with a resigned shrug while the flagship rebuild sits untouched in the corner.","player_mission":"Diagnose why the quota bonus made the design team game the metric, then choose the fix the glossary supports. Defeat the boss by identifying the routine-versus-nonroutine distinction and applying it.","key_concepts":["Nonroutine work is creative, conceptual, right-brain work that can't be reduced to a set of rules.","Routine work can be reduced to a script, a spec sheet, a formula, or a set of instructions.","External rewards can be effective in motivating routine tasks.","For creative, conceptual tasks, if-then rewards invariably do more harm than good.","Now-that rewards are less perilous for nonroutine tasks than if-then rewards, though still tricky.","If baseline rewards are inadequate or inequitable, motivation of any sort becomes extremely difficult.","Routine, rule-based work has become easier to send offshore and to automate.","The Goldilocks task is the sweet spot where tasks are neither too easy nor too hard.","Goldilocks tasks are essential to reaching the state of flow and to achieving mastery.","If-then rewards require people to forfeit some of their autonomy."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This battle tests the glossary's central contingency rule: if-then rewards can be effective for routine tasks but invariably do more harm than good for creative, conceptual tasks, while now-that rewards are less perilous — provided baseline rewards are adequate and equitable. The rule comes from the book's synthesis of research on how contingent rewards can undermine intrinsic motivation.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the glossary's key distinction: if-then rewards can sometimes work for routine tasks but invariably do more harm than good for creative, conceptual (nonroutine) work, with now-that rewards as the less perilous alternative once baseline pay is fair.","stage_1_observation":"Priya Raman, VP of product at a design-software firm, has tied the design team's bonus to a quota: ship sixteen features this quarter and get 10 percent of salary. Watch the studio: designers split features into the smallest shippable slivers to inflate their count, defer the ambitious rebuild they were once excited about, and the flagship project falls a month behind. Priya stares at the burndown chart, wondering why more money produced less of what she wanted.","stage_1_question":"Why did the quota bonus backfire with the design team, in the glossary's terms?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The designers do nonroutine work — creative, conceptual, right-brain work that cannot be reduced to a set of rules — and for such tasks the glossary says if-then rewards invariably do more harm than good; the quota pushed them to game the metric instead of pursuing ambitious work. The correct diagnosis names the routine-versus-nonroutine distinction rather than blaming the team's effort.","stage_2_intervention":"Priya weighs three options: (A) raise the bonus to 20 percent and keep the quota; (B) scrap the quota, keep salary at an adequate and equitable baseline, and give the team autonomy over which problems to tackle, with a now-that acknowledgment after the flagship ships; (C) convert the work into a documented step-by-step process so the quota system can apply cleanly. Which option does the glossary support, and why?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option B. The glossary says baseline rewards must be adequate and equitable or motivation of any sort becomes extremely difficult, and now-that rewards — though tricky — are less perilous for nonroutine tasks than if-then rewards. Option C treats creative work as routine by squeezing it into a rule-based script, which misclassifies the work, and option A deepens the motivational damage of the contingent quota."},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Sawyer Effect in the Backyard","visual_frame_description":"A sunny Saturday in a Minneapolis suburb: a backyard strewn with branches, an eleven-year-old girl in a red cap, and two adults at a picnic table — Josh, holding a pair of twenty-dollar bills, and his friend Salma with a book-club copy of Drive. The kids' energy visibly drains as they start debating payments.","player_mission":"Help Josh understand what happened to his daughter's enthusiasm and choose the response that restores it. Apply the Sawyer Effect's two sides — rewards turning play into work, and mastery focus turning work into play.","key_concepts":["The Sawyer Effect has a negative aspect: rewards can turn play into work.","The Sawyer Effect has a positive aspect: focusing on mastery can turn work into play.","The effect draws its name from the scene in The Adventures of Tom Sawyer in which Tom and friends whitewash Aunt Polly's fence.","Baseline rewards represent the floor for compensation — salary, contract payments, benefits, and a few perks.","If baseline rewards are inadequate or inequitable, motivation of any sort becomes extremely difficult.","If-then rewards are rewards offered as contingencies: if you do this, then you'll get that.","If-then rewards require people to forfeit some of their autonomy.","Type I behavior is a way of thinking built around intrinsic rather than extrinsic motivators.","The mastery asymptote — the knowledge that full mastery can never be realized — is what makes its pursuit alluring and frustrating.","Goldilocks tasks, neither too easy nor too hard, are essential to reaching flow and achieving mastery."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This scenario tests the Sawyer Effect — the negative side, where a contingent reward converts an intrinsically enjoyed activity into paid labor, and the positive side, where framing an activity around mastery restores engagement. The glossary presents the effect as a weird behavioral alchemy, consistent with the broader claim that contingent rewards can turn play into work.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"scenario_setup":"On a Saturday morning in a Minneapolis suburb, Josh Okonkwo watches his eleven-year-old daughter Zadie and her friends happily begin clearing brush from the backyard — until he makes a well-meaning mistake. He offers twenty dollars to clear the lot by noon, and the fun evaporates. He wants to support her interest without smothering it, and his book-club friend Salma has just read the Drive glossary.","dialogue":["Salma: Josh, you've got exactly the setup the glossary talks about — they were already playing at the work.","Josh: Zadie and her friends were hauling branches like it was a game. Then I jumped in with clear the lot by noon and I'll pay you twenty dollars.","Salma: And what happened?","Josh: Twenty minutes later they were arguing about how many trips they'd made and whether the twenty covered extra branches. The fun evaporated.","Salma: That's the effect they named after Tom Sawyer's fence — the negative side. A reward just turned their play into work.","Josh: So how do I get it back without becoming a stingy dad?","Salma: Think about what the glossary says the positive side of the effect is — then tell Josh what you'd do."],"choices":["The positive side of the Sawyer Effect is that focusing on mastery can turn work into play. Reframe the clearing as a chance to get better at something — treat it as craftsmanship, let the kids choose their own tools and order of attack, and acknowledge their effort afterward instead of dangling the money up front.","Raise the offer — twenty dollars was too cheap. Pay forty and the enthusiasm will come back, because kids respond to bigger carrots.","Forbid them from helping at all until they beg to do it again; scarcity will restore the novelty and their intrinsic interest.","Draw up a written contract listing penalties for stopping early; the glossary says clear contingencies make work more enjoyable."],"optimal_choice":0,"theoretical_principle":"The Sawyer Effect in both directions: rewards can turn play into work, while focusing on mastery can turn work into play.","psychological_breakdown":"Option 0 applies the positive side of the Sawyer Effect — reframing the chore around mastery and self-direction, with acknowledgment (a now-that-style gesture) rather than an up-front if-then contingency, restoring the intrinsic frame the offer destroyed. Option 1 amplifies the same contingent reward that turned play into work, deepening the negative side of the effect. Option 2 uses external control to manufacture scarcity, which does not restore enjoyment and makes the activity a punishment. Option 3 inverts the glossary's lesson by adding contingencies and penalties to an intrinsically enjoyable task, guaranteeing the play becomes work."}]},{"id":"ch21","title":"The Drive Discussion Guide: Twenty Conversation Starters to Keep You Thinking ...","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Four Aspects of Autonomy: Task, Time, Technique, Team","visual_frame_description":"A conference-room table in a modern office, with four labeled trays — Task, Time, Technique, Team — set out like a buffet. Six short scenario cards describing real workdays slide in front of the player, each showing a person acting on one kind of freedom. The player must place each card on the tray it exercises.","player_mission":"Read each scenario and sort it into the aspect of autonomy it primarily exercises: task (what you do), technique (how you do it), time (when you do it), or team (with whom you do it). Correctly place all six cards.","key_concepts":["The discussion guide's eighth question names four aspects of autonomy: task, time, technique, and team.","Choosing what you work on is autonomy over task.","Choosing when you work is autonomy over time.","Choosing how you do the work is autonomy over technique.","Choosing who you work with is autonomy over team.","The guide asks which aspect of autonomy has been most important in the reader's own best work.","The guide asks how much autonomy the reader has at work right now and whether it is enough.","Question seven asks bosses to replace if-then rewards with a more autonomous environment and the occasional now-that reward.","The guide's sixth question asks how much of a reader's work is routine and how much is nonroutine.","Question nine asks whether FedEx Days, 20 percent time, and ROWE would work in the reader's organization."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This task tests the four-aspect model of autonomy from the discussion guide — task, time, technique, and team — by classifying everyday choices into the freedom each primarily exercises. The model reflects the book's claim, grounded in self-determination research, that humans have an innate need for self-direction.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"The guide's eighth question distinguishes four aspects of autonomy. Read each scenario and sort it into the aspect it primarily exercises: task, technique, or time.","column_a_label":"Autonomy over task — what you do","column_b_label":"Autonomy over technique — how you do it","column_c_label":"Autonomy over time — when you do it","rows":[{"label":"Dario, a customer-success manager, chooses the healthcare accounts he will lead this quarter instead of receiving an assigned roster","col":"a"},{"label":"Nina, a data analyst, rewrites the firm's required dashboard template into a cleaner structure she finds easier to maintain","col":"b"},{"label":"Marcus, an engineer, starts work at 7 a.m., takes a long midday break for his run, and logs off at 6 p.m., because his firm values only deadlines","col":"c"},{"label":"Elena volunteers to own the new client-onboarding brief instead of waiting for her supervisor to delegate it","col":"a"},{"label":"Hassan swaps the mandated morning stand-up for an asynchronous written update he produces whenever his focus is sharpest","col":"c"},{"label":"Priya picks a cello teacher whose method matches how she learns best, ignoring the conservatory's recommended syllabus","col":"b"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Choosing which accounts to lead is time autonomy, because it depends on scheduling your quarter.","Swapping the stand-up for an asynchronous update is task autonomy, because it changes what gets produced.","Rewriting the dashboard template is time autonomy, because the rewrite takes extra time to complete."],"explanation":"The guide distinguishes four autonomous freedoms — what you do (task), when you do it (time), how you do it (technique), and with whom you do it (team). Rows one and four change the work itself, so they exercise task autonomy. Rows two and six alter the method of the work, so they exercise technique autonomy. Rows three and five alter the schedule of the work, so they exercise time autonomy. The correct answer for the lead row is 'a' because choosing which accounts to lead is a choice about what work gets done — the defining feature of task autonomy."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Piloting a FedEx Day Without Strangling It","visual_frame_description":"A Columbus insurance office on a Thursday: four teams huddle around whiteboards, one group building a working shelf-appraisal tool with real momentum. In a corner office, a manager taps a mandatory check-in timer on his phone while a ranked demo board with a perk-prize line hangs over the room.","player_mission":"Identify which detail is strangling the FedEx Day pilot, then choose how the CEO should run the initiative so it honors the autonomy it is meant to grant. Win the battle by keeping the burst of autonomy unscripted.","key_concepts":["A FedEx Day is a one-day burst of autonomy in which employees tackle any problem they want and show results by the end of twenty-four hours.","The guide asks whether initiatives like FedEx Days, 20 percent time, and ROWE would work in the reader's organization.","The guide also asks for ideas that would bring out more Type I behavior in the workplace.","20 percent time lets employees spend one day in five working on any project they choose.","A results-only work environment removes schedules; employees just have to get their work done.","Type I behavior is powered by the innate need to direct our own lives, to learn and create new things, and to do better.","The guide asks how well a job meets the need for baseline rewards — salary, benefits, and a few perks.","The guide asks readers to recall the seven deadly flaws of carrots and sticks in action.","The guide's introduction argues that ideas come to life when talked over in person — that the world changes conversation by conversation."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests whether a manager understands what makes an autonomy initiative a Motivation 3.0 practice: FedEx Days work by letting employees choose the problem and own the process, and control mechanisms stapled on top of the day defeat its purpose. The distinction between engagement and compliance runs through the book's treatment of autonomy as an innate psychological need.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the discussion guide's question of whether initiatives like FedEx Days, 20 percent time, and ROWE would work in an organization — specifically, whether the manager understands that a FedEx Day is a burst of autonomy and will strangle it with contingencies.","stage_1_observation":"At a Columbus insurance company, CEO Marta Villanueva launches her first FedEx Day after reading the discussion guide: four teams of twenty get a Thursday to tackle any problem they want, with demos due Friday afternoon. By lunch, three teams are building a shelf-appraisal tool with real promise; one manager, however, schedules mandatory check-ins every two hours, orders his team to work on his pet project, and announces the Friday demos will be ranked with the bottom team losing a monthly perk.","stage_1_question":"During the pilot, which detail most undermines the FedEx Day's motivational logic?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The manager's behavior: the two-hour check-ins, the assigned pet project, and the threatened loss of a perk convert the day of autonomous problem-choosing into precisely the controlled, contingent environment the initiative is meant to escape. A FedEx Day is a burst of autonomy — employees tackle any problem they want and show results within twenty-four hours — and that one team is not running the day at all.","stage_2_intervention":"Marta must decide how to proceed: (A) cancel the program because one day of self-chosen projects wastes productive time; (B) formalize it — require measurable output, tie the day to a bonus, and pre-approve every project; (C) keep the day unscripted, protect the teams from managerial oversight, let the Friday demos stand as the only yardstick, and ask whether ROWE or 20 percent time deserves a pilot next. Which step is most consistent with the guide's autonomy question?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option C. The guide treats FedEx Days, 20 percent time, and ROWE as vehicles for autonomy — over task, time, technique, and team — so protecting self-direction and letting each team own its problem, pace, and method is the point. Option A overreacts to a single piloted day, and option B wraps a freedom-granting initiative in the contingencies it exists to remove."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Type I or Type X: Reading the People Around You","visual_frame_description":"A living-room scene from a book club evening: three profile cards lie on the coffee table, each showing a person from a reader's life — a drummer practicing after dinner, a colleague watching the sales board, a volunteer at a food bank. The player must judge each behavior's motivational fuel.","player_mission":"Read each behavior and sort it as Type I behavior, Type X behavior, or a neutral everyday preference outside the book's two types. Complete the matrix to finish the chapter's self-audit.","key_concepts":["Type I behavior is built around intrinsic rather than extrinsic motivators.","Type I behavior is powered by our innate need to direct our own lives, to learn and create new things, and to do better by ourselves and our world.","Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires than intrinsic ones.","Type X behavior concerns itself less with the inherent satisfaction of an activity and more with the external rewards to which that activity leads.","The guide asks readers to consider whether they are more Type I or Type X.","The guide asks readers to classify three people in their lives and to explain the reasoning behind the classification.","Question eighteen asks whether a parent's home environment promotes more Type I or Type X behavior in children.","Question seventeen asks whether education is too Type X and how to reconcile intrinsic motivation with accountability.","The guide's flow question asks where and when the reader last experienced flow and how to tweak a role to bring on more optimal experiences.","Hobbies pursued for their own sake are everyday expressions of intrinsic motivation."],"difficulty_level":"Advanced","theory_summary":"This task tests the Type I/Type X distinction — intrinsic versus extrinsic motivational orientation — by classifying real-life behaviors into the two camps the discussion guide asks readers to examine in themselves and others. The distinction derives from self-determination theory research on the benefits of an autonomy orientation over a control orientation, as synthesized in the book.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"Read each behavior and sort it into Type I behavior, Type X behavior, or a neutral preference that the book's two-type distinction does not classify.","column_a_label":"Type I — built around intrinsic motivators","column_b_label":"Type X — fueled more by extrinsic desires and external rewards","column_c_label":"Neutral — an everyday preference with no clear motivational orientation","rows":[{"label":"Kofi practices drums nightly for the pure satisfaction of getting better, not for any trophy","col":"a"},{"label":"Leah chose her job chiefly because of the bonus structure and the promotion ladder","col":"b"},{"label":"Taking a lunch break because you enjoy the sandwich you packed","col":"c"},{"label":"Simone volunteers at the food bank on Saturdays because she loves the work itself","col":"a"},{"label":"Aidan checks his sales rankings hourly because the public board gratifies him","col":"b"},{"label":"Choosing the window seat because you like the view","col":"c"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Volunteering at a food bank is Type X because volunteers receive no payment, which proves the activity runs on reputation instead.","Practicing drums nightly for satisfaction is Type X because drumming builds a skill that could someday earn money.","A job chosen for its bonus structure is Type I because receiving a bonus is a form of recognition and recognition is intrinsic."],"explanation":"Type I behavior is powered by intrinsic motivators — the inherent satisfaction of the activity itself, the pull to learn and create — which is what Kofi's drumming and Simone's volunteering show. Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires and oriented to the external rewards an activity leads to, which is what Leah's job choice and Aidan's ranking-checking show. The neutral column holds ordinary preferences — a packed sandwich, a window seat — that involve no intrinsic-versus-extrinsic tension and that the guide's two-type frame does not classify. The correct answer for the lead row is 'a' because Kofi's motivation is the inherent satisfaction of getting better, the signature of Type I behavior."}]},{"id":"ch22","title":"FIND OUT MORE—ABOUT YOURSELF AND THIS TOPIC","pages":"","assets":[{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Rolling Out the Type I/Type X Assessment","visual_frame_description":"An HR office at a 400-person retail analytics firm: a large screen shows a completion dashboard ranking departments by assessment click-through, with a catered-lunch prize banner on top. Head of people Ade Adeyemi watches employees clicking through the free Type I/Type X assessment in under thirty seconds while a few whisper about surveillance.","player_mission":"Diagnose why the mandatory, ranked rollout of a personal self-assessment hollowed it out, then choose the rollout that matches the chapter's invitation to find out more about yourself. Win the battle by respecting the assessment's voluntary, self-directed purpose.","key_concepts":["The final chapter asks readers a direct question: are you Type I or Type X?","The chapter directs readers to a free online assessment at www.danpink.com/drive.html.","The assessment is framed as a tool for self-discovery about one's own motivation.","Drive Times is a free quarterly e-mail newsletter on the science and practice of human motivation.","Type I behavior is built around intrinsic rather than extrinsic motivators.","Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires than intrinsic ones.","A mandatory, ranked rollout converts a self-directed resource into a contingent, externally monitored task.","The chapter's heading frames its content as being about yourself and this topic — personal reflection plus subject learning."],"difficulty_level":"Beginner","theory_summary":"This battle tests whether a leader can create conditions for genuine self-discovery — the chapter's invitation to find out more about yourself and this topic — instead of extrinsic compliance. The assessment is a free, optional resource in the text, so coercive rollouts contradict its motivational spirit.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the final chapter's resources: a free online assessment for determining whether a reader is Type I or Type X, and the invitation to find out more about yourself and this topic — which requires voluntary, self-directed engagement rather than compliance-driven completion.","stage_1_observation":"Ade Adeyemi, head of people at a 400-person retail analytics firm, runs a Motivation Month: every employee must complete the free Type I/Type X assessment before Friday, and the HR dashboard ranks each department by completion percentage, with the winning department earning a catered lunch. By Thursday, completion sits at 96 percent, but a teammate tells Ade that people are clicking answers in thirty seconds to get it over with, and a few are muttering about surveillance.","stage_1_question":"The assessment is being completed, but the exercise is hollow. In this chapter's terms, why?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The mandatory, ranked, prize-linked rollout turned a free, self-paced assessment designed for personal insight into another contingent, externally monitored task — completion became an extrinsic chore, and the public ranking made it a competition for a prize rather than an occasion for reflection. The chapter frames the assessment as a resource for finding out more about yourself, which requires voluntary engagement.","stage_2_intervention":"Ade wants a do-over. Which plan best matches the chapter's framing? (A) Keep the completion quota but change the prize to a half-day off; (B) drop the quota and the ranking, host the assessment as a voluntary lunch-and-learn where people score themselves privately and share only if they choose, and link the free newsletter for anyone who wants to keep learning; (C) reprimand the employees who dismissed the assessment as surveillance and require written reflection essays from everyone else. Which option do you choose?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option B. The chapter positions the assessment as a free resource the reader chooses to use, so a voluntary, private, self-directed format preserves its intrinsic purpose. Option A keeps an external contingency and just swaps the prize, and option C introduces punitive control that directly contradicts the chapter's invitation to find out more about yourself — the very opposite of what the resource is for."},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"The Type I/Type X Self-Check","visual_frame_description":"A laptop screen fills the frame: the free online self-assessment from the book's final chapter, with a progress slider and a let's-find-out banner. Eight everyday behavior cards appear beside it, and the player must judge each one as Type I, Type X, or a compensation-baseline concern before the assessment reveals its verdict.","player_mission":"Read each behavior card and sort it into the category the glossary definitions support: Type I, Type X, or a baseline-compensation matter. Complete all six sorts to mirror what the free assessment invites readers to discover about themselves.","key_concepts":["The final chapter asks readers to determine whether they are more Type I or Type X via a free online assessment.","Type I behavior is powered by our innate need to direct our own lives, to learn and create new things, and to do better by ourselves and our world.","Type X behavior concerns itself less with the inherent satisfaction of an activity and more with the external rewards to which that activity leads.","Both Type I's and Type X's care about money.","An inadequate or inequitable baseline blocks motivation of any sort.","Activities pursued for their inherent satisfaction signal a Type I orientation.","Activities pursued mainly for external reward signal a Type X orientation.","Curiosity about one's own motivation is itself a self-directed, intrinsic impulse.","Drive Times, the book's newsletter, keeps readers current on the science and practice of motivation."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This task tests the Type I/Type X distinction that the chapter's assessment invites readers to apply to themselves, using the glossary definitions of intrinsic and extrinsic orientation. The classification rests on self-determination theory's contrast between autonomy orientation and control orientation as synthesized across the book.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"question":"The final chapter points you to a free online assessment that asks: are you Type I or Type X? Read each item and sort it into the category the glossary definitions support.","column_a_label":"Type I — built around intrinsic motivators","column_b_label":"Type X — oriented to external rewards","column_c_label":"Baseline concern — about the compensation floor, not orientation","rows":[{"label":"You keep improving your woodworking at night, purely for the pull of the craft itself","col":"a"},{"label":"You chose your side job because it pays the best, even though you dread the work","col":"b"},{"label":"You care deeply that your salary is adequate and equitable compared with peers","col":"c"},{"label":"You taught yourself video editing last winter simply because you wanted to create something","col":"a"},{"label":"You check your follower count hourly because the external recognition feels good","col":"b"},{"label":"You take the free assessment because you are curious what it will reveal about your own motivation","col":"a"}],"correct_answer":"a","distractors":["Teaching yourself video editing is Type X because the product of editing is something other people consume.","Caring about adequate pay makes you Type X, since genuinely Type I people never think about money.","Curiosity about your own motivation is Type X because focusing on yourself is self-interested."],"explanation":"Type I behavior is built around intrinsic motivators — the inherent satisfaction of the activity and the pull to learn, create, and improve — which is what the woodworking, video editing, and assessment-taking rows show. Type X behavior is fueled more by extrinsic desires and oriented to the external rewards the activity leads to, which is what the side-job and follower-count rows show. Caring that salary is adequate and equitable is a baseline concern rather than an orientation: the glossary says Type I's and Type X's both care about money, and an inadequate or inequitable baseline makes motivation of any sort extremely difficult. The correct answer for the lead row is 'a' because the craft itself pulls you — the defining feature of Type I behavior."},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Intrinsic Motivation | Self-Determination Theory","topic":"Drive Times: Making Motivation Learning Voluntary","visual_frame_description":"A Portland nonprofit's staff office: a shared tracker on the wall titled Newsletter Takeaways, filled with tautological bullet points, while two program officers wordlessly forward the quarterly e-mail newsletter Drive Times to an archive folder. Executive director Lena Cho looks on, puzzled that required learning produced resentment.","player_mission":"Diagnose why the mandatory newsletter requirement killed curiosity, then choose the redesign that treats ongoing motivation learning as the voluntary, self-directed pursuit the chapter describes. Win the battle to bring Drive Times back to life.","key_concepts":["Drive Times is a free quarterly e-mail newsletter on the science and practice of human motivation.","The final chapter invites readers to find out more about themselves and the topic.","Continuous learning about motivation works best when it is voluntary and self-directed.","Requiring a subscription and mandated takeaways turns a learning resource into an extrinsic obligation.","Contingent requirements attached to learning tend to crowd out intrinsic curiosity.","The book offers a free online Type I/Type X assessment for personal use.","The book's overall stance is that there is a gap between what science knows and what organizations do.","Voluntary, social formats — like a coffee chat where people share what they found interesting — preserve autonomy.","External contingencies can convert an invitation to learn into a compliance artifact and feed resentment."],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate","theory_summary":"This battle tests the chapter's invitation to keep learning about motivation science through free, self-directed resources — the assessment and Drive Times — and the damage done when a curiosity-offering is re-cast as a mandated, tracked obligation. The mechanism is the book's recurring claim that contingent controls can suppress the intrinsic interest they pretend to support.","credibility":{"level":"Medium","consensus":"Emerging","basis":"Popular science synthesis (Deci & Ryan self-determination theory; motivation research)."},"boss_battle_explanation":"The battle tests the final chapter's ongoing-learning resources — the free quarterly newsletter Drive Times on the science and practice of human motivation — and whether a leader treats continuing motivation education as an intrinsic pursuit or re-frames it as a compliance program.","stage_1_observation":"At a Portland nonprofit, executive director Lena Cho wants her staff to stay current on motivation science, so she requires every program officer to subscribe to the quarterly newsletter Drive Times and to paste three takeaways into a shared tracker each quarter. The tracker fills with tautological bullet points, and two officers quietly admit they forward the newsletter unread — the ritual is feeding resentment, not curiosity.","stage_1_question":"Why is the subscription requirement producing the opposite of continuous learning?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Requiring the subscription and the mandated takeaways reconfigured an invitation to learn — the chapter's promise of regular, free updates on the science and practice of human motivation — into an extrinsic obligation with a compliance artifact, the tracker. Contingent requirements attached to learning tend to crowd out the intrinsic curiosity the newsletter is meant to serve, so the ritual produced compliance and resentment.","stage_2_intervention":"Lena's options: (A) keep the tracker but grade the takeaways; (B) unsubscribe everyone and buy each officer a conference pass with a mandatory session schedule; (C) make learning voluntary and social — put one copy of the newsletter in the break room, let anyone who finds something interesting raise it in the standing Friday coffee chat, post the free assessment link for personal use, and let word of mouth carry it. Which option fits the chapter's spirit best?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Option C. The chapter frames these resources as free, optional, self-directed aids — find out more about yourself and this topic — so voluntary, social formats preserve autonomy and intrinsic interest. Options A and B retain contingent requirements, a graded tracker and a mandated conference schedule, that reproduce the compliance dynamic the chapter's framing is built to avoid."}]}]};
